Various techniques have been developed to address this issue, including vision-based localization systems \cite{naser2019infrastructure, yedidia2019using, lindell2019wave, ye2024plug, li2023nlost, velten2012recovering}, LiDAR \cite{ayyalasomayajula2020deep, malik2024transient}, and RF-based positioning \cite{yue2022cornerradar, gunia2023analysis, li2020wiborder, zetik2015looking}. Among these approaches, vision-based localization systems have attracted significant attention due to their high-resolution positioning capabilities. However, these methods are sensitive to lighting conditions and pose privacy concerns due to camera use.

Alternatively, radio frequency (RF) signals have shown promise for indoor localization by analyzing the differences between transmitted and received reflections. Existing RF-based technologies, such as Wi-Fi \cite{gunia2023analysis, adib2013see, yao2024wiprofile}, Bluetooth Low Energy (BLE) \cite{yu2021novel}, and RFID \cite{bouet2008rfid}, have been employed for this purpose. Compared to vision-based systems, RF methods are often criticized for limited range and accuracy. Recent research has focused on leveraging mmWave signals to improve localization accuracy, benefiting from their high frequency and wide bandwidth. However, most sensing methods, such as cameras or radar, rely heavily on a direct line of sight (LoS) to the target. This reliance limits their effectiveness in complex environments where objects may be occluded by walls, furniture, or other barriers.

Non-Line-of-Sight (NLoS) sensing approaches can be broadly categorized into passive and active techniques. Passive methods \cite{xu2023leveraging, woodford2022mosaic, wei2021nonline} attempt to identify suitable reflection surfaces to detect targets in NLoS areas. These methods often require additional hardware, such as LiDAR, and depend on reflective surface properties. Active methods deploy reflectors, such as metal reflectors or smart reflecting surfaces, to direct signals toward the target area. These reflectors can be placed on the target side or along the reflection path.

For example, SuperSight \cite{bae2024supersight} employs a triangular tag array with three retro-reflective tags on the target side for NLoS sensing, while PinIt \cite{wang2013dude} localizes NLoS objects using numerous reference tags. BiScatter \cite{okubo2024integrated} integrates two-way backscatter networks with radar sensing. However, these methods require the target to carry retro-reflective tags, which is impractical for sudden human appearances.

Other solutions place reflectors in the middle of the path. Metasurfaces \cite{chen2016review} are often used, with each supercell designed for a specific reflection angle. MetaSight \cite{woodford2023metasight} employs multiple metasurfaces to modulate mmWave NLoS signals, but this approach is complex and adds latency. Moreover, metasurfaces passively reflect waves, making it challenging to establish communication between the radar and the IRS using existing sensing signals. This reduces the flexibility and intelligence of metasurface-based NLoS sensing.

Motivated by these challenges, we present \sn, a novel scheme for precise, non-penetrable NLoS localization using only a single mmWave radar and an intelligent reflecting surface (IRS). By leveraging multipath reflections of mmWave radar signals, \sn achieves robust localization. We propose an innovative IRS hardware design based on Van Atta Array (VAA) theory, incorporating four transmission line combinations for a reconfigurable IRS.

Compared to metasurface-based IRS solutions \cite{kim2024nr}, VAAs offer a more stable reflection path with lower cost. While most VAA-based IRS designs only support retro-reflection, VAAs can also enable signal redirection by adjusting transmission line lengths \cite{ang2018passive}.

To facilitate radar-IRS communication, we introduce a novel amplitude modulation-based antenna encoding technique combined with an envelope detector, building communication atop the existing FMCW signal. The envelope detector differentiates antennas based on their amplitude signatures, enabling dynamic reflection angle adjustments.

Additionally, we design a time slot scheduling scheme that optimizes reflection angles and dynamically allocates sensing time, focusing on areas of interest. This significantly reduces localization latency and improves the signal-to-noise ratio (SNR).

Our key contributions are:
\begin{itemize}
    \item We propose \sn, a novel mmWave system for precise, device-free indoor localization. \sn achieves centimeter-level accuracy with a single mmWave radar and a VAA-based IRS, supporting robot-human interaction without target-side devices.
    \item We introduce an antenna encoding method enabling radar-IRS communication on top of existing FMCW signals, dynamically adjusting transmission angles and power without prior environmental knowledge.
    \item We develop a time slot allocation scheme that improves SNR and reduces scanning time by eliminating angles without targets and reallocating sensing resources to weaker reflections. This reduces scanning latency by 42\% for multiple targets and 75\% for a single target.
    \item Extensive experiments in an L-shaped corridor demonstrate \sn’s median localization error of 8.93\,cm at 3\,m, showing significant potential for real-world robot-human interaction applications.
\end{itemize}


