\subsection{\sn Overview}

\sn aims to create a precise and practical mmWave-based localization system for non-penetrable NLoS scenarios. To achieve this, \sn performs localization through the following procedure, as illustrated in Figure \ref{fig:nlos_working_overview}.

Initially, the IRS broadcasts its presence to inform the radar of its location. Once the radar detects the IRS, it uses antenna coding to transmit FMCW chirp signals toward the IRS, defining the areas of interest (AOI) for localization. The IRS receives and decodes these signals, configuring its reflection angle based on the transmitted information. Subsequently, the radar captures the modulated on-off keying (OOK) signals from the IRS to determine the current reflection angle.

The radar then detects potential targets and determines the scanning areas for the next round. To enhance signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) levels for different targets, \sn dynamically adjusts the reflection time for each angle. Additionally, a scheduling method is employed to allow the radar to select the optimal reflecting angle. This approach ensures the reflected radar signals continuously track moving targets while discarding angles without targets, significantly reducing localization latency.

Finally, \sn achieves precise localization by leveraging the position and angle information provided by the IRS. The system then updates the AOI for the next scanning round, enabling efficient and dynamic target tracking.


