As discussed in Section 3.1, achieving non-retro-reflective functionality in a Van Atta Array (VAA) can be accomplished by adjusting the lengths of the transmission lines, thereby introducing additional phase delays. By combining multiple sets of transmission lines, this modified VAA array can support reflections at different angles.

\sn is based on a linear Van Atta structure with eight sets of transmission lines connected to six patch antenna elements (see Figure \ref{fig:antenna}). This design allows the IRS to switch between four distinct reflection angles (30°, 45°, 60°, and 75°), thereby maximizing the scanning area in NLoS scenarios. Additionally, the series-fed patch array elements, which provide high gain, maximize the energy of radar signals reflected into the NLoS area.

To enable two-way reflection, two sets of transmission lines are selected for each specific reflection angle. The patch antennas in each Van Atta element utilize an insert-fed technique, which offers high S11 performance while reducing the overall size of the IRS.

