\subsection{IRS-Radar Communication}

To obtain and dynamically configure the value of $\alpha_i$, as described in Section 3.2, it is necessary to establish a communication channel that supports both uplink and downlink communication between the radar and the IRS. In this section, we discuss methods to set up these communication channels while minimizing additional hardware requirements.
\newline

\noindent\textbf{IRS to Radar communication} \newline
For communication from the IRS to the radar, \sn employs a simple On-Off Keying (OOK) modulation scheme, similar to the approach used in Milimetro \cite{soltanaghaei2021millimetro}. The radar can demodulate the signal by detecting variations in the reflection strength, while simultaneously localizing the position of the IRS. \newline
\newline
\noindent\textbf{Radar to IRS communication} 

\begin{figure*}[!htp]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=1\textwidth]{images/encoding_overview.png}
    \caption{\sn encoding overview}
    \label{fig:encoding_overview}
\end{figure*}

Communication from the radar to the IRS presents greater challenges, as the radar transmits FMCW chirp signals primarily designed for sensing purposes. To address this, we propose a novel antenna encoding technique, combined with an envelope detector, to sample and decode the amplitude of the incoming FMCW signal at a lower frequency, as illustrated in Figure \ref{fig:encoding_overview}.


The basic envelope detector circuit consists of a diode, capacitor, and resistor. When an amplitude-modulated signal passes through the diode, the diode rectifies the signal, allowing only the positive half-cycles to pass through. The capacitor then charges to the peak amplitude of the signal, creating a smoothed waveform that represents the envelope of the input. The resistor discharges the capacitor during the low-amplitude portions of the signal, which allows the circuit to follow the variations in the amplitude over time, accurately producing the envelope.

The output voltage \( V_{\text{out}} \) of an envelope detector depends on the peak amplitude \( A_{\text{in}} \) of the input signal. Assuming a sinusoidal input signal, the relationship between the input amplitude and the output voltage is approximately given by:
\[
V_{\text{out}} \approx A_{\text{in}} - V_{\text{diode}}.
\]

What's more, the amplitude of a received signal decreases as the distance between the transmitter and receiver increases. This can be described by the \textit{path loss model}, which provides a mathematical relationship between the received signal amplitude and the distance. 

The basic path loss model can be expressed as:
\[
P_r(d) = P_t \cdot \left( \frac{\lambda}{4 \pi d} \right)^2,
\]
where  \( P_r(d) \) is the received power at distance \( d \), \( P_t \) is the transmitted power, \( \lambda \) is the wavelength of the transmitted signal, and \( d \) is the distance between the transmitter and receiver.

Specifically, the envelope detector can detect the amplitude level of the incoming signal. Due to the spatial separation of the transmission antennas, each antenna generates a unique amplitude value, which can be detected by the envelope detector. And this feature is used to encode information by selecting which antenna transmits FMCW chirps. The encoded raw signal is illustrated in Figure \ref{fig:data_example}. We adopt a commonly used header-preamble-data packet structure. The header contains 2 unique bits for packet identification, while the preamble includes 3 bits for synchronization. Additionally, we allocate a 6-bit data field for communication. \newline

% \noindent\textbf{Radar to IRS communication Packet Structure:}

\begin{figure}[!htp]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.48\textwidth]{images/packet.png}
    \caption{\sn antenna encoding raw signal example}
    \label{fig:data_example}
    % \vspace{-3mm}
\end{figure}



\noindent\textbf{Radar to IRS communication Data Rate:} \newline
The radar symbol is defined by the number of TX antenna repetitions, \( N_r \), and the antenna switching frequency, \( F \). Consequently, the achievable data rate can be calculated using the following equation:

\[
\text{data\_rate} = \frac{F}{2N_r}
\]

To increase the data rate, we can either reduce the number of TX antenna repetitions or increase the antenna switching frequency. Theoretically, with only one TX antenna repetition and an antenna switching frequency of 3906.25 Hz (corresponding to a chirp period of \( 256 \, \mu\text{s} \)), a data rate of 1953.125 bps can be achieved. However, due to hardware limitations, the maximum configurable antenna switching frequency is restricted to 20 Hz. Despite this limitation, the achievable data rate is sufficient for the radar to communicate the desired reflection angles and allocate sensing slots dynamically for the IRS.\newline

\noindent\textbf{Multiple radar support:} \newline
When the environment contains multiple radars, the path loss model indicates that the modulated signal from a radar at a shorter distance will overshadow the signal from a radar at a greater distance. Moreover, the received information can become corrupted because the IRS cannot discern the number of senders.

To address this issue, we developed an antenna-based amplitude modulation technique utilizing two FMCW radar antennas, building upon the previous antenna encoding method. The carrier signal \( s(t) \) is defined as:

\[
s(t) = b(t) \cdot A \cos(2 \pi F t)
\]

where:
\begin{itemize}
    \item When \( b(t) = 0 \), \( s(t) = A_0 \cos(2 \pi F t) \), representing a carrier signal sent from antenna 0 with amplitude \( A_0 \).
    \item When \( b(t) = 1 \), \( s(t) = A_1 \cos(2 \pi F t) \), representing a carrier signal sent from antenna 1 with amplitude \( A_1 \).
    \item \( F \) is the antenna switching frequency, which is unique for each radar.
\end{itemize}

In addition to the carrier signal, information is encoded by adjusting the transmission power \( T_{\text{power}} \). The modulated signal \( m(t) \) is then defined as:

\[
m(t) = T_{\text{power}} \cdot s(t)
\]

Since each radar has a unique antenna switching frequency \( F \), as shown in Figure \ref{fig:multiple_radar_example}, the IRS can detect the number of radars using Fast Fourier Transform (FFT) and subsequently separate the signals using a bandpass filter.


