\begin{algorithm}
\caption{\sn IRS Working Procedure}
\label{alg:irs_working}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\STATE \textbf{Start:} IRS broadcasts ID.
\WHILE{TRUE}
    \STATE IRS takes ADC samples.
    \IF {Radar signal exists}
        \STATE IRS decodes the signal.
        \IF {Signal contains AOI (Angle of Interest)}
            \STATE Use AOI set from radar.
        \ELSE
            \STATE Use all reflecting angles as AOI.
        \ENDIF
        \FOR {Each angle $\alpha$ in AOI}
            \STATE Take angle $\alpha$ and broadcast in retro-reflection mode.
            \STATE Switch to transmission line with reflection angle $\alpha$ in reflection mode.
        \ENDFOR
    \ELSE
        \STATE Repeat ADC sampling.
    \ENDIF
\ENDWHILE
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\begin{figure}[!htp]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.45\textwidth]{images/radar_work_flow.png}
    \caption{\sn radar working procedure}
    \label{fig:overview_radar}
    % \vspace{-3mm}
\end{figure}

Since the radar initially lacks knowledge of the environment, the retro-reflection mode enables the radar to localize the position of the IRS and distinguish it from static reflectors, as shown in Figure \ref{fig:vaa_mode}. During this period, the IRS uses OOK modulation to broadcast its unique ID, which provides potential support for scenarios with multiple IRS devices. Algorithm~\ref{alg:irs_working} illustrates the detailed IRS working procedure as a master device, while Figure \ref{fig:overview_radar} describes the radar operating as a slave device.

After the radar determines the position and ID of the IRS, the IRS receives area of interest (AOI) sets from the radar and alternates between retro-reflection mode and normal reflection mode. Figure \ref{fig:naive_allocation} provides a detailed view of naive mode allocation. We define a complete scan across all possible angles as a super-frame, with each angle corresponding to a sub-frame. Each sub-frame consists of time slots allocated for both communication and sensing. The yellow portion represents communication time, during which the IRS operates in retro-reflection mode and broadcasts the reflecting angle of the sub-frame. 

Once the radar identifies the position of the IRS and receives the reflecting angle, it can localize targets in the NLoS area using the methods discussed in Section 3.2.


\begin{figure}[!htp]
    % \vspace{-5mm}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.3\textwidth]{images/naive_approach.png}
    \caption{Naive time slots allocation}
    \label{fig:naive_allocation}
    % \vspace{-3mm}
\end{figure}


\begin{figure}[!htp]
    % \vspace{-5mm}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.45\textwidth]{images/optimized_sample.png}
    \caption{Time series slot allocation example}
    \label{fig:optimized_allocation}
    % \vspace{-3mm}
\end{figure}

By default, the IRS needs to fully scan all angles to locate targets. For a high-speed target, it might go undetected and move out of the detection area, potentially causing collisions if the scanning latency for each angle is too long. Moreover, during a full scan, a significant portion of the scanning time is wasted, as there may be no targets in some reflection angles. To address this issue, we introduced an extra time slot for radar-to-IRS communication (colored in red) between each super-frame, and the total number of time slots between two radar-to-IRS communication slots is limited based on the target's speed. This allows the radar to dynamically adjust the area of interest (AOI) for sensing. After the communication phase, the IRS will only select reflection angles based on the AOI set, resulting in a reduced super-frame size and improved accuracy. This adaptive approach reduces localization time by focusing the scan on relevant areas. Additionally, according to the SNR theory given below, for a given mmWave radar sensing environment with targets at different positions, the amount of allocated sensing chirps $N$ would have a positive effect on SNR and then improve accuracy.

\[
    \text{SNR} = \frac{P_t \cdot G_{\text{RX}} \cdot G_{\text{TX}} \cdot c^2 \cdot \sigma \cdot N \cdot T_r}{f_c^2 \cdot (4\pi)^3 \cdot kT \cdot \text{NF} \cdot R^4}
\]

where:

\begin{itemize}
    \item $P_t$: TX output power
    \item $R$: Range of the object
    \item $G_{\text{RX}}, G_{\text{TX}}$: RX and TX antenna gain
    \item $\sigma$: RCS (Radar Cross Section) of the object
    \item $f_c$: Center frequency of the chirp ramp
    \item $N$: Number of chirps
    \item $T_r$: Chirp ramp time in seconds
    \item $\text{NF}$: Noise figure of the receiver
    \item $\text{SNR}$: Signal-to-noise ratio
    \item $k$: Boltzmann constant
    \item $T$: Effective noise temperature
\end{itemize}

To determine the AOI sets and their corresponding allocated sensing slots, we begin with the default all-angle scanning that outputs the target sets. As illustrated in Algorithm~\ref{alg:aoi_construct}, our approach essentially operates in a greedy manner: it first adds as many scanning angles as possible to form the initial AOI set \( U \), and then constructs the AOI set for the next scanning round.

The algorithm starts by gathering all target positions in \( \mathcal{T} \) (lines 6-8). It then identifies the target with the maximum energy, \( T_{\text{max\_energy}} \) (lines 13-15), as this target will determine the minimum number of time slots allocated to a target. The next step is to find the target with the highest speed (line 16), which will constrain the maximum number of time slots allocated for the entire super-frame. After determining these constraints, the algorithm allocates time slots \( D_i \) in a greedy manner (lines 17-24). Once all targets are added to the AOI set (lines 25-27), the algorithm waits for new data from the radar to construct new AOI sets that cover the updated target positions.


% Furthermore, Figure  illustrates the detailed optimization approach for NLoS sensing. Between super-frames, Radar-IRS communication is employed to dynamically adjust the scanning area and the size of each sub-frame based on previous sensing results. 


\begin{algorithm}
    \caption{AOI-Sets construction}
    \label{alg:aoi_construct}
    \begin{algorithmic}[1]
        \STATE \textbf{Input:} $\mathcal{R}$: Raw Radar Data Matrix
        \STATE \textbf{\hspace{26pt}} $\mathcal{A}$: Previous scanning angle-duration set
        \STATE \textbf{\hspace{26pt}} $\alpha$: Scanning resolution
        \STATE \textbf{Output:} $\mathcal{T}$: Target Set
        \STATE \textbf{\hspace{33pt}} $\mathcal{P}$: Next scanning angle-duration set
        \FOR{each $A_i \in \mathcal{A}$}
        \STATE $\mathcal{T}\ \cup = $ MUSIC\_search($A_i$, $R$)
        \ENDFOR
        \IF{$\mathcal{T} = \phi$}
        \STATE $\mathcal{A'} \gets \mathcal{A}$
        \STATE \textbf{Break}
        \ENDIF
        \STATE $T_{max\_energy} \gets $ max(Reflection\_energy\_search$(\mathcal{T}, R)$)
        \STATE $r_{max\_energy} \gets $ Range\_FFT $(T_{max\_energy}, R)$
        \STATE $D_{max\_energy} \gets $ Reflection\_duration\_search$(T_{max\_energy}, R)$
        \STATE  $r_{max\_speed}, v_{max} \gets$ max(Range\_Doppler$(\mathcal{T}, R)$)
        \FOR{$T_i \in \mathcal{T}$}
        \STATE $a_i \gets $ Reflection\_angle\_search($T_i$, $R$)
        \STATE $r_{i} \gets $ Range\_FFT $(T_{i}, R)$
        \ENDFOR
        \STATE $\underset{D_i}{\textnormal{minimise}}\quad D_{scan} = \sum_{i=1}^{len(\mathcal{T})}D_i$
        \STATE  $\textnormal{subject to} \quad$
        \STATE \textbf{\hspace{40pt}} $D_i \geq \frac{r_{max\_energy}^4}{r_i^4} D_{max\_energy}$
        \STATE \textbf{\hspace{40pt}} $v_{max} \times D_{scan} < \frac{\alpha}{180}\pi r_{max\_speed}$
        \FOR{each $a_i$}
        \STATE $\mathcal{P}\ \cup = \{ (a_i,D_i) \}$
        \ENDFOR


    \end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\end{comment}

\section{Performance Evaluation}

\subsection{\sn Prototyping}
Figure~\ref{fig:pcb} illustrates the \sn IRS prototype, with dimensions of \( 58 \times 58 \, \mathrm{mm}^2 \), fabricated using Rogers RO4003C radio frequency PCB. The IRS is controlled by an ATmega328P MCU~\cite{mcu}, and HMC1084LC4TR RF switches~\cite{switch} are employed for OOK modulation and transmission line selection to achieve retro-reflection or the desired reflection angle.
As shown in Figure~\ref{fig:antenna_design}, we designed insert-fed patch antennas with a peak gain of approximately 11\,\(\mathrm{dB}\) and an S11 below \(-10 \, \mathrm{dB}\) over the frequency range of 24–24.25\,\(\mathrm{GHz}\). These antennas connect to RFC ports, with four transmission lines featuring varying delays, enabling four distinct reflection angles and forming a Non-Retro-Reflection VAA.

An envelope detector (Analog Devices ADL6010)~\cite{envelope} is utilized to demodulate radar-to-IRS communication signals. The \sn system was implemented and evaluated using the EV-TINYRAD24G (Analog Devices)~\cite{radar}, a COTS 24\,\(\mathrm{GHz}\) radar with a bandwidth of 250\,\(\mathrm{MHz}\). This radar is equipped with two transmit and four receive antennas.

