\subsection{Micro Benchmark}

The main components required at the tag include RF switches, an envelope detector, and an MCU. We categorize the tag’s power consumption into three operating modes:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Sleeping mode}: In this mode, no robot is approaching the tag, and the IRS continuously monitors for any incoming FMCW signals. To minimize power consumption, the MCU is placed in a low-power sleep state with all other components turned off. Under this configuration, the typical MCU power consumption is approximately 46.5\,\(\mu\)W.

    \item \textbf{Communicating mode}: In this mode, both the envelope detector and the MCU are active, consuming about 8\,mW and 641.058\,\(\mu\)W, respectively. By disabling the CPU and using a hardware timer running at 64\,Hz, we periodically wake the CPU to power cycle the envelope detector and perform ADC sampling and decoding. As a result, the total average power consumption in this mode can be reduced to approximately 2.763\,mW.

    \item \textbf{Reflection mode}: In this mode, both the RF switch and the MCU are active. Each RF switch consumes 2.86\,\(\mu\)W. Applying the same strategy described above, by periodically waking the CPU and powering components only when needed, reduces the total average power consumption to about 50\,\(\mu\)W.
\end{enumerate}

Consider a use case where the tag spends 50\% of the time idle and 50\% of the time in communicating and reflecting modes. Assuming that 10\% of the time slots are allocated for radar-to-IRS communication, the total power consumption would be 183.9\,\(\mu\)W. With an AA battery rated at 2500\,mAh, we estimate that the tag could operate for approximately 566.43 days.

