\noindent \textbf{Van Atta Arrays:} VAAs exhibit unique capabilities, enabling diverse applications across various domains. For instance, Soltanaghaei et al. introduced Millimetro \cite{soltanaghaei2021millimetro}, leveraging VAA-based retro-reflection to achieve centimeter-level localization accuracy. Trzebiatowski et al. designed a 60\,GHz VAA tag \cite{trzebiatowski2022simple} for mmWave identification. While traditional VAAs support retro-reflection primarily in the azimuth plane, Hawkeye \cite{bae2023hawkeye} extends this concept at 77\,GHz to enable retro-reflection in both the azimuth and elevation planes. OmniScatter \cite{bae2022omniscatter} demonstrated a backscatter communication approach by attaching externally controlled switches to modulate the reflected signal using commodity mmWave radars. Moreover, Ang and Eleftheriades \cite{ang2018passive} proved that VAAs can achieve retro-redirection by adjusting the transmission line lengths for each antenna pair. However, existing studies primarily focus on LoS scenarios, and their performance degrades significantly in fully NLoS conditions.

\noindent \textbf{IRS-Aided Communication and Localization:} To simplify the reflection and propagation model and enhance reflected signal strength, IRS has been employed to create stable reflection paths \cite{liu2022sensing, lau2012reconfigurable, wu2019towards, ye2020joint}. Beyond simply reflecting signals at certain angles, IRSs can also modulate signal strength and direction, thereby supporting target positioning \cite{elzanaty2021reconfigurable, zhang2020towards}. For instance, Song et al. \cite{song2022intelligent} developed an NLoS wireless sensing system that infers target direction-of-arrival (DoA) from the AP-IRS-target-IRS-AP link, while Wen et al. \cite{wen2024polarized} introduced a polarized IRS architecture for Two-Dimensional DoA estimation in NLoS environments without requiring prior channel knowledge. Esmaeilbeig et al. \cite{esmaeilbeig2022irs} showed that deploying multiple IRSs can enhance radar performance, proposing a baseline for IRS-aided radar sensing. Although these works analyzed system models and largely considered LoS conditions, a recent study \cite{Dodds2024Around} tackles outdoor NLoS localization by identifying reflecting surfaces using mmWave radar. However, these solutions often depend on additional equipment like LiDAR or assume tagged targets, limiting their broader applicability.

In contrast, our proposed \sn achieves accurate NLoS localization and offers integrated communication capabilities without imposing any requirements on the target. To this end, we designed a VAA-based IRS capable of supporting multiple reflection angles, introduced a novel antenna encoding scheme to enable radar-IRS communication, and developed a time slot allocation algorithm to dynamically adjust reflection angles and sensing duration. This integrated approach enhances the signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) and improves localization accuracy, demonstrating substantial potential for real-world deployment in complex indoor environments.

