To the best of our knowledge, this study is among the first to implement an MD-SPH coupling approach to analyze thermal stress-induced deformation in polyamides, paving the way for advanced multiscale simulations in this class of materials.

Our sequential multiscale framework directly extracts key material properties of superelastic Kapton, such as its stress-strain behaviour, bulk modulus, equation of state, thermal conductivity, and expansion from MD simulations. These properties are then used as input parameters for the SPH model with thermo-mechanical coupling. However, standard SPH formulations have limitations, including instability near shock waves \cite{monaghan1983shock}, tensile instability \cite{monaghan2000sph}, particle interpenetration without sufficient dissipation, and inconsistent zeroth- and first-order accuracy at boundaries \cite{chen1999corrective}. To address these challenges, we incorporate several corrections to the conventional SPH equations based on established improvements from the literature. The results are verified through benchmark problems of heat transfer. Finally, we analyze the insulating capabilities of superelastic Kapton by simulation the thermal shock response of an aluminium plate. The result shows a considerable reduction in the development of stresses and displacement field in the aluminium plate when superelastic Kapton is used as an insulator. The present work demonstrates the effectiveness of the multi-scale method in capturing thermo-mechanical interactions in superelastic polymer/ Kapton. Our findings demonstrate that the multiscale MD-SPH model can effectively capture the responses of superelastic Kapton under thermal stresses. This manuscript is organized as follows: Section 2 details the MD simulations adopted to estimate the different material properties of superelastic Kapton. The SPH methodology with thermo-mechanical coupling is described in Section 3, and subsequently, our validation results are discussed in Section 4. In section 5, we perform numerical simulations to study the effects of insulations on aluminium plates. We end this manuscript with our conclusions and way forward.



\begin{enumerate}
    \item Inputs from MD --- elastic modulus, bulk modulus, linear EoS, superelasticity, thermal conductivity, coefficient of thermal expansion
    \item New in SPH methodology --- coupled thermomechanical stresses
    \item Validation results (without Kapton) of temperature distribution in 1-D and 2-D, thermal stress and thermo-mechanical stress .
    \item New Results --- Alumnium only coupled thermo-mechanical stress, Aluminum+Kapton sheet coupled thermo-mechanical stress.
\end{enumerate}


%Launching the first man-made satellite almost sixty years ago was a momentous event for humanity. Since then, we have launched thousands of man-made satellites at an increasing rate. We are now seeking to explore other planets in the solar system. The space environment is very harsh – the surface of a spacecraft is subjected to collisions with ambient oxygen and nitrogen; ultraviolet and ionizing (such as electrons, protons, and heavy ions) radiation constantly strike the spacecraft along with micro-meteoroids and debris – all of which cause surface degradation \cite{gouzman2019advances}. Apart from these, the ultrahigh vacuum space environment introduces additional distress as it results in the outgassing of the polymer. A common material used to protect the surface of a spacecraft is Kapton \cite{lewis1965kaptan}, a polymer of imide monomers. In aerospace structures, Kapton or its variants are commonly used as a space blanket. The low thermal conductivity of Kapton makes it an ideal material for insulation purposes. However, despite the excellent insulating properties, Kapton’s mechanical and thermal stability under the harsh space environment remains a serious concern \cite{rahnamoun2019chemical}. In conjunction with mechanical and thermal stresses, these conditions may cause significant changes in the molecular structure, altering the different material properties \cite{lu2015reaxff}.

First proposed over 45 years ago by DuPont, Kapton and other polyimide films are being increasingly studied through experiments and simulations. One of the earliest entirely reactive computational studies on Kapton and its derivatives was performed by Rahnamoun and van Duin \cite{rahnamoun2014reactive}. Under low earth orbit conditions, they studied the impact of collision of atomic oxygen on Kapton, polyhedral oligomeric silsesquioxane (POSS), silica, and Teflon using reactive molecular dynamics (MD) simulations through the ReaxFF force field \cite{van2001reaxff}. The authors found that Kapton is less resistant than Teflon towards ambient atomic oxygen damage, while amorphous silica shows the highest resistance. Adhesive properties of polyimide films and silica were investigated computationally by Min et al. \cite{min2017computational} using steered molecular dynamics. All three modes of detachment – pulling, peeling, and sliding – were investigated. Computational atomistic scale simulations have also been used for understanding the chemical dynamics of Kapton films damaged by electron beam irradiation \cite{rahnamoun2019chemical}, water absorption in amorphous Kapton \cite{marque2008molecular}, and pyrolysis in Kapton \cite{lu2015reaxff}. The thermal response of Kapton and other polyamides is yet to be adequately understood. The macroscopic response of Kapton and other polyamides is challenging to capture using MD simulations alone due to computational power limitations. 

Significant research has been done to understand the thermal properties of Kapton, its effect on UV resistance, and stability to ambient oxygen irradiation. Research has also been done to enhance the stability of Kapton by reducing ambient oxygen erosion by adding silicon and other materials to its bulk. However, very little research has been done on superelastic kapton, a variant of kapton polyimide containing central crosslinkers, and its application in aerospace and satellites. The fabrication and experimental studies have been detailed in the work \cite{cheng2021super}. Superelatic kapton, belonging to the covalently crosslinked polyimide family, exhibits properties of high elastic compressive strain and no loss of resilience after thermal shocks making it the perfect choice for aerospace applications. Due to their covalently crosslinked chemical structure, low shrinkage, and density, superelastic Kapton demonstrates exceptional superelasticity, maintaining full reversibility even at 90$\%$ strain and stability through more than $5000$ compressive cycles. Additionally, their outstanding elasticity and resistance to fatigue remain consistent across a broad temperature spectrum ($4$ K to $573$ K), exhibiting virtually no loss of resilience even after exposure to extreme thermal shocks ($\Delta T = 569$ K).

In the last few decades, multiscale modelling has emerged as a method for understanding macroscopic/ continuum scale behaviour through the information obtained at atomic scales \cite{fish2021mesoscopic, ghoniem2003multiscale, lu2004overview}. There are two broad categories of multiscale modelling: sequential and concurrent \cite{lu2004overview}. Sequential multiscaling, which is more prevalent, involves developing a continuum scale model using the information obtained from atomic scale models. Concurrent multiscaling, also called parallel multiscaling, involves using different length scale models in tandem to communicate. An accurate multiscale model's key attributes are the reliability of the continuum-scale and atomic-scale models and their handshaking accuracy. Attempts have been made to develop multiscale frameworks by coupling MD and mesh-based finite element method (FEM) \cite{lee2013multiscale, yamakov2008new}, or meshfree methods \cite{gu2006concurrent, wang2009multiscale} or by combining a coarse-grained continuum thermodynamics model with a fine-scale non-equilibrium MD simulation \cite{li2010multiscale}. The multiscale frameworks can address the scaling problems by leaving the domain of molecular dynamics and entering the domain of continuum scale simulations.

In this work, we propose a novel sequential multiscale modelling framework to study the finite deformation behaviour of superelastic Kapton \cite{cheng2021super} under thermal loading conditions. This approach links the atomic and continuum scales by coupling molecular dynamics (MD) simulations with the continuum-level smoothed particle hydrodynamics (SPH) method. The adoption of these two particle-based techniques for multiscale modelling is underpinned by three main considerations: (i) both MD and SPH rely on particle-based formulations, which naturally facilitate the transition between scales without requiring complex interfacing, (ii) while the two approaches operate at vastly different length and time scales, they have been shown to produce consistent predictions for both liquids and solids; earlier research demonstrated that an isomorphic relationship exists between MD and SPH for liquid systems when parameters are carefully tuned \cite{hoover2006smooth}; although this relationship does not hold directly for solids, equivalent results can still be achieved in solid systems by incorporating material parameters extracted from MD simulations into SPH models \cite{islam2022equivalence, ganesh2022multiscale}, and (iii) SPH, as a mesh-free technique, offers substantial advantages over traditional mesh-dependent methods when handling problems involving large deformations \cite{ganesh2022pseudo}. To the best of our knowledge, this study is among the first to implement an MD-SPH coupling approach to analyze thermal stress-induced deformation in polyamides, paving the way for advanced multiscale simulations in this class of materials.

Our sequential multiscale framework directly extracts key material properties of superelastic Kapton, such as its stress-strain behaviour, bulk modulus, equation of state, thermal conductivity, and expansion from MD simulations. These properties are then used as input parameters for the SPH model with thermo-mechanical coupling. However, standard SPH formulations have limitations, including instability near shock waves \cite{monaghan1983shock}, tensile instability \cite{monaghan2000sph}, particle interpenetration without sufficient dissipation, and inconsistent zeroth- and first-order accuracy at boundaries \cite{chen1999corrective}. To address these challenges, we incorporate several corrections to the conventional SPH equations based on established improvements from the literature. The results are verified through benchmark problems of heat transfer. Finally, we analyze the insulating capabilities of superelastic Kapton by simulation the thermal shock response of an aluminium plate. The result shows a considerable reduction in the development of stresses and displacement field in the aluminium plate when superelastic Kapton is used as an insulator. The present work demonstrates the effectiveness of the multi-scale method in capturing thermo-mechanical interactions in superelastic polymer/ Kapton. Our findings demonstrate that the multiscale MD-SPH model can effectively capture the responses of superelastic Kapton under thermal stresses. This manuscript is organized as follows: Section 2 details the MD simulations adopted to estimate the different material properties of superelastic Kapton. The SPH methodology with thermo-mechanical coupling is described in Section 3, and subsequently, our validation results are discussed in Section 4. In section 5, we perform numerical simulations to study the effects of insulations on aluminium plates. We end this manuscript with our conclusions and way forward.

\section{MD simulation of superelastic kapton}
MD simulations provide the numerical solution to the Newtonian equations of motion corresponding to all N atoms that comprise the system where the total energy is constant:

    \begin{equation}
    \dfrac{d{{x}}^\alpha_i}{dt} = {{v}}^\alpha_i, \ \ \ \dfrac{m_i d{{v}}^\alpha_i}{dt} = -\dfrac{\partial \Phi}{\partial {{x}}^\alpha_i},
    \label{eq:one}
    \end{equation}
the evolution is governed by the inter-atomic potential choice ($\Phi$). Here, ${x}_i^\alpha$ and ${v}_i^\alpha$ denote the $\alpha^{th}$ component of the position and the velocity, respectively, of an $i^{th}$ atom having a mass $m_i$. The numerical solutions provide the microstate of the system at any time $t$, which, when post-processed, can provide quantities of interest such as elastic modulus, fracture strength, thermal conductivity, viscosity, absorption coefficient, etc. Generally, real-life experiments involve either constant pressure and/or constant temperature. Therefore, equation (\ref{eq:one}) can be recast into the NPT ensemble, i.e., constant pressure and temperature environment \cite{kalibaeva2003constant} to consider the environmental effects for realistic modelling:

\begin{equation}
\begin{array}{rcl}
	\dfrac{d{{x}}^\alpha_i}{dt} = {{v}}^\alpha_i + \dfrac{p_\epsilon}{W}x_i^\alpha & , & \dfrac{m d{{v}}^\alpha_i}{dt} =  -\Phi_{i,\alpha}  - \left(\dfrac{N+1}{N} \right) \dfrac{p_\epsilon}{W}mv_i^\alpha - \dfrac{p_{\eta}}{Q}mv_i^\alpha \\
	\dot{V} = \dfrac{3Vp_\epsilon}{W} & , & \dot{p}_\epsilon = 3V (\mathcal{P} - P_0) + \dfrac{1}{N} \sum\limits_{i,\alpha}mv_i^\alpha.v_i^\alpha - \dfrac{p_{\eta}}{Q}p_\epsilon, \\
	\mathcal{P} = \dfrac{1}{3V} \left[ \sum\limits_{i,\alpha} \left( mv_i^\alpha.v_i^\alpha - \dot{x}_i^\alpha.\Phi_{i,\alpha} \right) \right]  & , & \dot{p}_\eta = \sum\limits_{i,\alpha}mv_i^\alpha.v_i^\alpha +  \dfrac{p_\epsilon^2}{W} - (3N+1)k_BT_0.
\end{array}
\label{eq:two}
\end{equation}

In Equation (\ref{eq:two}), the system reaches equilibrium at an average pressure ($P_0$) and temperature ($T_0$) using barostat and thermostat variables, $p_\epsilon$ and $p_\eta$, respectively. These governing equations adjust the instantaneous virial pressure, $\mathcal{P}$, to match $P_0$ by allowing the system's volume, $V$, to vary dynamically. Here, $W$ and $Q$ are the effective masses for the barostat and thermostat mechanisms \cite{kalibaeva2003constant}. During production simulations, the system transitions from the NPT ensemble to the NVT ensemble, wherein $p_\epsilon$ and its time derivative, $\dot{p}_\epsilon$, are set to zero, leaving only the thermostat active \cite{hoover1985canonical}. The time evolution generated by these equations is subsequently analyzed to extract system properties. This analysis assumes ergodic behaviour \cite{sri2021temperature}, implying that time averages of properties like pressure, stress, and temperature from a single molecular dynamics trajectory are representative of their phase-space averages.

\subsection{Potential function for superelastic Kapton:}
MD simulations are exact and free from assumptions if the interaction potential is correct. Thus, the output of the MD simulations depends on the choice of the interaction potential. Kapton and its variants have been typically modelled using two interaction potentials - ReaxFF \cite{van2001reaxff}, and INTERFACE force field \cite{heinz2013thermodynamically}. The ReaxFF uses the bond-order mechanism with polarizable charge descriptions for reactive and non-reactive interactions between the different atoms \cite{senftle2016reaxff}. Unlike the ReaxFF, the INTERFACE force field does not consider any penalty potential, and the potential for bond formation is harmonic. As a result, it cannot directly capture bond creation on the fly. A recent development called INTERFACE-Reactive force field \cite{winetrout2107implementing} uses Morse potential to describe the potential for bond formation, due to which bond creation on the fly can be achieved. In the present work, we have used the ReaxFF potential.

The energy of the system is divided into partial energy contributions given by the following equation:
\begin{equation}
    {E_{system}} = {E_{bond}}+{E_{over}}+{E_{under}}+{E_{val}}+{E_{pen}}+{E_{tors}}+{E_{conj}}+{E_{vdWaals}}+{E_{Coulomb}}
    \label{eq:three}
    \end{equation}

The bond energy $(E_{bond})$ is determined by the bond order. $E_{over}$ and $E_{under}$ represent the correction terms that quantify the extent of deviation of the total uncorrected bond orders surrounding an atomic center from its valency. ${E_{val}}$ represents the energy introduced into the system due to the deviation of the valence angle from its equilibrium value. $E_{pen}$ represents the penalty term. $E_{tors}$ represents the energy associated with the torsional angle, also referred to as the dihedral angle. It models the rotational energy barrier when one segment of the polymer attempts to rotate relative to another along the bond axis. $E_{conj}$ represents the conjugation effect. $E_{vdWaals}$ and $E_{Coulomb}$ represent the energy contributions from non-bonded interactions. Nonetheless, they are computed for all atom pairs within the system, including bonded pairs, to address discontinuities during dissociation when bonded interactions become irrelevant. Readers seeking further information are referred to the article \cite{van2001reaxff}.

\subsection{Evaluation of mechanical and thermal properties of pristine Kapton:}
