The thermal conductivity tensor $\kappa$ is the material property that measures the ability to transfer heat diffusively and is governed by Fourier's law \eqref{eq:Fourier}, where $Q$ is the heat flux vector and $\nabla T$ is the spatial gradient of temperature. Thermal conductivity can be determined through various methodologies in molecular dynamics (MD). One method utilized is non-equilibrium molecular dynamics (NEMD). The objective is to deliver a constant quantity of energy to the hot region while extracting an equivalent amount from the cold region through velocity rescaling, utilizing thermostats to create an energy flux \cite{ikeshoji1994non}. Alternatively, this can be accomplished by explicitly adding and removing heat rather than rescaling velocity. Reverse Non-Equilibrium Molecular Dynamics (rNEMD), also known as the Muller-Plathe method \cite{muller1997simple}, operates by exchanging energy between the hot and cold regions to create a temperature gradient, effectively performing the inverse of Non-Equilibrium Molecular Dynamics (NEMD). In this study, we utilize Non-Equilibrium Molecular Dynamics (NEMD) with temperature rescaling facilitated by Langevin thermostatting.

\begin{equation}
    Q= -{\kappa}{\nabla T}
    \label{eq:Fourier}
\end{equation}

The thermal conductivity of super elastic kapton is calculated by equilibrating the simulation box to a temperature of 300K and a pressure of 1 atm. The regions designated as "hot" and "cold", each of length 4$\AA$ in the z-direction, are established with a temperature of 350K in the hot region and 250K in the cold region. Upon achieving statistical convergence of the temperature difference, the cumulative heat energy added and removed is averaged. The coefficient of thermal conductivity is derived from the Eq. \eqref{eq:TC_calculation_equation}. In this equation, $Q_c$ represents the averaged cumulative heat energy, $A$ denotes the area perpendicular to the direction of heat flux, $\Delta t$ indicates the simulation time, $\Delta T$ signifies the temperature difference between the hot and cold regions, which is 100K in this instance, and $L_z$ refers to the distance separating the hot and cold regions. The cross-sectional area is multiplied by a factor of two to account for the influence of the periodic boundary condition. To verify that thermal conductivity remains unaffected by the dimensions of the simulation box, simulations are conducted using boxes of varying numbers of monomer replications. The outcomes are illustrated in \autoref{fig_TC}. The thermal conductivity approaches a value of 0.32 W/mK.

\begin{equation}
    \kappa= \frac{\frac{Q_c}{(2A \Delta t) }}{\frac{\Delta T}{L_z}}
    \label{eq:TC_calculation_equation}
\end{equation}



