\subsection{Elastic property of superelastic kapton under high compression}
A key advantage of superelastic Kapton compared to pristine Kapton, as the name implies, lies in its elastic behavior even under high compressive strain. To validate this property, the material must return to its initial virial stress state after compression and relaxation loading cyle. To evaluate this, a simulation box with 8 monomer units in each direction was prepared and equilibrated at 300 K and 1 atm pressure using the NVT ensemble. The simulation box was then compressed along the x-axis to half its original length over 125,000 iterations with a timestep of 0.1 fs, with atoms being remapped accordingly. Throughout the simulation, virial stress was continuously monitored. Subsequently, the box was stretched along the x-axis back to its original length over another 125,000 iterations. The results are presented in \autoref{fig_superelastic property}. It can be observed that the simulation box returns to the same virial stress state as at the beginning, confirming the superelastic property. However, the path taken during the process is not identical, which can be attributed to the hysteretic behavior commonly exhibited by superelastic materials \cite{gonzalez2019hysteretic}.

