    Hence, we conduct a 20-client split and present the convergence performance in Fig.~\ref{fig: exp_sparsity} (d). 
    According to the results, we conclude that FedPG maintains stable performance across different participation ratios. 







\section{Conclusion}
    Multi-level FGL heterogeneity, as illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig: heterogeneity_challenges}, poses as a prominent challenge to meet practical demands in distributed graph machine intelligence. 
    While some FGL methods address the DH, a general optimization framework is still lacking. 
    To address this issue, we propose a simple yet effective approach called FedPG.
    The SOTA performances coupled with low communication cost and high running efficiency demonstrate the practicality of our approach. 
    For future work, we believe it would be worthwhile to explore the integration of techniques such as graph augmentation or knowledge distillation to enable global prototypes to more effectively guide local updates.
    Additionally, investigating the effective adaptation of graph-based FPL in continual learning or contrastive learning scenarios presents a promising direction.


