\title{Stochastically Dominant Peer Prediction\thanks{This work was partly carried out at the DIMACS Center at Rutgers University and supported by NSF \#2313137.}}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Peer prediction mechanisms incentivize truthful reporting of private signals in settings where ground truth is unavailable. 
A \emph{truthful} mechanism, which ensures that truth-telling maximizes the \textbf{expected scores}, can elicit honest information while assuming agents' utilities are \textbf{linear functions} of their scores. 
However, in many practical scenarios, non-linear payment rules are preferred, or agents' utilities are inherently non-linear.
For example, students in peer grading may care about letter grades rather than the numerical scores computed by the mechanism.

In this paper, we introduce the concept of \emph{stochastically dominant truthfulness (SD-truthfulness)}, which requires that, in equilibrium, the \textbf{score distribution} of any non-truthful strategy is first-order stochastically dominated by that of truth-telling. 
An SD-truthful mechanism can elicit honest information as long as agents' utilities are \textbf{monotone increasing functions} of their scores.
To the best of our knowledge, no existing peer prediction mechanism naturally satisfies this criterion without strong assumptions. 

We first identify a common rounding reduction that can transform any truthful mechanism into an SD-truthful mechanism by normalizing scores into probabilities and awarding binary scores via a lottery.
However, this reduction often destroys \emph{sensitivity}, an important property concerning the fairness and efficiency of a mechanism.  
We overcome this limitation by showing how a more nuanced application of rounding can partially recover the sensitivity of the original mechanism.  
Furthermore,  we propose a novel \emph{enforced agreement (EA) mechanism} that achieves SD-truthfulness in binary-signal settings under mild assumptions. 
Our empirical results show that EA achieves the highest sensitivity among all known SD-truthful mechanisms.
Unfortunately, this approach does not generalize beyond the binary-signal setting, highlighting a key challenge in obtaining highly sensitive SD-truthfulness in general settings.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Information elicitation studies how to design mechanisms that incentivize honest and high-effort human feedback.
In this framework, a principal seeks reliable responses regarding a set of tasks from strategic agents who are motivated by the designed rewards.
For example, AI companies collect human preferences to align language models via RLHF \citep{ouyang2022training}, and MOOCs use peer grading to evaluate complex assignments at scale \citep{piech2013tuned}.
A key challenge is designing scoring mechanisms that accurately assess the informativeness of agents' reports.

Peer prediction provides an elegant solution for scenarios where the quality of collected reports cannot be directly verified against ground truth \citep{miller2005eliciting}.
This method is particularly valuable when obtaining ground truth is costly or infeasible, such as in subjective tasks.
The core idea is to evaluate each agent based on the correlation between their reports and those of a randomly chosen peer.
The scoring mechanism is designed to be \emph{truthful} so that everyone reporting their information truthfully forms a Bayesian Nash equilibrium, ensuring that truth-telling maximizes each agent's \textbf{expected score}.

A key assumption underlying the effectiveness of existing peer prediction mechanisms is that agents' utility is a \textbf{linear function} of their score. 
This assumption is reasonable in some cases, such as when agents are solely motivated by monetary payments, which can be straightforwardly designed as a linear transformation of the peer prediction scores.
However, practical implementations often favor non-linear transformations, such as tournaments, to map scores to payments due to their improved budgetary efficiency and flexibility \citep{zhang2023higheffort}.
Moreover, agents' intrinsic utility functions are sometimes inherently non-linear. 
For instance, student graders care more about their final letter grades (e.g., A, B, C) than the real-valued numerical scores assigned by the mechanism. 
When agents' utilities are non-linear functions of their scores, truth-telling is no longer guaranteed to be an equilibrium strategy.

Motivated by this key limitation, this paper proposes the concept of \emph{stochastic dominance-truthful (SD-truthful)} peer prediction mechanisms.
Under this stronger truthful guarantee, we aim to design mechanisms in which an agent's \textbf{score distribution}, when everyone reports truthfully, first-order stochastically dominates the score distribution if they unilaterally deviate to any other strategy.
As a result, an SD-truthful mechanism incentivizes truth-telling for agents with \textbf{any utility function that is monotone-increasing with the score}.

\subsection{Our Results}

Our first observation is that \textbf{no existing peer prediction mechanism is naturally SD-truthful under general information structures}.
We investigate three widely studied mechanisms that compute scores as the average of multiple simple discrete scores, including \emph{Output Agreement (OA)}, \emph{Peer Truth Serum (PTS)} \citep{faltings2017pts}, and \emph{Correlated Agreement (CA)} \citep{shnayder2016informed}. 
Mechanisms with complex scoring rules --- such as those that rely on continuous-valued unbiased estimates of mutual information \citep{kong2019information,kong2020dominantly,schoenebeck2020learning} --- are unlikely to satisfy SD-truthfulness. 

However, even these three simple mechanisms fail to achieve SD-truthfulness without imposing strong assumptions on the underlying information structure.
In particular, OA is truthful and SD-truthful only when signals are \emph{self-dominating}, meaning that if an agent observes a signal $\sigma$, their belief that another agent also observes $\sigma$ is always the highest.
While PTS and CA remain truthful under more general information structures, they are not naturally SD-truthful.

Our second observation is that \textbf{achieving SD-truthfulness is easy in principle but challenging in practice}.
Given any truthful mechanism $\mathcal{M}$ with bounded scores $S^{\mathcal{M}} \in [S_{\inf}, S_{\sup}]$, we can define a probability by normalizing the score: $\lambda^\mathcal{M}=\frac{S^{\mathcal{M}}-S_{\inf}}{S_{\sup}-S_{\inf}}\in [0,1]$.
We then score agents via a binary lottery: $\tilde{S}^{\mathcal{M}}=1$ with probability $\lambda^{\mathcal{M}}$ and 0 otherwise.
However, although this approach, called the \emph{direct rounding reduction}, trivially satisfies SD-truthfulness, it greatly reduces \emph{sensitivity} --- an important property related to the efficiency and ex-post fairness of a mechanism.
At a high level, sensitivity measures how much a mechanism's score responds to changes in the information contained in an agent's reports, normalized by the standard deviation of the scores.

To overcome this limitation, \textbf{we propose the \emph{partition rounding reduction}, which helps partially recover the sensitivity of the original mechanism}.
The key idea is to divide the $n$ tasks into $K$ disjoint partitions and apply the original mechanism separately within each partition to compute an individual score $S^{\mathcal{M}}_i$.
The final score for an agent is then the average of $K$ i.i.d.~individual scores after applying the direct rounding if necessary.
Intuitively, this approach improves sensitivity by reducing score variance through the aggregation of multiple independent individual scores.

However, the partition rounding process cannot fully recover the sensitivity of the original mechanisms, implying additional room for more sensitive peer prediction mechanisms.
\textbf{We introduce a novel mechanism called \emph{enforcement agreement (EA)} that is shown to be the most sensitive SD-truthful mechanism in the binary-signal setting}.
EA aims to replicate the key advantage of OA --- its high sensitivity --- while relaxing the strong assumptions on the information structure required for SD-truthfulness.

EA works by enforcing a pre-determined empirical distribution of signals.
In the binary setting, if Alice reports 0 on $m_0 > n_0$ questions (where $n_0$ is the enforced count), the mechanism randomly flips $m_0 - n_0$ of them to 1.
This trick removes agents' incentive to over-report majority signals --- a strategy that undermines the truthful properties of OA.
However, EA is only truthful but not SD-truthful when signals are non-binary, highlighting an important future challenge in designing high-sensitivity SD-truthful mechanisms for more complex settings.

Lastly, our empirical results reinforce and complement our theoretical insights.
In the binary-signal setting, we show that even a prior-free implementation of EA  --- simply enforcing a uniform distribution --- outperforms other SD-truthful mechanisms in most settings, including those that require prior knowledge.
For non-binary-signal settings, the partition rounding reduction of PTS emerges as the most sensitive SD-truthful mechanism.
As a robustness check, we validate our findings by showing that EA consistently achieves the highest \emph{budgetary efficiency}, a practical metric of peer prediction mechanisms that quantifies the minimum budget required to elicit a desired level of effort \citep{zhang2023higheffort}.

