\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related}

\citet{miller2005eliciting} first propose the idea of peer prediction --- using a peer's report to validate an agent's report via proper scoring rules.
Their mechanism is not \emph{detail-free}, meaning that the mechanism requires prior knowledge of the information structure to obtain truthfulness.
There are two ideas to mitigate this limitation: 1) the single-task mechanism, which elicits the information structure directly from agents \citep{prelec2004bayesian}; and 2) the multi-task mechanism, which assumes each agent responds to multiple i.i.d.~tasks but only requires eliciting signals rather than predictions \citep{dasgupta2013crowdsourced}.

Our study is primarily relevant to the multi-task peer prediction literature.
Connecting to our problem, we categorize multi-task peer prediction mechanisms into two types: peer agreement mechanisms \citep{dasgupta2013crowdsourced, faltings2017pts, shnayder2016informed, zhang2023omni, Agarwal2017-ty} and mutual information mechanisms \citep{kong2019information, kong2020dominantly, schoenebeck2020learning, rowdycrowds, Kong_vmi_24, lutextual2024}.

The score of a peer agreement mechanism can be viewed as an average of multiple individual scores, where each individual score usually takes values from a small finite space, e.g.~$\{0,1\}$.
For example, the output agreement (OA) mechanism scores an agent based on the average number of questions that they agree with another agent.
The first multi-task peer prediction mechanism proposed by \citet{dasgupta2013crowdsourced} lies in this category, which can achieve truthfulness in a binary-signal setting.\footnote{In fact, their mechanism is \emph{strongly truthful}, which guarantees the truth-telling equilibrium uniquely maximizes each agent's expected score among all strategy profiles.}
The correlated agreement (CA) mechanism generalizes their idea to any finite-signal setting \citep{shnayder2016informed}.
Later, \citet{kong2019information} show that the payment of CA is actually an estimate of total variation distance mutual information.
The peer truth serum (PTS) mechanism can be viewed as a generalization of OA, generalizing the self-dominating assumption to self-prediction, which basically requires signals to be positively correlated in the binary setting.
In addition, there are two follow-ups of CA aiming to handle heterogeneous agents \citep{Agarwal2017-ty} and more general cheating strategies \citep{zhang2023omni}.

In this paper, we primarily focus on peer agreement mechanisms because stochastic dominance-truthfulness implies that the score of the mechanism needs to take a simple form. 
Intuitively, this is because a complex scoring mechanism provides more opportunities for a cheating strategy to obtain one of the highest scores with a larger probability than truth-telling, which breaks SD-truthfulness.

Therefore, mutual information mechanisms that score each agent by estimating the mutual information between their reports and their peers' reports are unlikely to obtain SD-truthfulness, as these estimations are usually complicated.
In contrast, the \emph{determinant mutual information mechanism} scores an agent based on the determinant of the estimated joint distribution between two agents' reports \citep{kong2020dominantly}; and the score of the \emph{$\Phi$-pairing mechanism} needs to go through a complex machine learning process.
We thus consider more detailed discussions of these mechanisms beyond the scope of this paper.

We further note that the idea of rounding peer prediction scores into a binary lottery was previously proposed by \citet{miller2005eliciting} to address risk-averse agents.
Thus, at least the direct rounding reduction discussed in \cref{sec:rounding} is not novel.
What is new in our work is the comparative analysis of various rounding reductions based on sensitivity, allowing us to identify a more effective rounding approach.
Furthermore, we formally prove that this idea extends beyond risk-averse agents and can be applied to any agents with increasing utility functions.


\section{Model}

We consider the multi-task peer prediction setting where two agents, Alice and Bob, answer the same $n$ i.i.d.~questions.
For each question $j$, agents receive discrete signals $X_{a,j}, X_{b,j}\in\Sigma=\{0,\ldots,c-1\}$, drawn from a fixed joint distribution $\Pr(X_a, X_b)$.
With more than two agents, Bob is chosen randomly as Alice's peer for scoring.

Each agent $i\in \{a,b\}$ applies a strategy $\theta_i\in\Theta$, which maps from signals to distributions over the same space $\Delta_\Sigma$.
The report on question $j$ is denoted as $\hat{X}_{i,j} = \theta_i(X_{i,j})$.
We highlight two types of strategies: the truth-telling strategy $\tau$, which always reports the received signal, and the uninformative strategy $\mu$, where reports are independent of the input.
Examples of uninformative strategies include always reporting the same signal or randomly reporting signals according to the prior.

A peer prediction mechanism $\mathcal{M}$ maps vectors of reports $\hat{X}_a$ and $\hat{X}_b$ to a score for Alice.
Given an information structure (i.e., the joint distribution over signals) and a mechanism, the score is a random function of strategies, denoted as $S^\mathcal{M}(\theta_a, \theta_b)$.
The randomness arises from the signals, strategies, and the mechanism itself.
When it is clear from context, we omit the superscript $\mathcal{M}$.

\subsection{Truthful Guarantees}
Existing peer prediction mechanisms usually focus on the expected scores, guaranteeing that deviations from truth-telling will only reduce the expected scores.
In other words, truth-telling is a Bayesian Nash Equilibrium.
Because we focus on truth-telling under the equilibrium, we assume Bob is always truthfulg.
This means that the score of a peer prediction mechanism reduces to a function of Alice's strategy $\theta$, denoted as $S^\mathcal{M}(\theta)$.

\begin{definition}\label{def:truthful}
    A peer prediction mechanism is {truthful} if the expected score $\mathbb{E}[S(\tau)] \ge \mathbb{E}[S(\theta)]$ for any $\theta\in \Theta$.
\end{definition}

A truthful mechanism guarantees that if agents' utilities are linear functions of the scores, truth-telling is an equilibrium --- any deviation from truth-telling will result in a smaller expected utility.
Previous works have explored stronger notions of truthfulness, such as: \emph{informed truthfulness} \citep{shnayder2016informed}, which guarantees that truth-telling provides each agent with the highest expected score and yields a strictly higher score whenever an agent deviates to an uninformative strategy; and \emph{strong truthfulness} \citep{dasgupta2013crowdsourced}, which ensures that truth-telling uniquely maximizes every agent's expected score.
However, these concepts deal with the equilibrium selection among multiple equilibria, whereas in this paper, we are concerned with the properties of the truth-telling equilibrium. 

However, as we argued earlier, the linear-utility assumption is often undesirable and, in some cases, unrealistic.
% In this paper, our goal is to generalize these concepts to reason about not only expected scores but also the score distributions.
In this paper, we want to design mechanisms that are truthful for a much broader and more reasonable class of utility functions --- specifically, any utility function that is increasing in score.
This motivation naturally leads us to the concept of stochastic dominance.

\begin{definition}\label{def:SDT}
    A peer prediciton mechanism is {stochastic dominance-truthful (SD-truthful)} if the distribution of $S(\tau)$ first-order stochastic dominates (FOSD) the distribution of $S(\theta)$ for any $\theta\in \Theta$, i.e.~$\Pr(S(\tau) \ge t) \ge \Pr(S(\theta) \ge t)$ for any $t\in\mathbb{R}$.
\end{definition}

A standard result in microeconomics suggests that $S(\tau)$ FOSD $S(\theta)$ if and only if $\mathbb{E}[u(S(\tau))]\ge \mathbb{E}[u(S(\theta))]$ holds for every increasing utility function $u$ \citep{Hadar_FOSD}.
Since linear utility functions are a special case of increasing utility functions, it follows that every SD-truthful mechanism is also truthful.

\subsection{Sensitivity}


Our equilibrium concepts are designed to accommodate a variety of prior mechanisms developed for different settings and objectives. 
As a result, they do not require truth-telling to be a strict equilibrium.
However, this leads to an apparent flaw: a trivial mechanism that always assigns Alice a score of 1 is technically truthful --- and even SD-truthful --- by definition. 
Clearly, such a mechanism fails to capture the essence of what a good scoring mechanism should achieve.

This highlights the need for an additional dimension in peer prediction mechanism design called the \emph{sensitivity} \citep{zhang2023higheffort}.
Intuitively, an effective scoring mechanism should meaningfully evaluate the information contained in agents’ reports and ensure that more informative reports receive higher rewards than uninformative ones.

To motivate sensitivity, we introduce effort into our model.
On each question, Alice exerts effort with a probability of $e\in [0,1]$.
If Alice exerts effort on a question, she will observe $X_a$ and report truthfully; otherwise, she will randomly sample a signal from the prior $\Pr(X_a)$ and report it.
Suppose Bob exerts effort on all questions and both Alice and Bob are truth-telling.
Then, Alice's score under mechanism $\mathcal{M}$ becomes a function of $e$, denoted as $S^{\mathcal{M}}(e)$.


\begin{definition}\label{def:sensitivity}
    The sensitivity of a peer prediction mechanism $\mathcal{M}$ at effort level $e$ is 
    $\delta^{\mathcal{M}}(e)\coloneqq  \frac{\nabla \mathbb{E}[S^{\mathcal{M}}(e)]}{\text{std}\left(S^{\mathcal{M}}(e)\right)}$
    where $\nabla$ denotes the derivative operator and $\text{std}$ denotes the standard deviation.
\end{definition}

Sensitivity measures how well a mechanism reflects changes in effort, and thus changes in information, in its expected score.
The mechanism that consistently assigns 1 to all agents, regardless of their reports, has zero sensitivity since its expected score remains unchanged irrespective of effort (assuming $0/0=0$).

Sensitivity is closely related to other practical metrics of peer prediction mechanisms.
Prior work shows that when scores are normally distributed, sensitivity is a sufficient statistic of budgetary efficiency: mechanisms with higher sensitivity can elicit a desired level of effort at lower budgetary costs \citep{zhang2023higheffort}.
Furthermore, sensitivity is closely aligned with \emph{measurement integrity} \citep{burrell2023measurement}, a concept used to assess peer prediction mechanisms' ex-post fairness by measuring the correlation between the quality of agents' reports and their scores allocated by the mechanism \citep{xu2024spot}.

\textbf{Our goal is to design SD-truthful mechanisms that also achieve high sensitivity.}

\section{Prior Mechanisms}
\label{sec:prior}

To the best of our knowledge, no existing peer prediction mechanism naturally achieves SD-truthfulness without imposing strong assumptions on the information structure.
In this section, we introduce three simple and exemplary multi-task peer prediction mechanisms and explain why they are not naturally SD-truthful to motivate our methods. 

The final score $S^{\mathcal{M}}$ of these mechanisms is usually the average of $K$ (sometimes independent) scores $S^{\mathcal{M}}_i$.
We distinguish these two terms by calling $S^{\mathcal{M}}$ the final score and $S_i^{\mathcal{M}}$ the individual score.

\subsection{Output Agreement (OA)}
\label{subsec:oa}

Given two agents' reports on $n$ questions, a straightforward idea is to score Alice based on the fraction of questions on which both agents agree.
This mechanism is commonly termed as \emph{output agreement (OA)}.
In particular, OA repeatedly samples one of the $n$ questions (without replacement) and assigns Alice an individual score of 1 if both agents' reports agree and 0 otherwise. 
The final score is the average of $n$ independent binary individual scores, i.e.~$S^{OA} = \frac{1}{n} \sum_{i\in [n]}S_i^{OA}$ where $S_i^{OA}\in \{0,1\}$.

However, OA encourages agents to over-report the majority signal, which is truthful only under a special type of information structure.

\begin{definition}\label{def:sd}
    Agents' signals are self-dominating if $\Pr(X_b = \sigma | X_a = \sigma) > \Pr(X_b = \sigma' | X_a = \sigma)$ for any $\sigma'\neq \sigma\in \Sigma$.
\end{definition}

\begin{prop}
    The output agreement mechanism is truthful if agents' signals are self-dominating.
\end{prop}

Intuitively, self-dominating information structure implies that whenever Alice observes signal $\sigma$, she believes that Bob is most likely to also observe $\sigma$. 
Therefore, any manipulation will decrease the probability of agreeing with Bob.
Furthermore, self-dominance is almost necessary for OA to be truthful: ignoring the case where two signals have the identical conditioned probability, if there exists $\sigma, \sigma'$ such that  $\Pr(X_b = \sigma | X_a = \sigma) < \Pr(X_b = \sigma' | X_a = \sigma)$, then Alice is motivated to report $\sigma'$ whenever she observes $\sigma$.

In fact, OA is also SD-truthful when signals are self-dominating. 
This is because each individual score $S^{OA}_i$ takes only two possible values, meaning that $S^{OA}_i(\tau)$ FOSD $S^{OA}_i(\theta)$ if and only if $\Pr(S^{OA}_i(\tau)=1)\ge \Pr(S^{OA}_i(\theta)=1)$.
Furthermore, if multiple i.i.d.~individual scores each satisfies SD-truthfulness, their average also satisfies SD-truthfulness (we will formally state this in \cref{subsec:partition}).
Consequently, for OA, SD-truthfulness reduces to truthfulness.

\subsection{Peer Truth Serum (PTS)}
\label{subsec:pts}

Self-dominance is usually considered a strong assumption, especially when the signal space is large.
\citep{radanovic_pts_2016} propose the 
\emph{Peer Truth Serum (PTS)} mechanism which incentivizes truth-telling under more general information structures.

PTS assumes a symmetric and publicly known common prior $R(\sigma)=\Pr(X_a=\sigma)=\Pr(X_b=\sigma)$.
The key idea is to weight the output agreement score reversely to the prior of the signal.
The mechanism repeatedly samples a question $i$ without replacement and if there is an agreement $\hat{X}_{a,i}=\hat{X}_{b,i}=\sigma$, the mechanism assigns an individual score of 1 to Alice $S^{PTS}_i=1/R(\sigma)$, and assigns 0 if there is no agreement.
The final score of PTS is the average of all $S^{PTS}_i$.

By re-weighting the agreement score, PTS relaxes the assumption of self-dominance to \emph{self-prediction}.
When the signal space is binary, self-prediction is equivalent to positive correlation, which is a strictly weaker assumption than self-dominance.
However, self-prediction is not directly comparable with self-dominance when signals are non-binary.

\begin{definition}\label{def:sp}
    Agents' signals are self-predicting if $\Pr(X_b = \sigma | X_a = \sigma) > \Pr(X_b = \sigma | X_a = \sigma')$ for any $\sigma'\neq \sigma\in \Sigma$.
    % (\citet{faltings2017pts})
\end{definition}

\begin{prop}[\citep{faltings2017pts}]
    The peer truth serum mechanism is truthful if agents' signals are self-predicting.
\end{prop}

\smallskip
\textbf{Is PTS SD-truthful?}\;
The short answer is no.
Consider an individual score $S^{PTS}_i$, which can take at most $|\Sigma| + 1$ distinct values.
Let $\sigma_{min}$ be the least likely signal under the prior; then the largest score is $S_{max}=\max\left(S^{PTS}_i\right) = \frac{1}{R(\sigma_{min})}$.
A necessary condition for PTS to be stochastic dominance-truthful is that truth-telling must maximize $\Pr\left(S^{PTS}_i(\tau) = S_{max}\right)$, which is the probability of Alice agreeing with Bob on signal $\sigma_{min}$.

However, this is not true. 
The probability of obtaining an individual score of $S_{max}$ under truth-telling is defined by the joint distribution $\Pr(X_a = \sigma_{min}, X_b = \sigma_{min})$.
If Alice plays an uninformative strategy $\mu$ which always reports $\sigma_{min}$, the probability of getting $S_{max}$ increases to $\Pr(X_b = \sigma_{min})$.
Therefore, although $\mu$ decreases the expected score (because the mechanism is truthful), it leads to a score distribution that is not dominated by truth-telling, meaning that there exists an increasing utility function under which Alice strictly prefers $\mu$ to $\tau$.

\subsection{Correlated Agreement (CA)}
\label{subsec:ca}

SD-truthfulness requires reasoning about the score distribution.
Intuitively, mechanisms with a small number of possible scores are more likely to achieve SD-truthfulness.
We now introduce the correlated agreement (CA) mechanism \citep{shnayder2016informed} whose score has three possible values: -1, 0, and 1.
We show that this simple mechanism is not SD-truthful (for a different reason).

Suppose the joint distribution $\Pr(X_a, X_b)$ is known.
The CA mechanism first computes the delta matrix $\Delta_{\sigma,\sigma'} = \Pr(X_a = \sigma, X_b = \sigma') - \Pr(X_a = \sigma)\Pr(X_b = \sigma')$, which is the difference between the joint distribution and the product of marginal distributions.
Let $T_\Delta(\sigma,\sigma') = \text{Sign}(\Delta)_{\sigma,\sigma'}$ be the agreement function where $\text{Sign}(x) = 1$ if $x>0$ and 0 otherwise.
Intuitively, $T_\Delta(\sigma,\sigma')=1$ if the pair of signals $(\sigma,\sigma')$ appears more often on the same question than on distinct questions.

Then, the mechanism repeatedly draws a bonus question $j$ and a penalty question $k$.
For an individual score $S^{CA}_i$, Alice gets 1 if both agents ``agree'' on $j$ and gets $-1$ if Alice's response on $j$ ``agrees'' with Bob's response on $k$, where the agreement is determined by $T_\Delta$, i.e.~$S^{CA}_i=T_{\Delta}(\hat{X}_{a,j}, \hat{X}_{b,j}) - T_{\Delta}(\hat{X}_{a,j}, \hat{X}_{b,k})$.
Each bonus question can be paired with $n-1$ possible penalty questions.
Therefore, the final score of CA is the average of $n\cdot (n-1)$ individual scores.

Intuitively, the CA mechanism works by encouraging correlations on the same question and penalizing correlations on distinct questions. 
Consequently, if Alice always reports the majority signal, the frequency of agreements on the bonus question will be identical to the frequency of agreements on the penalty questions, which results in a score of 0.

Previous results suggest that if the information structure $\Pr(X_a, X_b)$ is known (or accurately estimated), CA is truthful \citep{shnayder2016informed}.\footnote{In fact, under these conditions, CA is informed-truthful.}

\smallskip
\textbf{Is CA SD-truthful?}\;
The answer depends on how we implement the mechanism.
In general, the original implementation of CA described above is not SD-truthful.
However, we first present an intermediate result focusing on scoring Alice with an individual CA score $S^{CA}_i$.

\begin{prop}\label{prop:ca_sdt_single}
    When $|\Sigma|=2$, instead of taking the average, the mechanism that scores agents using an individual CA score $S^{CA}_i$ is SD-truthful.\footnote{When $|\Sigma|\ge 3$, counter-examples exist showing that scoring agents with $S^{CA}_i$ is no longer SD-truthful for general information structure.}
\end{prop}

However, as we will soon discuss in \cref{sec:rounding}, scoring agents via a binary score is not sensitive.
In practice, we need to take the average over these samples as the final score.
Our second observation warns that we should be careful about this averaging process: \textbf{repeatedly using the same question as the bonus or the penalty question will sabotage the SD-truthfulness of the mechanism.}

\begin{example}\label{exm:counter_ca}
Suppose $n=2$, meaning that Alice knows each of the two questions she answers will be used once as the bonus question and once as the penalty question.
Her final score is the average of two samples, where each sample takes values $S^{CA}_i\in \{-1,0,1\}$.
Consequently, the final score can take five possible values: $S^{CA}\in \{-1,-0.5,0,0.5,1\}$.
For SD-truthfulness, a necessary condition is that for any strategy $\theta$, we must have $\Pr(S^{CA}(\tau)=-1)\le \Pr(S^{CA}(\theta)=-1)$.
However, we provide a counterexample in the binary-signal setting showing that this condition does not hold.

Let $J_{\sigma,\sigma'}=\Pr(X_a=\sigma, X_b=\sigma')$ denote the joint distribution, and let $M^a_\sigma = \Pr(X_a=\sigma)$ and $M^b_\sigma = \Pr(X_b=\sigma)$ denote the marginal distribution of Alice and Bob's signals, respectively, where $\sigma,\sigma'\in \{0,1\}$.
Suppose the signals are positively correlated, i.e.~$T_\Delta$ is a diagonal matrix.
Alice receives a score of $-1$ if and only if 1) she reports different signals on the two questions, and 2) the agents disagree on both questions.
Under truth-telling, this occurs with a positive probability: $\Pr(S^{CA}(\tau)=-1)=2J_{0,1}J_{1,0}$.
However, if Alice always reports the same signal, she can avoid this outcome entirely.
This suggests that an uninformative strategy $\mu(\sigma) = 0$ for any $\sigma$ is not stochastically dominated by truth-telling.
\end{example}

Consequently, while the original implementation of the CA mechanism achieves the highest sensitivity, it is not SD-truthful.
We will discuss how to implement CA (and other mechanisms) to achieve SD-truthfulness without destroying its sensitivity.

\section{A Rounding Reduction For Stochastically Dominant-Truthfulness}
\label{sec:rounding}

% Our insights from the previous section suggest that stochastic dominance-truthful mechanisms tend to favor simple designs.
In this section, we introduce a straightforward rounding reduction that can transform a truthful mechanism into an SD-truthful one. 
However, this approach often comes at a huge cost of sensitivity. 
To address this, we propose a more refined application of the rounding technique that partially restores the sensitivity of the original mechanism.

\subsection{Direct Rounding}
\label{subsec:rounding}

Consider a peer prediction mechanism $\mathcal{M}$ that assigns Alice a score $S^{\mathcal{M}}$ with bounded support $S^{\mathcal{M}}_{\sup}<\infty$ and $S^{\mathcal{M}}_{\inf}>-\infty$.
In \emph{direct rounding}, we normalize the score to a probability: $\lambda^{\mathcal{M}} = \frac{S^{\mathcal{M}} - S^{\mathcal{M}}_{\inf}}{S^{\mathcal{M}}_{\sup} - S^{\mathcal{M}}_{\inf}}\in[0,1]$.
Alice’s final score $\tilde{S}^{\mathcal{M}}$ is then determined by a binary lottery: receiving 1 with probability $\lambda^{\mathcal{M}}$ and 0 otherwise.

\begin{prop}\label{prop:direct}
    The direct rounding reduction of a truthful mechanism $\mathcal{M}$ with bounded scores is SD-truthful.
\end{prop}

The proof is straightforward.
First, note that the rounded score $\tilde{S}^{\mathcal{M}}$ is a Bernoulli random variable with success probability $\mathbb{E}[\lambda^{\mathcal{M}}]$.
For any truthful mechanism $\mathcal{M}$, truth-telling maximizes the expected score, which in turn maximizes the expected success probability of $\tilde{S}^{\mathcal{M}}$.
Since $\tilde{S}^{\mathcal{M}}$ takes only binary values, maximizing the probability of receiving a score of 1 ensures that truth-telling stochastically dominates the score distribution of any other strategy.
Therefore, scoring agents according to $\tilde{S}^{\mathcal{M}}$ is SD-truthful.

However, scoring agents via a single binary score is clearly not practical.
The following proposition suggests that direct rounding usually destroys the sensitivity of the original mechanism.
Let $\delta^{\mathcal{M}}(e)$ and $\tilde{\delta}^{\mathcal{M}}(e)$ be the sensitivity of $\mathcal{M}$ and its direct rounding reduction. 
Additionally, let $m^{\mathcal{M}}(e)$ and $\text{std}^{\mathcal{M}}(e)$ be the expected score and the standard deviation.

\begin{prop}\label{prop:sensitivity_direct}
    The sensitivity ratio between a mechanism and its direct rounding is:
        \[\frac{\delta^{\mathcal{M}}(e)}{\tilde{\delta}^{\mathcal{M}}(e)} = \frac{\sqrt{\left(S^{\mathcal{M}}_{\sup} - m^{\mathcal{M}}(e)\right)(m^{\mathcal{M}}(e)-S^{\mathcal{M}}_{\inf})}}{\text{std}^{\mathcal{M}}(e)}.\]
\end{prop}

\begin{proof}[Proof of \cref{prop:sensitivity_direct}]
    By definition, the sensitivity of a mechanism with score $S(e)$ at effort level $e$ is 
    \begin{equation*}
        \delta^{\mathcal{M}}(e) = \frac{\nabla m^{\mathcal{M}}(e)}{\text{std}^{\mathcal{M}}(e)}.
    \end{equation*}

    The direct rounding of $S^{\mathcal{M}}(e)$, denoted as $\tilde{S}^{\mathcal{M}}(e)$, is a Bernoulli variable with success probability $\mathbb{E}[\lambda^{\mathcal{M}}]=\frac{m^{\mathcal{M}}(e) - S^{\mathcal{M}}_{\inf}}{S^{\mathcal{M}}_{\sup} - S^{\mathcal{M}}_{\inf}}$.
    Therefore,
    \begin{align}
        & \nabla \mathbb{E}\left[\tilde{S}^{\mathcal{M}}(e)\right] = \frac{1}{S^{\mathcal{M}}_{\sup} - S^{\mathcal{M}}_{\inf}}\cdot \nabla m^{\mathcal{M}}(e) \notag\\
        & \text{std}\left(\tilde{S}^{\mathcal{M}}(e)\right) = \sqrt{\mathbb{E}[\lambda^{\mathcal{M}}]\left(1-\mathbb{E}[\lambda^{\mathcal{M}}]\right)} = \frac{1}{S^{\mathcal{M}}_{\sup} - S^{\mathcal{M}}_{\inf}}\cdot \sqrt{(m^{\mathcal{M}}(e)-S^{\mathcal{M}}_{\inf})\left(S^{\mathcal{M}}_{\sup} - m^{\mathcal{M}}(e)\right)}\notag\\
        \Rightarrow\quad& \tilde{\delta}^{\mathcal{M}}(e) = \frac{\nabla m^{\mathcal{M}}(e) }{\sqrt{(m^{\mathcal{M}}(e)-S^{\mathcal{M}}_{\inf})\left(S^{\mathcal{M}}_{\sup} - m(e)\right)}}.
    \end{align}\label{eq:delta_direct}
\end{proof}

Intuitively, this ratio is usually large.
Consider the output agreement mechanism, whose final score is the average of $n$ i.i.d.~binary scores, i.e.~a binomial distribution with a success probability of exactly $m(e)$.
A standard result for the binomial variable suggests that the standard deviation of OA is $\sqrt{\frac{m(e)(1-m(e))}{n}}$.
Therefore, the ratio between the sensitivity of OA and its direct rounding reduction is $\sqrt{n}$.
This great difference highlights a significant drawback of direct rounding: while it ensures stochastic dominance, it significantly reduces the sensitivity of the mechanism by preventing the score from averaging out over multiple questions.

\subsection{Partition Rounding}
\label{subsec:partition}

To reduce the variance of the final score and improve sensitivity, we need to make better use of the information from the $n$ questions.
In particular, if the final score of the peer prediction mechanism $S^{\mathcal{M}}$ is the average of $K$ individual scores $S^{\mathcal{M}}_i$, we can apply direct rounding to each individual score and then average the results after rounding.
The following lemma proves the feasibility of this idea.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:Kto1}
Let $S=\frac{1}{K}\sum_{i\in [K]}S_i$, where the individual scores $S_i$ are i.i.d..
Then, $S(\tau)$ first-order stochastically dominates $S(\theta)$ if and only if each individual score $S_i(\tau)$ first-order stochastically dominates $S_i(\theta)$.
\end{lemma}

We defer the proof, which follows from a straightforward coupling argument, to \cref{app:Kto1}.
\Cref{lem:Kto1} motivates the idea of \emph{partition rounding}, where the set of $n$ questions is divided into $K$ disjoint subsets.
Each subset is then used to compute an individual score, which is subsequently rounded using the direct rounding reduction.
% This approach is detailed in Algorithm \ref{alg:partition}.
The partition process guarantees that each individual score is i.i.d.~because the questions are assumed to be i.i.d.~and Alice's strategy is task-independent.
This design prevents the failure observed in the original CA mechanism, which suggests that each question should be used only once when computing an individual score.

\begin{prop}
    The partition rounding reduction of a truthful mechanism $\mathcal{M}$ with bounded scores is SD-truthful.
\end{prop}

The proposition follows directly from \cref{prop:direct} and \cref{lem:Kto1}.

We now show that the partition rounding reduction can partially recover the sensitivity of the original mechanism.
The key observation is that the final score under partition rounding is the average of a binomial variable, i.e.~$\hat{S}^{\mathcal{M}}(e)=\frac{1}{K}\mathrm{Bin}(K, \lambda^{\mathcal{M}}(e))$, where $\lambda^{\mathcal{M}}(e)=\frac{m^{\mathcal{M}} - S^{\mathcal{M}}_{\inf}}{S^{\mathcal{M}}_{\sup} - S^{\mathcal{M}}_{\inf}}$ with $m^{\mathcal{M}}$ being the expectation of the individual score.
Putting this into \cref{def:sensitivity} gives us the sensitivity of the partition rounding reduction
\begin{equation}\label{eq:delta_partition}
    \hat{\delta}^{\mathcal{M}}(e) = \frac{\nabla m^{\mathcal{M}}(e)\cdot \sqrt{K}}{\sqrt{(m^{\mathcal{M}}(e)-S^{\mathcal{M}}_{\inf})\left(S^{\mathcal{M}}_{\sup} - m^{\mathcal{M}}(e)\right)}}.
\end{equation}
The sensitivity of the partition rounding reduction is exactly $\sqrt{K}$ times that of the direct rounding reduction.
This means that by using partition rounding, we can recover a substantial portion of the original mechanism’s sensitivity.

\begin{remark}
    As discussed in \cref{sec:related}, \citet{miller2005eliciting} have proposed the rounding idea to address risk-averse agents.
    Our results in this section formally validate that this idea can be extended beyond risk-averse agents to any agents with increasing utility functions.
    Furthermore, we compare various rounding reductions based on sensitivity, suggest partition rounding reduction for its high sensitivity, and warn that questions cannot be repeatedly used.
\end{remark}

\subsubsection{Sensitivity of the Partition-Rounded Mechanisms}
\label{subsec:sensitivity_partition}

We now map the exemplary mechanisms discussed in \cref{sec:prior} into the partition rounding framework.
In particular, we specify the key parameters $K$, $S_{\inf}$, $S_{\sup}$, and $m_i(e)$ for each mechanism.
Consider an information structure with joint distribution $J$, and the marginal distribution of Alice and Bob being $M^a$ and $M^b$, respectively.
Furthermore, let $M_{\sigma,\sigma'} = M^a_\sigma M^b_{\sigma'}$ denote the product of marginal distributions.

\paragraph{Output Agreement}
An individual score of OA, $S^{OA}_i$, is 1 if both agents agree on a question $i$ and 0 otherwise.
Therefore, OA is the partition rounding reduction of itself with $K=n$, $S_{\inf}=0$, and $S_{\sup}=1$.
By definition, when Alice exerts effort, agents' signals on the same question is sampled from the joint distribution $J$; 
while if Alice does not exert effort, she will sample a signal from the prior, meaning that agents' signals on the same question is sampled from $M$.
The expectation of an individual score at the effort level $e$ is thus 
% \gs{must define $tr$ as trace, and ideally also define trace.  Not everyone will know.}
\[m_i^{OA}(e) = e\cdot\Pr(X_{a,i}=X_{b,i}) + (1-e)\cdot \Pr(\mu(X_{a,i})=\mu(X_{b,i})) = e\cdot \mathrm{tr}(J) + (1-e)\cdot \mathrm{tr}(M).\]
This means that the derivative of the expected score w.r.t.~effort $e$ is 
\[\nabla m^{OA}_i(e) =\mathrm{tr}(J-M)=\mathrm{tr}(\Delta),\]
where $\Delta$ is the difference between the joint distribution and the product of marginal distributions, and $\mathrm{tr}(\cdot)$ represents the trace of a matrix, i.e.~the sum of the diagonal entries.


\paragraph{Peer Truth Serum}
Similar to OA, each partition of PTS is composed of a single question so that $K=n$, $S_{\inf}=0$, and $S_{\sup}=\frac{1}{M^a_{\sigma_{\min}}}$ where $\sigma_{\min}$ is the signal occurs with the smallest probability in prior.
The expected individual score is 
\[m_i^{PTS}(e)=\sum_{\sigma\in\Sigma}e\cdot \frac{J_{\sigma,\sigma}}{M^a_\sigma} + (1-e)\cdot M^b_\sigma=1+e\cdot\sum_{\sigma\in\Sigma} \frac{\Delta_{\sigma,\sigma}}{M^a_\sigma};\qquad \nabla m^{PTS}_i(e) =\sum_{\sigma\in\Sigma} \frac{\Delta_{\sigma,\sigma}}{M^a_\sigma}.\]


\paragraph{Correlated Agreement}
The CA mechanism requires two questions to compute an individual score, meaning that $K=n/2$.
By \cref{prop:ca_sdt_single} and \cref{lem:Kto1}, when the signal space is binary, scoring Alice using $S^{CA} = \sum_{i\in [K]}S^{CA}_i$ without rounding is SD-truthful.
However, when $|\Sigma|>2$, we have to use the partition rounding reduction of CA to obtain SD-truthful where $S_{\inf}=-1$ and $S_{\sup}=1$.
On the bonus question, the probability of receiving a score of 1 is given by $\sum_{\sigma,\sigma'}\left(e\cdot J_{\sigma,\sigma'}+(1-e)\cdot M_{\sigma,\sigma'}\right)T_\Delta(\sigma,\sigma')$.
On the penalty question, the probability of penalizing a score of 1 is given by $\sum_{\sigma,\sigma'}\left(e\cdot M_{\sigma,\sigma'}+(1-e)\cdot M_{\sigma,\sigma'}\right)T_\Delta(\sigma,\sigma')$.
Combining these,
\[m_i^{CA}(e)=e\cdot\sum_{\sigma,\sigma'\in\Sigma}\Delta_{\sigma,\sigma'}\;T_\Delta(\sigma,\sigma')=e\cdot\sum_{\sigma,\sigma'\in\Sigma}(\Delta_{\sigma,\sigma'})^+;\qquad \nabla m^{CA}_i(e) =\sum_{\sigma,\sigma'\in\Sigma}(\Delta_{\sigma,\sigma'})^+,\]
where $(x)^+ = x$ if $x>0$ and 0 otherwise.
Putting these parameters into \cref{eq:delta_partition} returns the sensitivity of the partition rounding reduction for CA.
However, note that by \cref{prop:ca_sdt_single}, we do not need to round the individual CA scores when $|\Sigma|=2$.
This motivates a variant of an SD-truthful implementation of CA that may have a slightly larger sensitivity in the binary-signal setting. 
We will introduce and test this implementation in \cref{sec:experiment}.

We summarize the limitations of the above three SD-truthful mechanisms.
These limitations motivate the search for novel mechanisms, which we explore in the next section.
\begin{itemize}
    \item OA does not require the partition rounding reduction to achieve SD-truthfulness and thus has a high sensitivity. 
    However, OA is SD-truthful only when signals are self-dominating.
    \item PTS requires rounding to ensure SD-truthfulness. The normalizing factor $\frac{1}{M^a_{\sigma_{\min}}}$ implies that PTS will be less sensitive when the prior distribution of signals is biased.
    \item CA uses two questions to compute an individual score, so $K=n/2$.
    Furthermore, only bonus questions contribute to sensitivity, while penalty questions are used to enforce truthfulness.
    Therefore, even without rounding, e.g.~when $|\Sigma|=2$, only $1/4$ of the questions count for the sensitivity.
    Moreover, the rounding process divides each score by a factor of 2, which further harms the sensitivity of the mechanism.
\end{itemize}

