\section{A Multi-Preferential Logic with Typicality} \label{sec:conditional_logic}


{In this section we define a two-valued preferential logic with typicality, which generalizes Kraus Lehmann and Magidor preferential semantics \citep{KrausLehmannMagidor:90,whatdoes}, 
by allowing for multiple preference relations (i.e., preferences with respect to multiple aspects), rather that a single preference relation.
We also develop a notion of weighted conditional knowledge base, which will be instantiated, later, for Conditional ASP.


Let ${\cal L}$ be a propositional logic, whose formulae are built from a set $Prop$ of propositional variables using the boolean connectives $\wedge$, $\vee$, $\neg$ and $\rightarrow$, as usual. 
We assume that $\bot$ (representing falsity) and $\top$ (representing truth) are formulae of ${\cal L}$.

We extend ${\cal L}$ by introducing a typicality operator as done in \cite{lpar2007} and \cite{BoothCasiniAIJ19} for the propositional calculus.
Intuitively, ``a sentence of the form $\tip(\alpha)$ is understood to refer to the {\em typical situations in which $\alpha$ holds}" \citep{BoothCasiniAIJ19}.
%
The typicality operator allows for the formulation of  {\em  conditional implications} (or {\em defeasible implications}) of the form 
$\tip(\alpha) \rightarrow \beta$
whose meaning is that ``normally, if $\alpha$ then $\beta$'', 
or ``in the typical situations when $\alpha$ holds, $\beta$ also holds''.
They correspond to conditional implications $\alpha \ent \beta$ of KLM preferential logics \citep{whatdoes}, but here we consider a preferential semantics which exploits multiple preferences. 

As in PTL \citep{BoothCasiniAIJ19}, the typicality operator cannot be nested.
When $\alpha$ and $\beta$ do not contain occurrences of the typicality operator, an implication $\alpha \rightarrow \beta$ is called {\em strict}. 
We call ${\cal L}^\tip$ the logic obtained by extending  ${\cal L}$ with a unary typicality operator $\tip$. 
In the logic ${\cal L}^\tip$,  {\em general implications}  $\alpha \rightarrow \beta$ are allowed, where $\alpha$ and $\beta$ may contain occurrences of the typicality operator.


As mentioned above, the interpretation of a typicality formula  $\tip(\alpha)$ is defined with respect to a {\em multi-preferential interpretation}.
The KLM preferential semantics \citep{KrausLehmannMagidor:90,whatdoes,Pearl:88} exploits a set of worlds $\WW$, with their valuation and a preference relation $<$ among worlds (where $w < w'$ means that world $w$ is more normal than world $w'$). 
A conditional $A\ent B$ is  satisfied in a KLM preferential interpretation, if $B$ holds in all the most normal worlds satisfying $A$, i.e., in all $<$-minimal worlds satisfying $A$.

Here, instead, we consider a multi-preferential semantics, where preference relations are associated to distinguished propositional variables $A_1, \ldots, A_n \in\mathit{Prop}$ (called {\em distinguished propositions} in the following). The idea is that how much a situation (a world) is normal (or less atypical) with respect to another one depends on the aspects considered for comparison.
The semantics introduced below exploits a set of preference relations $<_{A_i}$, each associated to a distinguished proposition $A_i$, where $w <_{A_i} w'$ means that world $w$ is less atypical than world $w'$ concerning aspect $A_i$.
For instance, referring to the example in the introduction, a world $w$ can be regarded as capturing a more plausible situation describing a student than world $w'$ (i.e., $w <_\mathit{student} w'$),
but a less plausible situation than $w'$ describing an employee (i.e., $w' <_\mathit{employee} w$).

Multi-preferential semantics have been previously considered for defining refinements of the rational closure construction \citep{AIJ21}, 
for defining preferences with respect to different modules of a conditional knowledge base \citep{NMR2020}, for defining a concept-wise semantics in ranked defeasible $\el$ knowledge bases \citep{TPLP2020}, and weighted conditionals in many-valued DLs with typicality \citep{IJAR23}.  

Given a finite set of {\em distinguished propositions} $A_1, \ldots, A_n$, we let  $\leq_{A_i} \subseteq W\times W$ be the {\em preorder} associated with $A_i$ ($w \leq_{A_i} w'$ means that $w$ is not less plausible than $w'$). 
A strict partial order $<_{A_i}$ and an equivalence relation $\sim_{A_i}$ can be defined as usual from the preorder $\leq_{A_i}$ by letting 
$w <_{A_i} '$ iff $w \leq_{A_i} w'$ and  $w' \not \leq_{A_i} w$, and letting $w \sim_{A_i} w'$ iff $w \leq_{A_i} w'$ and  $w' \leq_{A_i} w$.

\subsection{Multi-preferential semantics}

In the following, we  limit our consideration to finite KBs, and restrict our attention to preferential interpretations with a finite set of preference relations $<_{A_i}$, one for each distinguished proposition  $A_i$. 
For the moment, we assume that, in any typicality formula $\tip(A)$, $A$ is a distinguished proposition.
In Section \ref{sec:combining_preferences} we will lift this restriction.  



\begin{definition}\label{MPinterpretations}
A {\em (multi-)preferential  interpretation}  is a triple $\emme= \sx \WW, \{<_{A_i}\}, v \dx$ where:
\begin{quote} 

$\bullet$ \ $\WW$ is a non-empty set of worlds;


$\bullet$ \  each $<_{A_i} \subseteq \WW \times \WW$ is an irreflexive and transitive relation on $\WW$; % for all formulae $A_i \in {\cal L};


$\bullet$ \ $v: \WW \longrightarrow 2^\mathit{Prop} $ is a valuation function, assigning to each world $w$ a set of propositional variables in $\mathit{Prop} $, the variables which are true in $w$.
\end{quote}

\end{definition} 
\noindent
A \em ranked interpretation} is a (multi-)preferential interpretation  $\emme= \sx \WW,\{<_{A_i}\}, v \dx$ for which all preference relations $<_{A_i}$ are {\em modular}, that is: for all $x, y, z$, if
$x < _{A_i} y$ then $x <_{A_i} z$ or $z <_{A_i} y$ (the preorder $\leq_{A_i}$ is total).
%
A relation $<_{A_i}$  is {\em well-founded} if it does not allow for infinitely descending chains of worlds $w_0, w_1, w_2, \ldots$. with $w_1 <_{A_i} w_0$, \  $w_2 <_{A_i} w_1$, $\ldots$.

The valuation $v$ is inductively extended to  all formulae of ${\cal L}^\tip$: 
\begin{quote}
 $\emme, w \models \top$  \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \  $\emme, w \not \models \bot$

 $\emme, w \models p$ \  iff 	 $p \in  v(w)$, \ \ for all $p \in \mathit{Prop}$

$\emme, w \models A \wedge B$ iff $\emme, w \models A $ and $\emme, w \models B$

$\emme, w \models A \vee B$ iff $\emme, w \models A $ or $\emme, w \models B$

$\emme, w \models \neg A$ iff $\emme, w \not \models A $ 

$\emme, w \models A \ri B$ iff $\emme, w \models A $ implies $\emme, w \models B$

$\emme, w \models \tip(A_i)$ iff $  \emme, w \models A_i $ and $ \mathit{\nexists w' \in \WW}$ \ s.t.$ \mathit{ w' <_{A_i} w} \mbox{  and } \emme, w' \models A_i.$

\end{quote} 
Whether $\tip(A_i)$ is satisfied at a world $w$  also depends on the other worlds of the interpretation $\emme$.


Let $ [[A]]^ \emme$ be the set of all the worlds in $\emme$ satisfying a formula $A$ (i.e., $ [[A]]^ \emme=\{w \in \WW : \emme,w \models A \} $)
and let $Min_{<_{A}}(\mathcal{S})$ be the set of $<_{A}$-minimal worlds in $\mathcal{S}$, for any set of worlds $\mathcal{S} \subseteq \WW$, and strict partial order $<_A$, that is:
\begin{quote}
$Min_{<_{A}}(\mathcal{S})= \{ w \in \mathcal{S} \mid   \mbox{ there is no } w' \in \mathcal{S},  \mbox{ such that } w' <_{A} w   \}$
\end{quote}
Note that, when $\mathcal{S} \neq \emptyset$ and the preference relation $<_{A} $ is well-founded, the set $Min_{<_{A}}(\mathcal{S})$ is non-empty. For a well-founded preference relation $<_{A} $,
one can reformulate the semantic condition for the typicality operator as follows:

\begin{quote}
$\emme, w \models \tip(A_i)$ \  iff \  $w \in Min_{<_{A_i}}([[A_i]]^\emme)$.
\end{quote}

We say that a formula  {\em $A$ is satisfiable} in the multi-preferential semantics if there exist a multi-preferential interpretation $\emme= \sx \WW, \{<_{A_i}\}, v \dx$  and a world $w \in \WW$ such that $\emme,w \models A$.
A formula {\em $A$ is  valid in an interpretation $\emme$} (written $\emme \models A$) if, for all worlds $w \in \WW$, $\emme,w \models A$.
A formula $A$ is {\em valid} in the multi-preferential semantics (simply, {\em A is valid}) if $A$ is valid in any multi-preferential interpretation $\emme$.
%
Restricting our consideration to ranked interpretations, leads to the notions of satisfiability and validity of a formula in the {\em ranked (or rational) multi-preferential semantics}.



As mentioned above, when a defeasible implication has the form $\tip(A)  \rightarrow B$, with $B$ in ${\cal L}$, it stands for a conditional  $A \ent B$ in KLM logics \citep{KrausLehmannMagidor:90}. 
Note that, if we assume that all preference relations are well-founded, a defeasible implication $\tip(A)  \rightarrow B$ is valid in a preferential interpretation $\emme$ (i.e., $\emme \models \tip(A)  \rightarrow B$) {\em iff} for all worlds $w \in \WW$, $w \in Min_{<_{A}}([[A]]^\emme)$ implies $w \in [[B]]^\emme$, i.e., {\em iff} $Min_{<_{A}}([[A]]^\emme) \subseteq [[B]]^\emme$ holds. 
In this case, if we assume that all the preference relations  $<_{A}$ coincide with a single well-founded preference relation $<$, then a multi-preferential interpretation $\emme$ becomes a preferential interpretation as in KLM semantics: 
\begin{center}
$\emme \models \tip(A)  \rightarrow B$ 
{\em iff} $Min_{<}([[A]]^\emme) \subseteq [[B]]^\emme$,
\end{center}
i.e., $\emme \models \tip(A)  \rightarrow B$ holds when  the conditional formula $A \ent B$ is satisfied in the preferential model $\emme$ according to the preferential KLM semantics \citep{KrausLehmannMagidor:90} (resp., to  the rational KLM semantics, when $<$ is assumed to be modular). 
The multi-preferential semantics is indeed a generalization of the KLM preferential semantics.  


Let a {\em knowledge base $K$} be a set of (strict or defeasible) implications.
A {\em preferential model of $K$} is a multi-preferential interpretation $\emme$ 
such that $\emme \models A \ri B$, for all implications $A \ri B$ in $ K$.
%
Given a knowledge base $K$,  we say that an implication {\em  $A  \rightarrow B$ is  preferentially entailed from}  $K$  \ if \ $\emme \models A  \rightarrow B $ holds, for all preferential models $\emme$ of $K$. We say that  {\em  $A  \rightarrow B$ is rationally entailed from}  $K$  \ if \ $\emme \models A  \rightarrow B $ holds, for all ranked models $\emme$ of $K$.

It is well known that preferential entailment and rational entailment are weak. As with the rational closure \citep{whatdoes} and the lexicographic closure \citep{Lehmann95} for KML conditionals, also in the multi-preferential case one can strengthen entailment by restricting to specific preferential models,  
based on some {\em closure constructions}, which allow to define the preference relations $<_{A_i}$ from a knowledge base $K$, also exploiting the ranks and weights of conditional implications, when available.
Some examples of closure constructions for the multi-preferential case have been considered,
e.g., for variants of the rational closure \citep{AIJ21} and of the lexicographic closure \citep{NMR2020}, and for ranked or weighted defeasible DLs with typicality \citep{TPLP2020,IJAR23}. 


In the next section, we define an approach for combining an ASP program with a  conditional knowledge base, and develop a construction based on the weighted conditional KB for reasoning on the answer sets of the ASP programs.  



\subsection{Weighted conditional knowledge bases} \label{sec:weighted_KBs}

It is well known that preferential entailment and rational entailment are weak. As with the rational closure \cite{whatdoes} and the lexicographic closure \cite{Lehmann95} for KML conditionals, also in the multi-preferential case one can strengthen entailment by restricting to specific preferential models,  
based on some {\em closure constructions}, which allow to define the preference relations $<_{A_i}$ from a knowledge base $K$, also exploiting the ranks or weights of conditional implications, when available.
Some examples of similar constructions, in the multi-preferential case, have been considered in \cite{AIJ21,NMR2020}, and for ranked and weighted defeasible description logics \cite{TPLP2020,IJAR23}.
In this paper, we consider conditional knowledge bases in which weights are associated to conditionals.

A {\em weighted conditional knowledge base}  for the distinguished propositions $A_1, \ldots, A_n$ is a pair $K=(K_s,K_d)$ where $K_s$ is a set of {\em strict implications} and $K_d$ is a set of  {\em defeasible implications}, each one associated with a weight $h$, an integer number.
More precisely, each distinguished formula $A_i$ is associated with a set of pairs $(\tip(A_i) \sqsubseteq B_{i,j}, h_{i,j})$, consisting of a defeasible implication with a weight $h_{i,j}$.
The weight  $h_{i,j}$ is a positive or negative number and is intended to represent the {\em plausibility} (or {\em implausibility}) of the associated defeasible implication, where negative weights represent penalties.
\begin{example} \label{exa:student1}
As an example of weighted conditional knowledge base, we  consider a knowledge base $K$ describing the properties of employees and students, where $\mathit{Emp}$ and $\mathit{Student}$ are distinguished formulae.
We assume $K$ contains the set of strict implications
$K_s=$ $ \{\mathit{Emp  \ri  Adult}$; \  $\mathit{Adult  \ri   has\_SSN}$; \ $\mathit{PhdStudent  \ri  Student}\}$. 
and the set $K_d$ of weighted defeasible implications $(d_1), \ldots, (d_6)$ from the introduction.


\end{example}

Given a weighted conditional knowledge base $K$, in the following we will construct a multi-preferential interpretation $\emme= \sx \WW, \{<_{A_i}\}, v \dx$ such that, the set of worlds $\WW$ is the set of all the propositional interpretations ($I \subseteq \mathit{Prop}$) satisfying all the strict implications in $K$, while we will use the defeasible part of $K$ to define the preference relations $<_{A_i}$ on $\WW$ with respect to the distinguished formulae $A_i$. 

Let us consider all the defeasible implications $(\tip(A_i) \ri B_{i,j} \; , h_{i,j})\in  K_d$, with $\tip(A_i) $ in the l.h.s. of the implication. 
The preference relation $<_{A_i}$ associated with the distinguished formula $A_i$ is determined by computing, for each propositional interpretation $I \in \WW $, the weight $W_{A_i}(I)$ of $I$ with respect to formula $A_i$, as follows:   
\begin{equation}\label{weight}
W_{A_i}(I) = \sum_{{j: I \models B_{i,j}}} h_{i,j} 
\end{equation}
where $\sum_{{j: I \models B_{i,j}}} h_{i,j}=0$, when there is no defeasible implication $(\tip(A_i) \ri B_{i,j} \; , h_{i,j})\in  K_d$.

Informally, the weight $W_{A_i}(I)$ of $I$ wrt $A_i$ is the sum of the weights of all defeasible implications for $A_i$ satisfied by $I$. The more plausible are the satisfied implications, the higher is the weight of $I$ with respect to $A_i$. 
The lowest weight, $-\infty$, is given to the propositional interpretations which do not satisfy $A_i$. If there is no defeasible implication $(\tip(A_i) \ri B_{i,j} \; , p_{i,j})$ in $K_d$ for the distinguished proposition $A_i$, we have $W_{A_i}(I)=0$.

For each distinguished formula $A_i$, a total preorder $\leq_{A_i}$ is defined on the set of the interpretations in $\WW$ as follows: for all $I_1, I_2 \in \WW$, 
\begin{align*} 
I_1 & \leq_{A_i}  I_2  \mbox{  \ \ iff \ \ }  W_{A_i}(I_1) \geq W_{A_i}(I_2)
\end{align*}

Given a weighted conditional knowledge base $K=(K_s,K_d)$, we define a multi-preferential model of $K$.
\begin{definition}
Given a weighted conditional knowledge base $K=(K_s,K_d)$, a {\em multi-preferential model of $K$} is a preferential interpretation $\emme= \sx \WW, \{<_{A_i}\}, v \dx$ such that: 

- for all the strict implications in $A \ri B \in K_s$, $\emme \models A \ri B$;

- for all distinguished formulae $A_i$, the preference relation $<_{A_i}$ is defined as follows: for all $w_1, w_2 \in \WW$, 
\begin{align}  \label{pref}
w_1 & <_{A_i}  w_2  \mbox{  \ \ iff \ \ }  W_{A_i}(v(w_1)) > W_{A_i}(v(w_2))
\end{align}
\end{definition}

A weighted knowledge base $K$ is satisfiable if $K$ has a model.
As we are considering a finite knowledge base $K$, we can take $\mathit{Prop}$ as the finite set of the propositional variables occurring in $K$ and limit our consideration to finite interpretations. When $K$ is satisfiable,  a {\em canonical model} of $K$ can be defined.
\begin{definition}
For a satisfiable weighted knowledge base $K=(K_s,K_d)$, the {\em canonical multi-preferential model  $\emme^K= \sx \WW, \{<_{A_i}\}, v \dx$ of $K$} is defined as follows:

- $\WW$ is the set of all the propositional interpretations $I \subseteq Prop$ satisfying the strict implications in $K_s$ (considering satisfiability in the PC);

- the valuation function $v$ is defined by letting: $v(I)=I$, for all $I \in \WW$;

- for each distinguished formula $A_i$, the preference relation $<_{A_i}$ is the strict partial order induced by the preorder $\leq_{A_i}$ on the propositional interpretations in $\WW$.
\end{definition}


Let us consider again Example  \ref{exa:student1}. 

\begin{example}  \label{exa:student2}
The propositional interpretations $I_1=\{student, employee, adult, has\_SSN,$ $ young, has\_classes\}$ and 
$I_2 =\{student, employee, adult, has\_SSN, has\_boss, has\_classes\}$
both satisfy the strict implications in $K_s$, and belong to the set of worlds $\WW$ of the canonical model of $K$. 
The weight of $I_1$ with respect to proposition $\mathit{student}$ is $W_\mathit{student}(I_1)= 90+80=170$,
while the weight of $I_2$ with respect to proposition $\mathit{student}$ is $W_\mathit{student}(I_2)= 0$.
Hence, $I_1 <_{\mathit{student}} I_2$.
On the other hand, $W_\mathit{employee}(I_1)=  (-50) + (-70)= -120$, and $W_\mathit{employee}(I_2)= 100+ (-70)=30$.
Hence, $I_2 <_{\mathit{employee}} I_1$.
\end{example}

Given a satisfiable weighted knowledge base $K$, and an (strict or conditional) implication $A  \rightarrow B$, 
we say that  $A  \rightarrow B$ is  {\em  entailed from the weighted knowledge base}  $K$ (written $K \models^w  A  \rightarrow B$), \ if \ $\emme^K \models A  \rightarrow B $ holds.


Restricting to preferential models over a set of propositional interpretations is a standard approach in KLM logics such as, for instance, in the definition of the semantics of the rational closure and of the lexicographic closure of a conditional knowledge base \cite{whatdoes,Lehmann95}. In both cases, a single preference relation between worlds is computed from the ranks of the worlds, and the ranks of worlds is determined by the rational closure construction.

Here, we have assumed that the weights of the conditionals are provided in the KB, and they are used to define the different preorder relations $\leq_{A_i}$.
Another option to define the preorders $\leq_{A_i}$ is to associate ranks to the conditionals in the knowledge base 
and to exploit user defined ranks, rather than computing the ranks from the rational closure construction. This option has been considered in the concept-aware multi-preferential extension of DL, where the rankes of conditionals are given \cite{TPLP2020}, and also in the framework for a modular multi-concept lexicographic closure presented in \cite{NMR2020}. The idea of having ranked knowledge bases was previously explored in the framework for qualitative preferences by Brewka \cite{Brewka89,Brewka04}, in which basic preference relations $\geq_K$  are associated with different ranked knowledge bases $K$, and new preference relations can be defined by combining the basic preference relations. In Section \ref{sec:ranked_KBs}, we will consider an example with conditionals with ranks in a CondASP program.

In the general case, an interesting option would be to allow for different ways of specifying preferences for different distinguished propositions $A_i$. For instance, for a distinguished proposition $A_i$ one could specify the ranks $r_i$ of conditionals $\tip(A_i) \ri D_{i,j}$, while for another distinguished proposition $A_j$ weights of the conditionals could be given. 
Different approaches may be viable for the definition of preferences, provided a preorders $\leq_{A_i}$ on worlds can be defined.



