\subsection{Answer Sets}\label{sec:ASP}

In this section, we shortly recap the answer set semantics \citep{Gelfond}.
For simplicity, 
rather than considering the grounding of an ASP program with variables, in the following we limit our consideration to a propositional program over a set $\mathit{Prop}$ of propositional variables.

Given a set $\mathit{Prop}$ of propositional variables,  let a {\em literal} $l$ be a propositional variable $a \in \mathit{Prop}$ 
or its {\em default negation} $not \; a$.
%
An ASP program is a set $\Pi$ of {\em rules} having the form:
\begin{equation}  \label{rule1}
 a  :- \; l_{1}, \ldots , l_m 
\end{equation}
where $a$ is a propositional variable, the {\em head} of the rule, and $l_{1}, \ldots , l_m$ is the rule {\em body}, where the $l_i$ are literals.
Constraints (rules with empty head) and choice rules, used in the example, can be suitably mapped into sets of rules of the form
(\ref{rule1}) \cite{ASP_Gebser2012}.

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%


Given a set $S \subseteq \mathit{Prop}$ of propositional variables,
the {\em satisfiability of a formula $\alpha$ in $S$}
(written $S \models \alpha$) is defined as follows:

$S \models p$ \  \emph{iff} \  $ p \in S$, for any propositional variable $p \in \mathit{Prop}$

$S \models not\; p$ \  \emph{iff} \  $S \not \models p$

\noindent
The satisfiability of rule bodies 
in $S$ is defined as:

$S \models l_1, \ldots, l_n$  \ \emph{iff} \  $S \models l_i$ for $i = 1, \ldots, n$.

\noindent
A rule $H \leftarrow Body$ is satisfied in $S$ if,  $S \models Body$
implies $S \models H$.

Let us define the notion of answer set of a program $\Pi$
that does not contain default negation.


\begin{definition}
A set $S \subseteq \mathit{Prop}$ is an {\em answer set of $\Pi$} if
$S$ is minimal (in the sense of subset inclusion) among the $S'\subseteq \mathit{Prop}$ such that $S'$ is a satisfies the rules in $\Pi$.

\end{definition}
Answer sets of a program $\Pi$ possibly containing default negation is defined by exploiting
Gelfond and Lifschitz' transform \citep{Gelfond&Lifschitz:88} 
to compute the reduct of $\Pi$ relative to $S$.
The {\em reduct, $\Pi^S$, of  $\Pi$ relative to $S$}
is the set of all the rules
$ H \leftarrow l_1, \ldots , l_m $
such that
$H \leftarrow l_1, \ldots , l_m , not\; l_{m+1} , \ldots , not\; l_n $
is in $\Pi$ and  $S \not \models  l_i$, for all $i=m+1,\ldots,n$.


\begin{definition}
A set $S \subseteq \mathit{Prop}$ is an {\em answer set of $\Pi$}  if
$S$ is an answer set of the reduct $\Pi^S$.
\end{definition}


