\begin{abstract}
Consider the following discrete evacuation model.
The evacuation terrain is modeled by a simple graph $G=(V,E)$ whose certain vertices $X\subseteq V$ are called \emph{exits}.
Initially, each vertex is either \emph{empty} or \emph{occupied} by an agent.
We assume that each vertex has a unique \emph{id} (and therefore the agents do have unique ids), each agent has finite but arbitrarily large memory, and the graph is initially stored in the memory of each agent.
In other words, the agents do know the topology of the network along with the locations of the exits, but they do not know the initial positions nor the quantity of other agents.
The time is divided into \emph{steps}; in each step any pair of agents present at vertices at a distance of at most two can exchange an arbitrary number of messages, and then each agent can either make a move or stay put.
The agents should make moves in a collision-free manner, i.e., no two agents can be located at the same vertex in the same step.
At the end of each step, any agent located at an exit \emph{evacuates}, i.e., it is removed from the graph.
The goal is to provide an algorithm to the agents (referred to as an evacuation strategy) that ensures the evacuation of all agents and minimizes the number of steps.

This work provides an algorithmic framework that allows constructing valid evacuation strategies for arbitrary input graphs.
Specifically, we focus on the properties of the input graphs that lead to evacuation strategies with constant competitive ratios.
In particular, we describe an application of the above framework that gives an asymptotically optimal evacuation for grids (and by extension hexagonal or triangular grids as well).

\end{abstract}

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{Keywords}: discrete evacuation, mobile agents, distributed algorithm, graph coloring.

% ==============================================================================
% ================================================================
\section{Introduction} \label{sec:intro}
A substantial amount of work has been devoted to continuous evacuation models, by which we mean `flow-like' modeling of the traffic, often combined with geometric modeling of the evacuation area.
%
However, much less is known about discrete counterparts of the  evacuation problems, which we address in this work.
Our approach is to use a well-established mobile agent computing terminology, used mainly in the context of classical distributed tasks like graph exploration, rendezvous and gathering, or map construction---see the considered model description in Section~\ref{sec:model}.
%
Our motivation to address the problem of discrete evacuation as a distributed one is twofold---as we explain in the following section, on the one hand, we are interested in its purely theoretical aspects; but on the other hand, we also refer to the practical applications of our considerations.

% ----------------------------------------------
\subsection{Motivation}

%First, 
From a theoretical point of view, it is known that the centralized (offline) version of our problem can be solved efficiently via network flows \cite{BorDasDerKusz25}.
Hence, this study aims to investigate the extent to which the absence of information impacts the quality of a solution.
We take a qualitative approach regarding the input sensitivity, where we compare the distributed (online) solutions to the problem with centralized (offline) solutions in cases when the former has no information on the quantity and location of other agents.
For a motivation of the choice of this parameter, consider the following.
If the agents have no a priori information on the graph, then the evacuation problem generalizes well-known distributed tasks like treasure hunt (see, e.g. Bouchard et al. \cite{BouchardDLP21}) or graph exploration (see, e.g. Kolendarska et al. \cite{KolenderskaKMZ09}.
These are well-studied and the connection suggests that in such case efficient distributed evacuation algorithms (e.g., those with constant competitive ratios in comparison to the best offline solutions) do not exist.
Similar reasoning holds for the case when agents know the graph but do not know the locations of the exits.

Our second motivation is more practical, i.e., it is difficult to expect that in an emergency scenario a centralized evacuation plan will be developed that takes into account specific locations of agents.
%Efficient evacuation has a critical impact during emergencies, including floods, fires, and earthquakes.  
%For example, in the case of floods, even advanced satellite weather forecasting provides people with limited time to prepare and evacuate from low-lying areas to safe ground. 
The traditional predefined evacuation plans frequently rely on statistical data, and do not take into account the current situation.
This may make them inefficient in practice because the socio-economic development has led to more densely populated and complex multi-level structures like high-rise buildings and pavement systems, or sports arenas, making evacuation scenarios more complicated and harder to predict (see, e.g., Hai et al. \cite{HHZR24}). 
%We acknowledge that another level of flexibility of evacuation strategies would be to consider dynamic networks, which would reflect, e.g., blocked evacuation passages. This, however, is left for future works.
%
%Imagine a fire evacuation from the complex building according to a static plan when the evacuation path leading to an exit is blocked. 
%Without additional assistance, those who escape from a fire may not find another existing clear path.
%
%In other scenarios, where a swarm of mobile robots is required to gather in selected places (called exits in the context of evacuation terminology), time is also usually of the essence.
%
Whether we consider human beings or autonomous robots, in both cases the advanced computing abilities of handheld devices (such as smartphones) used by evacuees, internal processors of their vehicles, or robot control units might be used to calculate an evacuation strategy {'on the fly'}---just at the time when the evacuation process takes place (see, e.g. Avil{\'{e}}s del Moral et al. \cite{MoralTK14}).
Certainly, technological advancements will allow for wider practical applications of distributed algorithms in the future.

%Centralized computation of the evacuation strategy has an important drawback as the security of the system relies strongly on the central computing unit and the communication between the central unit and agents. Hence, for safety reasons, a distributed approach could be a better or even the only possible solution.

%On the other hand, the distributed setting must not rely on the centralized data and typically the current state of the system is not available to the agents. Beside blocked or clear corridors also the positions of other agents may influence the quality of a solution, e.g., by creating bottlenecks when too many agents try to go to the same exit or using the same path.

% ----------------------------------------------
\subsection{Related work} \label{sec:related-work}

%{Terrain ....}
A theme that seems to stand out in the literature devoted to evacuation problems is that of terrain evacuation models, that in general can be classified as continuous (geometric) or discrete---see, e.g., a broad, comprehensive review on terrain modeling and agent behavior by Hai et~al.~\cite{HHZR24}. The first approach, in the context of distributed algorithms for collaborative agents, due to Chrobak et~al.~\cite{CGGM15}, considered the evacuation from a line. This has been later generalized from a single line to multiple 'rays' (see Brandt et al.~\cite{BrandtFRW20}). A continuous space in the form of a disc has also been studied by many authors---see, e.g. \cite{BrandtLLSW17, CGGKMP14, CGKNOV15, DisserS19, GeorgiouLLK23, PattanayakR0S18}.  
%
The relationship of terrain modeling and the problem analyzed in this paper results, e.g., from the possibility of discretizing the Euclidean space that leads to a partition into small polygons, allowing its further modeling using various types of lattice and grid graphs.
%
Although we focus on the classical static graphs, it is worth pointing out that some attention has already been paid to modeling terrain changes using the concept of temporal graphs, see, e.g. Akrida et~al. \cite{AkridaCGKS19}, and Erlebach et~al.~\cite{ErlebachS23}.

%\textbf{Evacuation and searching.}
The main goal of evacuation is usually to minimize the time when the last agent reaches an exit. In contrast, the collaborative search problem (see, e.g. Alspach et~al.~\cite{Alspach04}) focuses on searching for an exit by the first robot as fast as possible. Also, an important difference between the two is that in searching, the number of robots is a parameter to be optimized, while in evacuation this constitutes a part of the input.
For a survey on other possible goals, like rescuing as many agents as possible in a given time, see the PhD thesis by Kappmeier~\cite{Kappmeier15}.

Despite the problems mentioned above may differ significantly, it is possible to identify many common and even universal ideas and algorithmic methods; for example, the authors in~\cite{AltshulerYWB11, BhadauriaKIS12, KaraivanovMSV14, Markov_2016} discuss terrain modeling using graphs, while~\cite{DereniowskiKO21} addresses searching using heterogeneous agents.
We note that using grids for discretizing search in two-dimensional geometric shapes appeared in~\cite{DereniowskiO19}.
A similar approach can be used for our problem for modeling, however we leave as an open problem whether so called partial grids (see~\cite{DereniowskiO19}) also adopt constant competitive evacuation strategies.

The discrete model for distributed evacuation we consider in this paper was introduced in~\cite{BorDasDerKusz25}, where the authors proved that there does not exist any $(2-\Theta(1/k))$-competitive distributed algorithm for evacuation of $k$ agents from a tree, and they give a constant-competitive distributed evacuation algorithm from such graphs.


% --------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{The distributed Evacuation Model}
\label{sec:model}

Following the model assumed in~\cite{BorDasDerKusz25}, where we are given a finite, simple, undirected graph $G=(V,E)$ with the node set $V$, the edge set $E$, and the two distinguished nonempty node subsets $H$ and $X$ called \emph{homebases} and \emph{exits}, respectively.
Initially, each of the $k$ agents resides in one of the homebases in $H$ such that no two agents occupy the same homebase (i.e., $|H|=k$ and $H\cap X=\emptyset$).
Also, each agent a priori knows the graph and the location of all exit nodes, by which we understand that each agent has an isomorphic copy of $G$ in its memory, together with the set $X$.
However, an important assumption is that no agent knows $k$ or $H$.

The model is synchronous, i.e., the time taken by the evacuation process is divided into \emph{steps} of unit duration. 
In each step, each agent: first communicates with the other agents, then performs local computations, and finally decides on its action/movement.

%--------------------------------------------------
\paragraph*{The communication within a single step}

In the model, each agent can directly communicate with the other agent when they are located at the nodes that are neighbors or the nodes that have a common neighbor.
We refer to such communication as \emph{direct}.
The number of messages two agents may exchange at the beginning of a step is finite, but we do not assume any specific bound.
The consequence of this assumption is the following.
Suppose that there are two agents located at nodes $u$ and $v$ at a distance greater than $2$ and there is a $uv$-path $P$ such that for each vertex $x$ of $P$, $x$ is occupied by an agent or both neighbors of $x$ in $P$ are occupied by some agents.
Then, through multiple message exchanges (consider gossiping along $P$) the agents at $u$ and $v$ may communicate at the beginning of the step.
We refer to such communication as \emph{indirect}.

% ---------------------------------------------------------
\paragraph*{The computation and movement within a single step}
%
%Once an agent decides that it does not need to perform any other message exchanges, it performs a local computation, i.e., it executes its algorithm that, as an input, receives the current state of the agent, i.e., agent's memory content and its actual location.

Once an agent decides that it does not need to exchange any further messages, it performs a local computation, i.e., it executes its algorithm that as an input receives the \emph{current state of the agent} that is the contents of the agent's memory and its current location.
%
Such local computation results in determining the \emph{action} of the agent performed in the given step, which is one of the following:

\begin{enumerate}[label={\normalfont{(\Alph*)}},leftmargin=*]
 \item\label{action:1} the agent \emph{moves}, i.e., changes its location from the currently occupied node $v$ to one of its neighbors $u$, or
 \item\label{action:2} the agent \emph{stays still}, which means that it remains at the currently occupied node $v$.
\end{enumerate}

We assume that all moves occur simultaneously and it is not allowed that two agents are present at the same node once the moves occur.
More precisely, in any step, \eqref{action:1} is feasible if no other agent decides to move to $u$, and if $u$ is occupied at the beginning of the considered step, then the occupying agent needs to move from $u$ to another vertex $u'$ in the same step.
(We note that we allow $u'=v$, which results in a \emph{swap} of the agents).
Action \eqref{action:2} is feasible if no other agent decides to move to $v$ in the considered step.
An agent that occupies an exit node at the end of a step \emph{evacuates}, i.e., it is automatically removed from the graph.
As a consequence of our model, no two agents may evacuate through the same exit in a single step.

% -----------------------------------------------
\subsection{The problem statement}

The node occupied by an agent $i$ at the end of a step $s$ is denoted by $\loc[s]{i}$ while for the initial position (the homebase) of the agent we write $\loc[0]{i}$. Naturally, the values of $\loc{i}$ in subsequent steps reflect the actions performed by the agent $i$ during the evacuation process and uniquely determine its route.
%
A tuple $(\loc{1},\ldots,\loc{k})$ of such 'agent positioning' functions is called an \emph{evacuation strategy} if for each agent $i\in\{1,\ldots,k\}$ there is a step $s_i$ such that $\loc[s_i]{i}\in X$ and for each pair of distinct agents $i,j$ in each step $s\in\{1,\ldots,\min\{s_i,s_j\}\}$ it holds 
%
         $\loc[s]{i}\neq\loc[s]{j}$.
%
The \emph{length} of an evacuation strategy $(\loc{1},\ldots,\loc{k})$ is defined as $\max\{s_1,\ldots,s_k\}$.

\medskip
Our discrete evacuation problem $\problemEvac$ is defined as follows:

\medskip\noindent
\textsc{Discrete Evacuation  ($\problemEvac$)}\\
\begin{tabular}{rp{0.75\textwidth}}
   \emph{Input:} & A graph $G$, an integer $\ell$, a set $X$ of exits, a set $H$ of homebases occupied by $k$ agents. \\
   \emph{Question:} & Does there exist a \emph{distributed} algorithm providing an evacuation strategy $(\loc{1},\ldots,\loc{k})$ of length at most $\ell$?    \\
\end{tabular}

\medskip
We denote by $\OPT$ the minimum length of an evacuation strategy for a given input.

\medskip
Since we consider $\problemEvac$ in a distributed setting, we assume that the nodes of the graph have unique identifiers, and the agents have unique identifiers.
At the beginning of each step, each agent can access the identifier of a node $v$ it occupies, and for each edge $e$ incident to $v$ the agent is given the identifier of the other node of $e$.
Thanks to this assumption, the agent can correctly perform a move in a given step.
Formally, by a \emph{distributed algorithm} we understand a sequential algorithm executed independently by each agent that (provided the input described earlier) determines the actions of the agents in each step.


% ----------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Our contribution and paper outline} \label{sec:contribution}

The main part of this work is Section~\ref{sec:framework} where we develop our distributed discrete evacuation strategies framework.
The central tools in the framework are $\B$-partitions and the zone graph (cf. Section~\ref{sec:framework:partitions}).
Regardless of how these two objects are computed for the input instance, the framework allows turning them into a valid evacuation strategy.
A formal statement of the formula that gives the evacuation time achieved by the resulting strategies is given by Theorem~\ref{thm:EvacuationTime} at the end of Section~\ref{sec:framework:outline}, where we also present an intuitive outline of the framework.
Next, Section~\ref{sec:grids} provides an application of the framework to prove the following result concerning the evacuation in grids.
%
\begin{theorem} \label{thm:grids}
There exists a $\bigo(1)$-competitive distributed evacuation strategy for each grid.
\end{theorem}

We point out that also other topologies are used to model two-dimensional space. For example, a triangular lattice or hexagonal grids are commonly used in the area of programmable matter, see, e.g. \cite{Chalopin0K24,DaymudeGHKSR20,DaymundeGHKKRRCS18,HinnenthalLS24,LunaFSVY20,NavarraPBT23}. We note that our approach for grids can be analogously applied to those as well to get a constant-competitive algorithm for evacuation.

% =====================================================================
\section{Preliminaries and notation} \label{sec:preliminaries}

For an agent $a$, $N[a]$ (respectively $E[a]$) denotes the set consisting of the vertex $v$ occupied by $a$ and all neighbors of $v$ in $G$ (the set of all edges incident to the vertex occupied by $a$, respectively).
A set of agents $A$ is called a \emph{group} at a given step if the subgraph induced by $\bigcup_{a\in A}N[a]$ is connected.
%
For any $U\subseteq V$, $G[U]=(U,\{\{u,v\}\colon u,v\in U\})$ is the subgraph of $G=(V,E)$ \emph{induced} by $U$.
The following is a direct consequence of our computational model.
\begin{proposition} \label{prop:group-communication}
If a set of agents $A$ forms a group at the beginning of a step, then any two agents in $A$ can communicate and therefore exchange any number of messages in this step.
\qed
\end{proposition}

Using groups will be one of the basic algorithmic tools since one can treat a group of agents as a `centralized' computational unit.
More precisely, at the beginning of each step, all members of a group can send all data they possess (i.e., their memory content) to a single agent $a$ in the group.
Next, agent $a$ may perform a centralized computation that takes into account the 'knowledge' of the entire group.
The result of such a calculation can be a decision regarding the actions in the current step or a series of actions for several subsequent steps for all agents in the group.
After the calculation, $a$ sends the result to all agents (directly to its neighbors or indirectly to other agents in the group).
The above acquisition of data and sending out the results are typical distributed tasks, a \emph{convergecast} to $a$ and then a \emph{broadcast} from $a$.

The problem $\problemEvac$ admits the following monotonicity property.
\begin{proposition} \label{prop:monotonicity}
If $(G,\ell,X,H)$ is a $\YES$-instance of the $\problemEvac$ problem, then $(G,\ell,X,H')$, where $H'\subseteq H$, is also a $\YES$-instance of $\problemEvac$.
\qed
\end{proposition}

We use the following distance measure: $\dist{u}{v}$ for two vertices $u$ and $v$ is the length of a~shortest $uv$-path in $G$ that does not contain an exit.

\medskip
It does happen in our algorithm (cf. Algorithm \ref{alg:SinglePhase}) that an agent $a$ located at a node $v$ needs to move along a path with subsequent vertices $v_0=v,\ldots,v_p$ and the internal nodes $v_1,\ldots,v_{p-1}$ are occupied by agents.
If the latter agents will stay idle for the next $p$ steps, then the movement of $a$ from $v_0$ to $v_p$ will be implemented as follows.
For the first $p-1$ steps $a$ stays idle and in the following $p$-th step for each $i\in\{0,\ldots,p-1\}$ the agent at $v_i$ moves to $v_{i+1}$.
In addition, the agent that was at $v_i$ prior to the moves, $i\in\{1,\ldots,p-1\}$ transfers its state (memory and id) to the agent at $v_i$ after the moves.
Moreover, the agent at $v_p$ receives the state of $a$.
Note that state transfers are done via message exchanges.
Informally speaking, this approach simulates the situation as if $a$ moved from $v_0$ to $v_p$ within $p$ steps, while the other agents remained idle.
Whenever this occurs we say that $a$ \emph{skips} the agents located at $v_1,\ldots,v_{p-1}$.

% =====================================================================
\section{The evacuation framework} \label{sec:framework}

In this section, we give a distributed algorithm that performs an evacuation in an arbitrary graph.
%
The algorithm strongly relies on the concepts of $\B$-partitions and zones (cf. Section~\ref{sec:framework:partitions}), according to which several different partitions may be obtained for the same graph.
%
Besides $\B$-partitions and zones, an important concept is also that of a zone graph that results from a particular $\B$-partition and allows determining the phases of the algorithm.
%
As we will see in Theorem~\ref{thm:EvacuationTime}, the partitions we manage to compute may significantly impact the performance of the algorithm (the length of evacuation).
In this context, it is also worth mentioning that every graph admits a trivial $\B$-partition (e.g., we can assume that each $V_i$ is a singleton) that easily provides a successful but not necessarily efficient strategy.
Therefore, in order to properly use our framework, we need to provide $\B$-partitions and zones that ensure fast or even optimal evacuation, which we demonstrate for grids in Section~\ref{sec:grids}.


% --------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Partitions and zone graphs} \label{sec:framework:partitions}

In our algorithms, we will work with an integer $\B$ that is used to split a strategy into phases (cf. Section~\ref{sec:framework:phase}).
One may view $\B$ as a time limit that bounds the duration of particular phases of the algorithm.
We will prove that if $\B$ is large enough (this will simply be $\B\geq\OPT$), the evacuation strategy succeeds in the particular phase.

% ------------------------------------------
\paragraph*{Graph $\B$-partitions and zones}

We say that $(V_1,\ldots,V_l)$ is a \emph{$\B$-partition} of a graph $G=(V,E)$, where $\B$ is a nonnegative integer, if the following conditions are satisfied:

\begin{enumerate}[label={\normalfont{(\roman*)}},leftmargin=*]
 \item\label{it:partition1} $V_i\cap V_j=\emptyset$ for each $1\leq i<j\leq l$ and $V_1\cup\cdots\cup V_l=V$,
 \item\label{it:partition2} for each $i\in\{1,\ldots,l\}$, there exists a spanning tree of $G[V_i\setminus X]$ with the root $v_i\in V_i$ such that for each $v\in V_i$ the distance between $v_i$ and $v$ along the spanning tree is at most $\B$.
\end{enumerate}

Each set $V_i$ with $i\in\{1,\ldots,l\}$ (and with a slight abuse of notation, also the subgraph induced by $V_i$) is called a \emph{zone} and the vertex $v_i$ is its \emph{center}.

\medskip
Intuitively, the role of the spanning trees in Condition~\eqref{it:partition2} is to ensure that each agent occupying a vertex $u$ of $V_i$ can determine $v_i$ and compute a path connecting $u$ and $v_i$, and traverse this path in at most $\B$ steps.
The fact that the paths are taken along the spanning tree, as opposed to taking arbitrary paths, finds its applications, e.g., in the proof of Lemma~\ref{lem:first-grouping}, where we bound the number of steps required to form a group by the agents located in particular zones.
At this point, we emphasize that in general an agent in our algorithm will not necessarily traverse the entire path due to the presence of other agents, but we will prove that thanks to the spanning trees, all agents located in $V_i$ are able to form a group in $\B$ steps (cf. Lemma~\ref{lem:first-grouping}).

In what follows, whenever we discuss the properties of a zone $Z$ without explicitly pointing out the corresponding $\B$-partition, it should be understood that they hold regardless of the $\B$-partition to which $Z$ belongs.
In fact, we will only need to ensure that $Z$ satisfies Condition~\eqref{it:partition2}, when we consider $Z$ out of the context of other zones.
Also, note that for each $Z$ that satisfies this condition, there exists a $\B$-partition whose $Z$ is a part.

\medskip
Let $\B$ be an integer and let $(V_1,\ldots,V_l)$ be a $\B$-partition of a graph $G$.
We say that two zones $Z$ and $Z'$ are \emph{close} if $Z$ has a vertex $z$ and $Z'$ has a vertex $z'$ such that
\begin{equation}\label{eq:dist}
   \dist{z}{z'}\leq 2\B.
\end{equation}

As we will see, in the considered framework, the role of simple yet very useful concepts of internal and exclusive strategies cannot be overestimated. 
%
Both notions pertain to the set of agents initially located in a zone $Z$ (with their homebases in $Z$) that we denote by $\agents(Z)$.

An evacuation strategy is called \emph{internal} for agents in $\agents(Z)$ if during the execution of the strategy each agent in $\agents(Z)$ remains on the vertices of $Z$. 
Additionally, we say that a zone $Z$ is \emph{self-sufficient with respect to $\B$} (we omit $\B$ if clear from the context) if there exists an internal evacuation strategy that can be computed by the agents and completed in $\B$ steps.

An evacuation strategy is called \emph{exclusive for} $\agents(Z)$ if it is defined for the following input instance: the entire graph $G$, only the agents in $\agents(Z)$, but with all exits $X$ available exclusively for the evacuation of agents in $\agents(Z)$.
(In other words, the instance we obtain from the original one by removing all agents except those initially in $Z$.)
Because an exclusive strategy is defined for a subset of agents, we immediately get from Proposition~\ref{prop:monotonicity} the following.

\begin{lemma} \label{lem:eclusive-strategy}
  For any zone $Z$, there exists an exclusive strategy for $\agents(Z)$ that completes in at most $\OPT$ steps.
\qed
\end{lemma}

% -------------------------------------------------------
\paragraph*{Zone graphs}

The next element comprising our framework is a zone graph. 
%
Suppose we are given a nonnegative integer $\B$, and let $(V_1,\ldots,V_l)$ be a $\B$-partition of a given graph $G=(V,E)$. The graph $\ZG=(\ZGV,\ZGE)$ with $\ZGV=\{V_1,\ldots,V_l\}$ and $\{Z,Z'\}\in\ZGE$ is a \emph{zone graph} if and only if the zones $Z$ and $Z'$ are close and none of them is self-sufficient.

We point out that in the above definition, the latter condition implies that each self-sufficient zone is an isolated vertex in the zone graph $\ZG$. Since in our analysis, the chromatic number of $\ZG$ will multiplicatively contribute to the evacuation time, modeling self-sufficient zones as an independent set in the corresponding zone graph $\ZG$ ensures that they do not lengthen the evacuation strategies. Consequently, in what follows we say that the zones $Z_1,\ldots,Z_p$ are \emph{independent} if $\{Z_i,Z_j\}\notin\ZGE$ whenever $1\leq i<j\leq p$. For a zone $Z\in\ZGV$, we will also use
%
\[N_{\ZG}(Z) = \{Z'\in\ZGV \colon \{Z,Z'\}\in\ZGE \}\]
%
to denote the set of its neighbors in $\ZG$.

\medskip
The intuition behind the above definitions is as follows.
Regardless of what the zone graph looks like, the algorithm based on its structure will be correct, but the number of evacuation steps may not be satisfactory.
For this reason, we treat self-sufficient zones independently from the other ones.
This prevents unnecessary increases in the density of the zone graph, which in turn could result in an increase in its chromatic number. 
Also, the self-sufficient zones, according to the definition above, may in general have internal evacuation strategies that are `difficult' to compute in a distributed way.
Despite this, we will aim at selecting these zones in such a way that they will be the `easy' ones to work with. 
Hence, in particular, the assumption on the computability of internal strategies in Lemma~\ref{lem:SinglePhase:S}.


% --------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{The framework outline} \label{sec:framework:outline}

An evacuation strategy $\cS$ is \emph{successful} if all agents for which $\cS$ is defined evacuate during the execution of $\cS$.

We will split the entire strategy into epochs and phases.
Each epoch contains many phases.
An epoch aims at computing a successful evacuation strategy for all agents, and for this reason, it picks a value of $\B$ to be a bound on $\OPT$.
We use binary search for the appropriate value of $\B$, by taking $\B=2^j$ for the $j$-th epoch.
If $\B$ is too small, i.e., $\B<OPT$, then the work done to evacuate the agents in such an epoch will fail, i.e., not all agents will evacuate.
The agents can detect such a failure and hence every agent that did not evacuate within the assumed time limit starts over from its homebase in the next epoch.
We remark that backtracking to the starting position (which is a mechanism run after each phase) may not be necessary, but significantly simplifies the analysis and adds only a constant factor to the final competitive ratio.
The same holds for the binary progressive search, i.e., doubling gives a constant factor as well.
Since such a general framework will not, by construction, match the known lower bounds (see, e.g., Theorem 4.1 in \cite{BorDasDerKusz25}) we do not attempt to optimize the constant in the competitive ratio, thus getting the benefit of simplification of certain parts of the strategy.

The computation of the zone graph $\ZG$ can be done without knowing the locations of other agents and therefore each agent does that in the first step at the beginning of each epoch.
(Recall that the definition of the zone graph depends on $\B$ and thus the zone graph is different in each epoch).
Next, all agents find the same $d$-\emph{coloring} of $\ZG=(\ZGV,\ZGE)$ (for brevity, a \emph{coloring} of $\ZG$), by which we mean a function $c\colon\ZGV\rightarrow\{1,\ldots,d\}$ such that for each edge $\{Z,Z'\}$ of $\ZG$ it holds $c(Z)\neq c(Z')$. 
Consequently, since in our algorithm each epoch will have $d$ phases, at the beginning of each epoch an agent knowing $\B$ is able to determine exactly how many phases there will be in the epoch. 

\medskip
In each phase $i\in\{1,\ldots,d\}$ we attempt to evacuate agents in zones colored $i$, leaving other agents idle. The zones having color $i$ can be treated in the following two different ways: 

\begin{enumerate}[label={\normalfont{(\alph*)}},leftmargin=*]
\item The agents in self-sufficient zones compute the internal evacuation strategies and execute them. In our framework, we do not specify how to determine internal strategies (it may greatly depend on the structure of the input graph and on the locations of the exits), but we assume that for each self-sufficient zone such a strategy exists (see the definition of self-sufficient zone) and will be provided in any application of the framework (which we demonstrate for grids in Section \ref{sec:grids}).
\item The other zones also compute their strategies in a distributed way, but we have two differences here.
%
First, the framework says how to do it.
We emphasize that these zones are the `difficult' ones to process and constitute the central difficulty in our computation and hence the main contribution of the framework lies in the computation of such strategies.
In other words, the framework computes an evacuation strategy for such a zone in a distributed way regardless of the structure of the underlying input graph.
The second difference is that the computed strategies evacuate the agents using the exits in the zone, but also outside the zone.
\end{enumerate}

After several epochs that fail, there finally comes an epoch in which the algorithm finds $\B$ such that $\B\geq\OPT$ holds.
As we prove later, this ensures that a successful evacuation strategy will be computed in this epoch. Consequently, from now on our routine working assumption will be $\B\geq\OPT$.

\medskip
The main result of this section is Theorem~\ref{thm:EvacuationTime} with the proof postponed to Section~\ref{sec:prf} just after the detailed presentation of the whole framework.

\begin{theorem} \label{thm:EvacuationTime}
If $d_j$ is the number of colors used for the zone graph  computed in the $j$-th epoch, then all agents evacuate in $s$ steps, where
\[s=6\sum_{j=1}^{p}d_j2^j\]
and $p=\lceil\log_2\OPT\rceil$.
\end{theorem}

