\subsection{A single phase} \label{sec:framework:phase}

We now move towards formulating an algorithm (cf. Algorithm \ref{alg:SinglePhase}) that will handle the evacuation of agents in a collection of zones $\cZ$ given as input.

It is important to note that the zones in $\cZ$ do not give a $\B$-partition; they form a subset of the zones of a $\B$-partition such that, intuitively speaking, the evacuation of all agents in $\cZ$ can be scheduled in parallel,
%\remr{The selection of the zones in $\cZ$ is such that, intuitively speaking, the algorithm will schedule the evacuation of all agents in  all zones in $\cZ$ in parallel,} 
and the attempt for each $Z\in\cZ$ will be to evacuate all agents $\agents(Z)$ in at most $3\B$ steps (the constant comes from the fact that the strategy is divided into two parts, each of duration $\Theta(\B)$).
%
The method of selecting the zones to be included in $\cZ$ will be described later (we just note here that this is done by Algorithm \ref{alg:OneAttempt} that calls Algorithm \ref{alg:SinglePhase} with $\cZ$ as part of the input).

Recall that our working assumption is that $\B\geq\OPT$. This, however, may not be met in general: we simply assume it holds in this section (see the appropriate assumptions in the lemmas below) to deal later with the opposite case.
%
Additionally, let us assume that the zones in $\cZ$ at the input are independent (again, this will be ensured by Algorithm \ref{alg:OneAttempt}).

Formally, the $3\B$ steps performed by the agents as a result of a single call to Algorithm \ref{alg:SinglePhase} are called a \emph{phase}.

We say that any strategy defined for an arbitrary subset of agents \emph{groups} these agents if at the end of the execution of the strategy the agents form a group. 

We precede the statement of Algorithm \ref{alg:SinglePhase} with a few intuitions.
The algorithm treats self-sufficient zones differently than the other ones.
For a self-sufficient zone, an internal evacuation strategy is executed in steps $t,\ldots,t+\B-1$, where $t$ is the step that occurs at the moment when Algorithm \ref{alg:SinglePhase} is called.
For a zone $Z$ that is not self-sufficient, the same steps are used for grouping the agents (see line~\ref{ln:phase:grouping}).
This will indeed result in a group if $\B$ is large enough as we assumed (cf. Lemma~\ref{lem:first-grouping}).
For the strategy computed in line~\ref{ln:phase:exclusive-strategy} we assume that agents start from their current locations (the locations at step $t+\B-1$) as opposed to their initial homebases.
The strategy $\cS$ computed in line~\ref{ln:phase:exclusive-strategy} may use an exit $x\notin Z$ for some agents in $\agents(Z)$.
However, we will argue that such an exit $x$ is never used by agents coming from two different zones in $\cZ$. This observation is crucial because it ensures that the strategy $\cS$ computed for $\agents(Z)$ will not be `slowed down' by moving agents from other zones in $\cZ$ (determined in different iterations of the `foreach' loop but executed in parallel with the strategy $\cS$).
We also take into account that on their way, while executing $\cS$, the agents in $\agents(Z)$ may encounter some idle agents from the zones that are the neighbors of $Z$ in $\ZG$, or some self-sufficient zones. In such a situation, however, the time needed to execute $\cS$ will not be affected since any moving agent from $\agents(Z)$ will simply skip the idle agents (cf. Section \ref{sec:preliminaries}).
%
%We will argue, however, that this does not affect the time needed to execute $\cS$. \remr{In particular, a moving agent from $\agents(Z)$ will skip the idle agents whenever this situation happens.}

\begin{figure}[htb]
\begin{center}
\begin{minipage}{.95\linewidth}
\begin{algorithm}[H]
\SetAlgoRefName{{\upshape\scshape SinglePhase}}
   \caption{(Input: an integer $\B$ and a set of zones $\cZ$)}
   \label{alg:SinglePhase}
      Let $t$ be the step to be executed \label{ln:phase:start}\;
      \ForEach{$Z\in\cZ$}{
         \If{$Z$ is self-sufficient}{
            Compute an internal strategy $\cS$ for $\agents(Z)$ \label{ln:phase:internal-strategy}\;
            Execute $\cS$ in steps $t,\ldots,t+\B-1$
            \label{ln:phase:execute-internal-strategy}
         }\Else{
            Group the agents $\agents(Z)$ in steps $t,\ldots,t+\B-1$\label{ln:phase:grouping}\;
            Compute an exclusive strategy $\cS$ for $\agents(Z)$ assuming the current positions as homebases \label{ln:phase:exclusive-strategy}\;
            Execute $\cS$ in steps $t+\B,\ldots,t+3\B-1$ \label{ln:phase:execute-exclusive-strategies}
      }
   }
\end{algorithm}
\end{minipage}
\end{center}
\end{figure}

\begin{lemma} \label{lem:first-grouping}
Consider a zone $Z$ of a $\B$-partition of $G$.
If $Z$ is not self-sufficient, then there exists an algorithm that groups the agents in $\agents(Z)$ in at most $\B$ steps.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Condition~\eqref{it:partition2} in the definition of $\B$-partition distinguishes a vertex $v\in Z$ (the root vertex) such that any other vertex $u$ of $Z$ is connected to $v$ by a path $P_{uv}$ of length at most $\B$.
Consider a grouping strategy that makes the agent $a$ that occupies $u$ 
%(if any)
follow a path $P_{uv}$ from $u$ to $v$ (recall that we consider the paths along a fixed spanning tree).
The moves of the agent are defined such that if $a$ is able to get closer to $v$ (moving along $P_{uv}$), then the move is done; otherwise $a$ stays idle in a given step.
%
In this context, observe that Condition~\eqref{it:partition2} implies that whenever the paths of agents starting at distinct vertices $u$ and $u'$ have a common vertex $v'$, then they have to share a subpath $P_{v'v}$ connecting $v'$ with $v$.
%
Hence, whenever $a$ needs to stay idle in a given step, then either all vertices of the path $P_{uv}$ are occupied by agents
or there is some agent occupying a node of $P_{uv}$ that gives a way to an agent $a'$ on the other path that shares with $P_{u,v}$ a subpath with the end in $v$. Without loss of generality, we may assume that this is the path $P_{u'v}$. So the number of \emph{unoccupied nodes} on $P_{v'v}$ cannot increase (recall that the endnode $v$ is the root) and since the length of $P_{v'v}$ is at most $\B$, a simple inductive argument shows that $\B$ steps are enough to form a group consisting of all agents in $\agents(Z)$.
\end{proof}

We prove in Lemma~\ref{lem:SinglePhase:S} that $2\B$ steps are enough to evacuate the agents from a zone that is not self-sufficient, assuming that $\B\geq\OPT$.
Recall that for such zones, in general, some agents need to evacuate through exits that do not belong to the zone.
For this, we need one more definition.
A zone $Z'$ is \emph{idle} in a phase if whenever Algorithm \ref{alg:SinglePhase} is called with input $\cZ$, then $Z'\notin\cZ$.

\begin{lemma} \label{lem:SinglePhase:S}
Let $\B$ and $\cZ$ be an input to Algorithm \ref{alg:SinglePhase}.
Suppose that $\B\geq\OPT$, the zones in $\cZ$ are independent, all zones not in $\cZ$ are idle in this phase and each self-sufficient zone $Z$ in $\cZ$ admits an internal evacuation strategy of length at most $\B$ that can be computed by $\agents(Z)$. Then, the strategy executed by the agents in $\agents(Z)$ is successful for each $Z\in\cZ$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Consider any two zones $Z$ and $Z'$ in $\cZ$. If both zones are self-sufficient, then the strategies defined for them in line~\ref{ln:phase:internal-strategy} are internal and hence both may be correctly executed in steps $t,\ldots,t+\B-1$.
(The fact that such a strategy exists for each self-sufficient zone and can be computed independently by the agents initially located in the zone follows from the assumption of the lemma.)

If neither $Z$ nor $Z'$ is self-sufficient, then because of the independency of the zones in $\cZ$, we infer that $Z$ and $Z'$ are not close (recall that by \eqref{eq:dist} this implies $\dist{z}{z'}>2\B$ for each $z\in Z$ and $z'\in Z'$).
This means, by the definition of the zone graph and $\B\geq\OPT$, that in every optimal strategy the paths traversed by the agents $a$ and $a'$ such that $a\in\agents(Z)$, $a'\in\agents(Z')$ do not share any vertex; in particular they end with different exits.
%
Take any optimal evacuation strategy $\tilde{\cS}$ and consider the two exclusive strategies $\cS$ and $\cS'$ obtained by restricting $\tilde{\cS}$ to the agents in $\agents(Z)$ and $\agents(Z')$, respectively.
Since in the strategy $\tilde{\cS}$ no agent in $\agents(Z)$ communicates with an agent in $\agents(Z')$, the strategies $\cS$ and $\cS'$ `inherit' this property.
This implies that the strategies for $\agents(Z)$ and $\agents(Z')$ computed in line~\ref{ln:phase:exclusive-strategy} in the corresponding iterations of the main loop of Algorithm \ref{alg:SinglePhase} `inherit' this property.
Thus, the assumption $\B\geq\OPT$ implies that the strategies executed in line~\ref{ln:phase:execute-exclusive-strategies} are successful, if there exists a successful exclusive strategy of length at most $2\B$ for the agents in $\agents(Z)$.
(The time limit of $2\B$ comes from line~\ref{ln:phase:execute-exclusive-strategies} of Algorithm \ref{alg:SinglePhase}).
The required strategy does exist because there is a successful strategy of length at most $2\OPT$ that does the following: first let all agents in $\agents(Z)$ backtrack their moves so that they arrive at their homebases, and second, execute a successful exclusive strategy of length $\OPT$ from the initial homebases.
The first part can be done in $\B$ steps by definition, and the second part is valid due to Proposition~\ref{prop:monotonicity}.

The last case to consider is when $Z$ is self-sufficient and $Z'$ is not.
Note that by Lemma \ref{lem:first-grouping} the first $\B$ steps (i.e., the steps $t,\ldots,t+\B-1$ of the strategy computed for $Z'$ realize the grouping (cf. line~\ref{ln:phase:grouping})).
Moreover, during the process of grouping, no agent leaves $Z'$.
Thus, the grouping in $Z'$ can be successfully executed along with the internal evacuation strategy for $Z$ (using the same steps, cf. line~\ref{ln:phase:execute-internal-strategy}).

By the assumption of the lemma on the computability of internal strategies for self-sufficient zones, at the end of the step $t+\B-1$ all agents in $Z$ evacuated (due to $\B\geq\OPT$) and hence the strategy executed in line~\ref{ln:phase:execute-exclusive-strategies} cannot be `affected' by any agent in $\agents(Z)$.
\end{proof}

Using the fact that the strategy executed in line~\ref{ln:phase:execute-internal-strategy} needs $\B$ steps and the strategies in lines~\ref{ln:phase:grouping} and~\ref{ln:phase:execute-exclusive-strategies} combined need $3\B$ steps, we get the following.

\begin{lemma} \label{lem:singlePhaseTime}
An execution of Algorithm \ref{alg:SinglePhase} with input $\B$ results in $3\B$ steps used by an evacuation strategy.
\qed
\end{lemma}

