\subsection{Combining all phases} \label{sec:framework:phases}

In this section we combine several phases into a strategy that performs all actions for a given $\B$-partition $(V_1,\ldots,V_l)$.
We point out that if $\B$ is not large enough, then the strategy may fail in the sense that it will not evacuate all agents. 
Hence we focus on the analysis under the already mentioned assumption that $\B\geq\OPT$, dealing with the opposite case in the next section.

In what follows, we prove that under the above assumption Algorithm \ref{alg:OneAttempt} gives a successful evacuation strategy.
%
Moreover, this applies regardless of the choice of zones, and consequently of the corresponding zone graph $\ZG=(\ZGV,\ZGE)$, and regardless of the choice of the coloring computed in this algorithm. 

For a color $i$ in coloring $c$ of the zone graph, we use $\agents(c,i)$ to denote the set of agents initially located in all zones of color $i$, i.e.,
\[\agents(c,i) = \bigcup\agents(Z),\mbox{ where } c(Z)=i.\]

