\subsection{Efficiency of the framework --- proof of Theorem \ref{thm:EvacuationTime}}
\label{sec:prf}

In the following, we finally bound the evacuation time achieved by Algorithm \ref{alg:Evacuation}.
For the purpose of the proof, all steps that occur as a result of the execution of the $j$-th iteration of the while-loop of Algorithm \ref{alg:Evacuation} (i.e., with $\B=2^j$) are called the \emph{$j$-th epoch} of the evacuation strategy.


\begin{proof}[Proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:EvacuationTime}]
Consider a single epoch $j$ in which $\B=2^j$.
Suppose first that the bound $\B=2^j$ is not large enough, i.e., $2^j<\OPT$.
This means that the call to Algorithm \ref{alg:OneAttempt} in line~\ref{ln:evac-phase} will not give a successful evacuation strategy.
By Lemma~\ref{lem:colored-zones}, the agents that are still present in the graph after this call occupy their homebases.
Denote by $\agents'$ the set of those agents.
By Proposition~\ref{prop:monotonicity}, there exists an evacuation strategy for $\agents'$ of length at most $\OPT$.
In other words, the upper bound on the number of steps of a successful evacuation strategy is non-increasing over consecutive epochs.
The number of steps in an epoch comes from the call in line~\ref{ln:evac-phase} of Algorithm \ref{alg:Evacuation} and it is $6d_j2^j$ due to Lemma~\ref{lem:colored-zones}.

Suppose now that $2^j\geq\OPT$.
The analysis is analogous except that by Lemma~\ref{lem:colored-zones} the evacuation ends after the call in line~\ref{ln:evac-phase} of Algorithm \ref{alg:Evacuation}.
The minimum $j$ that satisfies $2^j\geq\OPT$ is the $p$ in the statement of the lemma.
\end{proof}

