\section{Asymptotically optimal evacuation in grids} \label{sec:grids}

In this section we consider an evacuation in square grids that can also be seen as the Cartesian product of paths. 
By $n\times n$ \emph{grid} we mean a graph $G=(V,E)$ with the vertex set $V=\{(i,j)\colon 1\leq i,j\leq n\}$ and edge set $E=\{\{(i,j),(i',j')\}\colon |i-i'|+|j-j'|=1\}$.

For any $\B\geq 2$, a \emph{$\B$-area} is a subgraph of a grid graph $G$ induced by the vertices $(i,j)$ such that $a\B/2 < i \leq(a+1)\B/2$ and $b\B/2 < j \leq(b+1)\B/2$ for some $a,b\geq 0$.
Less formally, for $\B$-area we take out a $\B/2\times\B/2$ sized square whose right bottom vertex is $((a+1)\B/2,(b+1)\B/2)$ while $(1,1)$ is considered to be the left upper vertex of $G$.

Now, we consider any $\B$-area $H$ and divide it into zones.
%
(Technically, without loss of generality, we denote the vertices of $H$ by $(i,j)$, $1\leq i,j<\B/2$, i.e., we rename the indices of $H$ so that the upper left vertex is $(1,1)$).
%
Ideally, $H$ can be treated as a single zone (its diameter equal to $\B$ allows for that) but the location of exits may not allow forming a group of agents in sufficient time.
However, note that whenever this happens, $H$ has sufficiently many exits to be partitioned into many zones, all of which will be self-sufficient except for possibly a single one.

We say that a path $P\subseteq H$ is \emph{monotone} if $(1,x)\in V(P)$ and $(\B/2,y)\in V(P)$ for some $x$ and $y$, and for each $v=(i,z)\in V(P)$ either $\{v,(i+1,z)\}\in E(P)$ or $\{v,(i,z+1)\}\in E(P)$.


\begin{lemma} \label{lem:no-monotone-path}
If a $\B$-area $H$ does not have a monotone path, then each row of $H$ is a self-sufficient zone.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
If $H$ does not have a monotone path, then this in particular means that for each $j\in\{1,\ldots,\B/2\}$, one of the vertices $(1,j),\ldots,(\B/2,j)$ is an exit.
These vertices are one row in $H$ and let them form a zone $Z$.
Indeed, otherwise such a row itself is a monotone path.
The fact that $Z$ has an exit $x$ makes the following evacuation strategy successful: each agent in $\agents(Z)$ simply moves towards $x$ and evacuates through $x$.
Since $|\agents(Z)|\leq|V(Z)|\leq\B/2$, the duration of this strategy is at most $\B/2$.
This proves the necessary conditions required in a self-sufficient zone do hold.
\end{proof}
We note that it is important that the strategy `hidden' in the proof of Lemma~\ref{lem:no-monotone-path} can be computed by each agent in the zone only on the basis of its input to the problem.
In other words, no communication with other agents is necessary, which ensures that our internal strategies can be computed and executed within at most $\B$ steps.

\begin{lemma} \label{lem:monotone-path}
If a $\B$-area $H$ has a monotone path $P$, then there exists a $\B$-partition of $H$ with exactly one zone that is not self-sufficient.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Define for $H$ the following spanning tree $T_H$.
First, let $P\subseteq T_H$.
Then, for each $i\in\{1,\ldots,\B/2\}$, let the path induced by the vertices $(i,j)$, $j\in\{1,\ldots,\B/2\}$, belong to $T_H$.
(Informally, add all vertical paths to $T_H$).
Then, $T_H$ provides the zones contained in $H$ in the following way.
The only zone that is not self-sufficient will be denoted by $Z$.
For each $i\in\{1,\ldots,\B/2\}$ do the following.
Let the $i$-th column $C_i$ be the subset of vertices $\{(i,y)\colon y\in\{1,\ldots,\B/2\}\}$.
The maximal subset of $C_i\setminus X$ that contains the vertex in $C_i\cap V(P)$ and induces a connected subgraph of $H$ is added to the zone $Z$.
Note that such zone $Z$ fulfills Condition~\eqref{it:partition2}, where the required spanning tree is a subtree of $T_H$ whose diameter is at most $\B$.

The rest of the $\B$-area is partitioned into self-sufficient zones as follows.
Any other maximal subset $C_i'$ of $C_i\setminus (Z\cup X)$ that induces a connected subgraph of $H$ is made to be a self-sufficient zone, together with a unique exit $x$ in $C_i\setminus C_i'$ adjacent to a vertex in $C_i'$, in the partition.
This implies two facts: each of our self-sufficient zones has an evacuation strategy of length at most $\B$ and all these strategies use pairwise different exits for evacuation hence can be executed simultaneously (as done in Algorithm \ref{alg:OneAttempt}). 
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma} \label{lem:grid-coloring}
Let $G$ be a grid.
There exists a $\B$-partition of $G$ whose zone graph has a $\Theta(1)$-coloring.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
For each $\B$-area $H$ use Lemmas~\ref{lem:no-monotone-path} and~\ref{lem:monotone-path} to obtain a $\B$-partition that has at most one zone $Z$ that is not self-sufficient.
Let $a$ and $b$ be such that $v=(a\B/2+1,b\B/2+1)\in V(H)$ and call $H$ the $\B$-area with \emph{corner $(a,b)$}.
Consider which zones $Z'$ may be close to $Z$.
We only need to consider the case when $Z'$ is not self-sufficient.
Indeed, by the definition of the zone graph, the self-sufficient zones are isolated vertices and hence they do not affect the number of colors.
It holds that the corner of $Z'$ is $(a',b')$, where $|a-a'|\leq 2$ and $|b-b'|\leq 2$.
Hence, there are at most $24$ potential zones $Z'$.
Thus, the degree of each vertex of the zone graph is at most $24$ and hence the graph has $25$-coloring as required in the lemma.
\end{proof}
Note that the coloring in the above lemma gives the same number of colors regardless of the value of $\B$ we select.
Therefore, the number of colors does not change in subsequent epochs in the case of grids.


\begin{proof}[Proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:grids}]
First, run Algorithm~\ref{alg:Evacuation} using $\B$-partitions guaranteed by Lemma~\ref{lem:grid-coloring}.
Hence, for each $\B\in\{2^j\colon j\geq 1\}$, we have that $d_j=\Theta(1)$ is the number of colors used for the zone graph computed in the $j$-th epoch.
This gives, thanks to Theorem~\ref{thm:EvacuationTime}, a bound on the evacuation time
\[\Theta(1)\cdot\sum_{j=1}^{p}2^j=\Theta(1)2^p=\Theta(\OPT),\]
because $p=\lceil\log_2\OPT\rceil$.
\end{proof}

