In the opposite case, i.e., when $T$ misses some edges of some star $S_n^j$ (w.l.o.g. suppose that $\{ v_0^j, v_1^j\} $ does not belong to $T$), consider a single agent placed in $v_1^j$, i.e., $H = \{v_1^j\}$. In the optimal solution, one step is enough to reach the exit in $v_0^j$, but using $T$, at least $s$ steps are required to reach an exit.
Putting $k = s = \log n$, we show that a constant competitive solution using any arbitrarily selected spanning tree is impossible.

The graph used in the above construction contains nodes of high degree, but the grid graphs, which are of interest from a practical point of view, are bounded degree graphs, so an interesting question is whether a `spanning tree approach' can give a constant-competitive strategy for bounded degree graphs. 
This seems particularly interesting because we know that
for graph searching problems, see, e.g. Hollinger et~al.~\cite{HolKeh10}, and Katsilieris et al. \cite{Katsilieris10} such approach was successfully applied.

