I/O devices in public clouds have integrated increasing numbers of hardware accelerators, e.g., AWS Nitro, Azure FPGA and Nvidia BlueField.  
However, such specialized compute (1) is not explicitly accessible to cloud users with performance guarantee, (2) cannot be leveraged simultaneously by both providers and users, unlike general-purpose compute (e.g., CPUs).  
Through ten observations, we present that the fundamental difficulty of democratizing accelerators is insufficient performance isolation support. 
