\myparagraph{Challenges}
We find two fundamental challenges to achieve predictable accelerator performance. 
First, state-of-art multi-tenancy techniques widely used for I/O resource allocation and I/O performance isolation are not feasible for accelerators. % such as such as token bucket based rate limiting and Deficit Round Robin (DRR)
The fundamental reason is that, accelerators have computational \textit{non-linearity}.
They exhibit the following characteristics: 
\mycircle{1} egress bandwidth does not necessarily equal to its ingress bandwidth (e.g., compression vs.~decompression), 
\mycircle{2} accelerator throughput does not necessarily increase linearly with increasing message size, 
and \mycircle{3} different accelerator types and implementations exhibit different characteristics in terms of the above two factors. 
In contrast, current proposals for I/O accelerators still implicitly inherit the \textit{linearity assumption}\footnote{This is naturally true for network send/receive I/Os and disk read/write I/Os, though not explicitly clarified by prior multi-tenancy I/O works.} that all accelerators have equal egress/ingress bandwidth requirements and larger bandwidth can be linearly allocated for larger messages~\cite{grant2020fairnic,lin2020panic,eran2019nica,min2021gimbal,liu2019ipipe}. 
As a result, prior proposals fail to accurately allocate and precisely isolate accelerator throughput in public clouds. 
