Second, traffic shaping relying on hypervisors for accelerator traffic isolation~\cite{} is not feasible. 
In many cases, the hypervisor is bypassed for near-native performance and low virtualization cost~\cite{sr-iov}. %\jz{Hypervisor doesn't have the permission to shape the traffic to get detailed knowledge, VMs have the permission but don't have the trustworthy from providers perspective, and we think I/O device is the one to have such controllability. }
To solve this issue, our work proposes an accelerator-side traffic shaping that gets rid of host involvement. 
This approach controls the pace of accelerator DMA operations from accelerators to the host memory. It also splits accelerator DMA messages and manages the rates, burst sizes, buffer allocation and scheduling of colocated accelerator traffic flows. 
Finally, we enable the two designs to work together, i.e., traffic shaping with a priori knowledge of accelerator characteristics. 
