% The significant improvement when training on Set-B and evaluating on Set-A is particularly noteworthy, suggesting that including widely-spoken languages like English in the training set can enhance a model's ability to generalize to other languages. These findings highlight the importance of carefully considering the composition of training and evaluation sets in multilingual hate speech detection. They also emphasize the need for developing models that can better generalize across diverse language groups, particularly when moving from less commonly spoken languages to more widely spoken ones.

