\noindent Fix $w\in \mathbb{R}^p$, $\phi:\mathbb{R}^p\times \mathbb{R}^d\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^p$, $z\in \mathbb{R}^p$, $x\in \mathbb{R}^d$.  Fix a continuous paths $y_{\cdot}:=(y_s)_{s\in [0,t]}$ and $x_{\cdot}:=(x_s)_{s\in [0,t]}$, and define the paths
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:controlled_reservoir_system}
\begin{aligned}
    z_t & = z + \int_0^t\, \phi(z_s,x_s)\,ds\\
    \hat{\beta}_t & = \pi(z_0^0,y_0) + \int_0^t\, \pi(z_s^0,y_s)\, y_s\,ds
    ,
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
where $\pi(z,y):= (zz^{\top})^{-1}zy$.  Given the paths $x_{\cdot}$, $y_{\cdot}$, and the reservoir $\phi$, the evolution $\hat{\beta}_{\cdot}$ is optimal in the sense that it minimizes the cumulative distance between the (controlled) reservoir system $(z_{\cdot},\beta_{\cdot})$ and the target $y_{\cdot}$; quantified by
\[
    \hat{\beta}_{\cdot}\in \operatorname{argmin}_{\beta_{\cdot}}\,\int_{0}^t \Big\| \beta_s^{\top}\,z_t - y_s \Big\|^2 \,ds
.
\]
Unfortunately, we cannot observe the path $y_{\cdot}$ beyond the current moment in time, and therefore, one must extrapolate.  A robust extrapolation tends to take one of two forms, one is a worst-case adversarial form where one considers all possible trajectories of $y_{\cdot}$ as being equally likely and tries to optimize the readout/control $\beta_{\cdot}$ for it, leading to a very conservative readout.  Another, more practical alternatively, which also allows for generation, is to assume a \textit{system} of (stochastic/random) priors paths on $x$, run the optimal (controlled) reservoir system~\eqref{eq:controlled_reservoir_system} for each assumed prior on $x$, allowing us to predict the evolution of $y$ well into the future.


\noindent  This can be done by calibrating several SDE models to the observed path $x_{\cdot}$.  For $i=0,\dots,N$, $X^i_{\cdot}$ be a strong solution to the SDE
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:Prior_X}
    X_t^i = x + \int_0^t\, \mu^i(X_s^i)ds + \int_0^t\, \sigma^i(X_s^i)\,dW^i_s
    .
\end{equation}
These will function as our system of \textit{encoders}.
Each of these induces a stochastic reservoir system
\[
\begin{aligned}
        Z_t^i & = z + \int_0^t \,\phi(Z_s^i,X_s^i)\,ds \\
        Y_t^i & = \beta^{i\top}\, Z_t^i,
\end{aligned}
\]
for $i=1,\dots,N$.
Each $Y^i_{\cdot}$ represents one possible evolution of the reservoir under the assumption that the inputs path will evolve according to the SDE~\eqref{eq:Prior_X}.  These acts as our system of \textit{decoders}.  

The main role of these decoders is that, once an input path is generated by~\eqref{eq:Prior_X} one may forecast the most likely evolution of the target into the future, assuming that: 1) the prior on $X_{\cdot}$ is correct and 2) that the reservoir accurately approximates the causal map $x_{\cdot}\mapsto y_{\cdot}$ pathwise.  Since there is ample research on if and when (2) holds \cite{XXX}, we focus on efforts on the implications of (1).

Our objective is to route the information of each stochastic reservoir system into our \textit{primary} (controlled) stochastic reservoir model $Z^0_{\cdot}$ by controlling the readout map $\beta_{\cdot}^0$ so as to aggregate the average information across all plausible reservoirs.  We do this by optimizing the objective function
\[
J^0_t(\beta^0_{\cdot}) := 
\mathbb{E}\Big[
    \int_0^{t+\tau}\, \|\beta^{0}_s - \hat{\beta}_{s\wedge t}\|^2 + 
    \|
    \beta_t^0 Z_s^0 -
    \sum_{i=1}^N\,w_{t}^i\,Y_s^i
    \|^2
    \,ds
\Big],
\]
where the weights $(w_t^i)_{\cdot}$ are chosen by \textbf{...TBD...} and satisfy $\sum_{i=1}^N\,w^i_t=1$ and $0\le w^i_t\le 1$ for all $i=1,\dots,N$ and all $t\ge 0$.  The optimal readout (control) is given of rolling-window regression type
\[
    \beta^0_t = \pi\Big(Z_0,\sum_{n=1}^N\,w_{t}^n\,Y_s^i\Big) + \int_0^t\, \pi\Big(Z_s,
    \sum_{i=1}^N\,w_{t}^i\,Y_s^i
    \Big)
    \,
    ds
\]
which is found by solving the associated first-order conditions to this control problem.  Unfortunately, however, such a model is not adversarially robust in the sense that it does not arise as an equilibrium solution to a \textit{game} where each ($i^{th}$) player is trying to fool (control) the system to sway $\beta_{\cdot}^0$ towards $Y_{\cdot}^i$.

