For example, for $m=2$ we have $\Mon=\{x_0x_1,x_1,x_0,\bm{1}\}.$ Furthermore, the \emph{support of monomial} $f=x_{l_1}\dots x_{l_s}$ is $\ind(f)=\{l_1,\dots,l_s\}$ and its degree is $\deg(f)=|\ind(f)|.$ The degree induces a ranking on any monomial set $\I\subseteq\Mon$, that is, $\I=\bigcup_{j=0}^{m}\I_j$, where $\I_j=\{f\in\I\mid\deg(f)=j\}.$
\begin{remark}
    By convention, we use the indices of the most reliable sub-channels to define polar codes. These indices correspond to $\overline{\bin(i)}$ of all monomials $\mathbf{x}^{\bin(i)}\in\I$. For example, the index $12=(1100)_2$ for $m=4$ corresponds to $x_0x_1$ where $\bin(i)=(0011)_2$. %; that is, a variable $x_{i_j}$ indicates the position . Therefore, given $\mathbf{x}^{\bin(i)}$, the index of the corresponding sub-channel is obtained 
    Throughout this paper, we may use sub-channel indices and monomials, or decimal and binary representation of indices interchangeably. 
\end{remark}

