\subsection{Reliability distribution of same-degree monomials}
Each bit position $j$ contributes a term $i_j \cdot \beta^j$ to $\PW(i)$. %, where  $j=0$ (least significant bit) contributes $\beta^0=1$, $j=1$ contributes $\beta^1$, $j=2$ contributes $\beta^2$, and so on up to $j=m-1\left(\beta^{m-1}\right)$. 
Higher bit positions (closer to $m-1$ ) contribute larger values due to the exponential increase of $\beta^j$ when $\beta>1$. %For example, for the factor $\beta=2^{\frac{1}{4}}$, every increment of 4 in $j$ doubles the factor value. Thus, $\PW(i)$ depends not only on the number of 1's (Hamming weight, or equivalently degree of monomial) but also on their positions.

\begin{example}\label{ex:WP_max-min}
For $m=8$, $\beta>0$, and Hamming weight $t=4$, the maximum $\PW(i)$ occurs when the 1's occupy the highest bit positions; $\underbrace{\tiny 11\cdots11}_{(t-1)\times \text{`1'}}\underbrace{\tiny 00\cdots00}_{(m-t+1)\times \text{`0'}}$ (here $i=11110000_2$):
$$
\PW(i)_{\max }=\beta^4+\beta^5+\beta^6+\beta^7 \approx 10.57.
$$
The minimum $\PW(i)$ occurs when the 1's occupy the lowest bit positions; $\underbrace{00\cdots00}_{(m-t)\times \text{`0'}}\underbrace{11\cdots11}_{(t)\times \text{`1'}}$ (here, $i=$ $00001111_2$ ):
$$
\PW(i)_{\min }=\beta^0+\beta^1+\beta^2+\beta^3 \approx 5.285.
$$
Since the powers of $\beta$ grow exponentially, the distribution of $\PW(i)$ values in $\left[5.285, 10.57\right]$ will not be uniform, but will spread more widely (the difference between adjacent $\PW(i)$'s increases) toward $\PW(i)_{\min}$ and $\PW(i)_{\max}$.% when the 1's are concentrated in the highest or lowest bit positions, respectively, due to the exponential weighting of $\beta^j$..
