\overline{\bin(i)}=\left(\left(1-i_{m-1}\right)\left(1-i_{m-2}\right) \cdots\left(1-i_0\right)\right)_2, 
$$
where the Hamming weight is $m-t$, since $\bar{i}_j=1-i_j$ flips all bits of $\bin(i)$. Then, the polarization weight of $\overline{\bin(i)}$ is
$$
\PW(\bar{i})=\sum_{j=0}^{m-1} \bar{i}_j \cdot \beta^j=\sum_{j=0}^{m-1}\left(1-i_j\right) \cdot \beta^j = \sum_{j=0}^{m-1} \beta^j-\PW(i).
$$
Let $S_m=\sum_{j=0}^{m-1} \beta^j=\frac{\beta^m-1}{\beta-1}$, which is a geometric series sum if $\beta \neq 1$. Then, the polarization weights $\PW(i)$ and $\PW(\bar{i})$ are symmetric around $S_m / 2$; that is,
\begin{equation}
    \PW(\bar{i})=S_m - \PW(i).
\end{equation}
Furthermore, for every $m$, the number of all possible indices with Hamming weight $t$ and $m-t$ is equal as 
$$
    {m \choose t}={m \choose m-t}. 
$$
Therefore, the distributions of $\PW(i)$ for the indices of the Hamming weights $t$ and $m-t$ are reflections of each other around $S_m / 2$. %This means the distribution of polarization weights for Hamming weight $t$ is identical in shape to that of Hamming weight $m-t$, but shifted in opposite directions relative to $S_m / 2$. 

