We split our fine-grained data into training, validation, and test sets with a ratio of 8:1:1 using the stratified sampling strategy. We finally map each word's label to the tokens contained as the token-level annotations.

\section{Streaming Content Monitor}%
\label{sec:method}

In this section, we describe the design of the proposed Streaming Content Monitor (SCM) and its corresponding training strategy, with an overview of the training and inference (\Sref{subsec:overview}), the details of the optimization objective in hierarchical consistency-aware learning strategy (\Sref{subsec:training}), and the usage of SCM during inference in real-world deployment (\Sref{subsec:inference}).

\subsection{Overview}
\label{subsec:overview}

\begin{figure*}[tb] \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.95\textwidth]{pics/training_v5.pdf}
    \caption{Overview of the modules. During the training phase, \ourseqcls is a detachable module that helps to build a comprehensive sight of the whole response and a rough perception of the possible future tokens. During the inference phase, we only use the well-trained \ourextractor and \ourtokenclf to follow LLMs' output streaming to perform timely judgment with acceptable latency, and a hyperparameter $k$ is used to determine the minimum number of detected harmful tokens that terminates generation.}
    \label{fig:training}
    % \vspace{-1em}
\end{figure*}

The SCM is designed to work in parallel with the streaming generation of the monitored LLM.
During the inference phase, the LLM begins to generate a response $\mathcal{T}=\{t_1,t_2,\cdots,t_n\}$ autoregressively according to the given prompt $\mathcal{P}=\{p_1,p_2,\cdots,p_m\}$, where $m$ and $n$ are the number of tokens in the prompt and response, respectively.
At each time step $i$ in output streaming, the monitored LLM generates the token $t_i$ conditioned on the previous tokens $\mathcal{T}_{<i}=\{t_1,t_2,\cdots,t_{i-1}\}$ and the prompt $\mathcal{P}$.
Together \ourextractor and \ourtokenclf, the SCM follows the LLM's output streaming to perform timely judgment that determines the contribution scores of a current token $t_i$, denoted as $c_{t_i}$, to the harmfulness of the response:
\begin{equation}
    c_{t_i} = P(y=1\mid \mathcal{P},\mathcal{T}_{<i},t_i) = P\bigl(f_\text{tok}(h_i)\bigr),\hspace{5pt}
    h_i = z(\mathcal{P},\mathcal{T}_{<i},t_i),
    \label{eq:token_classifier}
\end{equation}
where $y=1$ denotes the harmful class, $f_\text{tok}(\cdot)$ is the \ourtokenclf, and $z(\cdot)$ is the \ourextractor that integrates the information from the prompt and the generated tokens into a token representation $h_i$.

During the training phase, to mitigate the training-inference gap caused by treating the incomplete output as a complete one, the training strategy should adapt the SCM to make predictions with incomplete information. Therefore, we adopt a multi-task learning framework to inject holistic response-level knowledge into token-level representations, where the former helps the latter build a comprehensive sight of the whole response and a rough perception of the possible future tokens.
% response classifier
As shown in \Fref{fig:training}, the representation of the last token in a response is regarded as the overall representation of the response, which is transferred to the \ourseqcls to judge the harmfulness of the response $c_\text{hol}$ similar to \eref{eq:token_classifier}:
\begin{equation}
    c_\text{hol} = P(y=1\mid \mathcal{P},\mathcal{T}) = P\bigl(f_\text{hol}(h_n)\bigr),
    \label{eq:response_classifier}
\end{equation}
where $f_\text{hol}(\cdot)$ is the \ourseqcls.
To enhance the correlation of token- and response-level features, we follow an assumption that \textit{harmful tokens only exist in harmful responses} and employ a propositional boolean expression constraint, which explicitly regularizes the SCM with the implicit logical correlation of predictions between the response ($c_\text{hol}$) and the tokens contained ($c_\text{tok}$). The token representation is aggreagated by applying certain pooling operation $g(\cdot)$ over all $c_{t_i}$, \ie, $c_\text{tok}=g\left(\{c_{t_i}\}_{i=1}^n\right)$.

\subsection{Hierarchical Consistency-Aware Learning}
\label{subsec:training}

To implement the training strategy described in \Sref{subsec:overview}, the overall optimization objective includes three components, formulated as:
\begin{equation}
    L = \alpha \cdot L_\text{tok} + (1-\alpha) \cdot L_\text{hol} + \beta \cdot L_\text{logic},
    \label{eq:loss}
\end{equation}
where $L_\text{tok}$ and $L_\text{hol}$ are the loss functions to optimize the predictions of \ourtokenclf and \ourseqcls, respectively, $L_\text{logic}$ is the loss function to constrain the logical consistency between the two above, and $\alpha$ and $\beta$ are the hyperparameters to balance the three components. We use the standard binary cross-entropy loss for both $L_\text{tok}$ and $L_\text{hol}$.

To better exploit the ability to understand potential future outputs given the incomplete semantics, the training strategy we designed tries to inject future knowledge reflecting the harmfulness towards the whole response to the judgment of every single token. We exploit the implicit logical constraint that exists between those two levels of information.
Specifically, if the response is predicted as harmful by \ourseqcls using the last token finally, at least one token should be predicted as harmful by \ourtokenclf. If the response is benign, then all the tokens in it should be judged as benign.
Inspired by works that regularize neural network with logic-driven loss functions~\citep{logic2015, logic2020}, we thus consider the propositional rule $F=f_\text{hol}(h_n)\Rightarrow g\left(\{f_\text{tok}(h_i)\}_{i=1}^n\right)$.
Then we have
\begin{equation}
    \begin{aligned}
    P(F) &= P\left( f_{\text{hol}}(h_n) \Rightarrow g\left(\{f_{\text{tok}}(h_i)\}_{i=1}^{n}\right) \right) \\
    &= \neg P\left(f_{\text{hol}}(h_n)\right) \lor P\left(g\left(\{f_{\text{tok}}(h_i)\}_{i=1}^{n}\right)\right) \\
    &= \neg c_{\text{hol}} \lor c_{\text{tok}} \\
    &= 1 - c_{\text{hol}} + c_{\text{hol}} \cdot c_{\text{tok}},
    \end{aligned}
    \label{eq:logic_loss}
\end{equation}
where $c_\text{hol}$ is calculated by \eref{eq:response_classifier} and $c_\text{tok}$ is obtained by taking operations $g(\cdot)$ over all $c_{t_i}$ calculated by \eref{eq:token_classifier}.
Specifically, we max-pool over all tokens' scores to select the most potentially harmful token, \ie, $g(\cdot)=\max (\cdot)$.
Our objective here is maximizing $P(F)$, \ie, $L_\text{logic}=-\log P(F)$, to improve the logical consistency between coarse-grained and fine-grained predictions.

\subsection{Application in Practice}
\label{subsec:inference}

After training ends, \ourseqcls is detached, and only \ourextractor and \ourtokenclf are used to form the SCM, which can easily collaborate with existing LLMs as a plug-and-play module for LLM systems to provide timely feedback on the safety of the generated tokens.

\paragraph{Naive partial detection.}
The basic usage of the SCM is to directly use the token-level prediction $c_{t_i}$ to estimate the probability of potential harmfulness of the entire response (both those having generated and to be possibly generated) as of the current token $t_i$. 
For the most recent output token $t_i$ generated by the monitored LLM during the streaming generation, the probability of the response being harmful is calculated as
\begin{equation}
    \textit{Harm}_{@1}(\mathcal{P},\mathcal{T}) =\mathds{1}\left(c_{t_i}\right)=
    \begin{cases}
        1,\quad \text{if } c_{t_i} \geqslant \theta; \\
        0,\quad \text{otherwise},
    \end{cases}
    \label{eq:naive}
\end{equation}
where $\theta$ is a threshold to determine whether the token is harmful or not, $\mathds{1}(\cdot)$ is the indicator function.
Different from the existing works, ours is capable of having a better awareness of the overall semantic harmfulness due to the understanding of potential future outputs learned in the training phase. In other words, \textit{the model is aware that the output is unfinished}, so it should not merely consider the token having been determined but also the possible future generation.

\paragraph{Delay-$k$ partial detection.}
Compared with the naive partial detection that determines the harmfulness of the response with only one token, it is better for LLM service providers to have a more controllable way to balance the sensitivity and tolerance of content moderation.
High sensitivity to harmful output means low tolerance for the number of harmful token occurrences, which catch any potentially harmful content to ensure a low missing alarm rate~(MAR), but oversensitivity may lead to misclassification of harmless sentences as harmful, causing high false alarm rate~(FAR).
We argue that the control of sensitivity should be determined by the service providers according to actual scenarios.
To implement such a flexible adjustment mechanism, one can set a threshold, say $k$, to determine the minimum number of detected harmful tokens that trigger generation termination.
In this case, the monitored LLM will continue to generate the response until there are $k$ harmful tokens being detected, so for the most recent output token $t_i$ during the streaming generation, the harmfulness of the entire response is predicted by the following :
\begin{equation}
    \textit{Harm}_{@k}(\mathcal{P},\mathcal{T}) =\mathds{1}\left(\sum_{j=1}^{i} \mathds{1}(c_{t_j})\right)=
    \begin{cases}
    1,\quad \text{if } \left( \sum_{j=1}^{i} \mathds{1}(c_{t_j})\right) \geqslant k; \\
    0,\quad \text{otherwise}.
    \end{cases}
    \label{eq:aggregate}
\end{equation}
According to Eq.~(\ref{eq:aggregate}), naive partial detection is a special case of Delay-$k$ partial detection where $k=1$.
The larger the $k$, the lower the FAR and the higher the MAR.
This allows LLM service providers to adjust the strictness of harmfulness monitoring flexibly according to the requirements of specific scenarios.
For example, children's content filtering requires smaller $k$ for rigorous moderation, so a low MAR is preferred; however, for intelligent online customer services that value user experiences, the high FAR may lower user experience, so a compromise may be made on the MAR.
Recalling \Fref{fig:figure1} (b), it illustrates Delay-\textit{2} partial detection, where the words ``look'' and ``filthy'' are judged as harmful tokens, and the generation will be terminated at the position of ``filthy''.

\section{Experiment}
\label{sec:Experiments}

In this section, we present the comprehensive experimental results and analysis of the proposed SCM.
Additional results are provided in \Apdxref{appendix:additional_results}.

\subsection{Experiment Setup}
\label{subsec:experiment_setup}

\paragraph{Baselines}
From our knowledge, there are no publicly off-the-shelf partial detection models yet, so we fine-tuned several models with full parameters on the training set of \ourdataset to reflect the performance of full and partial detection.
We use the pre-trained ModernBERT~\citep{modernbert} (\textit{large}; 0.4B parameters) and Qwen2.5~\citep{qwen25} (0.5B, 1.5B, and 7B parameters) as the encoders and replace their output heads with an MLP for binary classification.
For the full detection, we input the entire response and take the hidden state of the target token in the last layer (the [CLS] token in ModernBERT and the last token in Qwen2.5) as the representation of the entire response to obtain an overall response-level prediction.
For partial detection, the prediction of each token is obtained by conducting the full detection based on the cumulative input of all the preceding tokens.
\paragraph{Metrics}
We mainly use the precision, recall, and $F_1$ scores as the evaluation metrics.
In detail, for the full detection, we directly obtain the response-level predictions and then calculate the metrics for both benign and harmful responses.
For the partial detection, we first obtain the token-level predictions and then leverage the Delay-$k$ partial detection to determine the response-level prediction as described in \Sref{subsec:inference}.
The best $k$ is selected from $\{1,2,\cdots,10\}$ based on the validation $F_1$ score.
We report the macro $F_1$ score, which is the average of $F_1$ scores of the benign and harmful responses.

\paragraph{Training Recipe}
Our SCMs take different scales of Qwen2.5 from 0.5B to 7B as the base models and replace their output head with the \ourextractor and \ourtokenclf.
For all models, we fine-tune them with full parameters, and the maximum length of the input sequence is set to 4,096 tokens.
More details to reproduce the experiments are in \Apdxref{appendix:additional_results}.

