\subsection{Experimental Results on Data Applied with Different Token-Level Label Annotation Strategies}
\label{appendix:annotation_details}

We have made multiple attempts to obtain the fine-grained annotations:

\textbf{Assigning the harmfulness label to the token that has large differences between its likelihoods in safety-aligned and uncensored LLMs~(Diff).} Motivated by~\citet{criticaltoken}, we input the harmful response into both the safety-aligned LLM and its counterpart uncensored one in a teacher-forcing manner and then calculate the likelihood of each token in the response. The tokens with a large difference in likelihood (here, 0.5)  between the two models are considered as harmful.

\textbf{Locating harmful words using the understanding capability of the strongly-capable LLM~(Delete).} We follow~\citet{tlcr}, instructing external strong LLMs (here, \gptfouromini) to change the given response from being harmful to being benign by token deletion only. The deleted tokens are considered as harmful.

We manually analyzed the results labeled with the above approaches, none of which guarantees an acceptable rationality to a reliable fine-grained annotator. We find that harmful intents are more likely to be indicated through \textit{the notional words (such as nouns, verbs, and adjectives), while the function words (such as prepositions, conjunctions, and exclamations) are less indicative.}
Based on this assumption, we design a heuristic annotation approach that leverages the part-of-speech~(POS) tagging to filter out function words and only label the notional words as harmful words.

