\paragraph{SCM}
We use the pre-trained Qwen2.5 family (specifically, 0.5B, 1.5B, and 7B versions) as the encoder, drop its output head, and add two new heads: \ourtokenclf and \ourseqcls.
The \ourtokenclf consists of a linear layer that projects the dimension of the hidden state to 2 classes and a dropout layer with a dropout rate of 0.1.
The \ourseqcls is a simple linear layer that projects the dim of the hidden state to 2 classes.
For every prompt-response pair in the training set, we fetch the last layer's hidden states of all tokens and then put all the hidden states into the \ourtokenclf to obtain the token-level logits and put only the hidden state of the last token into the \ourseqcls to obtain the response-level logits.
