DPO algorithm utilizes the policy model itself as a reward model.
Given the preference dataset $\mathcal{D}=\{x_i, y_{i}^c, y_{i}^r\}_{i=1}^M$ where $y^c$ and $y^r$ are chosen and rejected responses, the objective of which could be formulated as:
\begin{equation}
\small
    \begin{aligned}
        \phi(x, y)  = \gamma \log \dfrac{\pi_{\theta}(y \mid x)}{\pi_{\textrm{ref}}(y \mid x)},\hspace{5pt}
        L_{\textrm{DPO}}  = -\sum^{M}_{i = 1} \log \sigma\left(\phi\left(x_{i}, y_{i}^c\right)-\phi\left(x_{i}, y_{i}^r\right)\right),
    \end{aligned}
\end{equation}
where $\pi_{\theta}$ is the policy model, $\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}$ is the reference model, $\gamma$ is the coefficient for the KL divergence penalty, and $\phi(x,y)$ represents an implicit reward function (ignore the partition function).
The DPO method is simple and effective, but the data are labeled at the sequence level, and it tends to miss the detailed significance and context of specific words and phrases within sequences, so some works have attempted to extend DPO to token-level with fine-grained signals~\citep{tlcr,criticaltoken,treg}.
Following~\citet{criticaltoken}, the token-level DPO is extended by incorporating the token-level scores $s_i^r=\{s_{i,t}^r\}_{t=1}^T$ where $T$ is the length of the response $y$:
\begin{equation}
\small
        \phi_{s}(x,y,s)= \gamma \sum_{t = 1}^{T} s_{t}\log \dfrac{\pi_{\theta}\left(y_{t}\mid x, y_{<t}\right)}{\pi_{\textrm{ref}}\left(y_{t}\mid x, y_{<t}\right)}, \hspace{5pt}
        L_\textrm{TokenDPO} = -\sum_{i = 1}^{M} \log \sigma \left(\phi\left(x_{i}, y_{i}^c \right)-\phi_{s} \left(x_{i}, y_{i}^r, s_{i}^r \right)\right).
\end{equation}

We predict token-level scores to the rejected responses in the train set of PKU-SafeRLHF~\citep{pkusaferlhf} using our SCM-7B, and then conduct experiments on Llama-3.1-8B-Uncensored mentioned in \Sref{sec:data_construction} with DPO and token-level DPO to observe the pure effect of them.

\Tref{tab:dpo_tokendpo_training_details} shows the training details of DPO and token-level DPO.
We use the AdamW optimizer and the training process is performed on 4 NVIDIA A800 GPUs with 80GB VRAM.
We use the HuggingFace's transformers package~\cite{wolf-etal-2020-transformers} and deepspeed libraries\footnote{\url{https://github.com/deepspeedai/DeepSpeed}} for implementation.

