\subsection{Greedy schedule}
\label{sec.algo.greedy}
We now describe how to compute a greedy schedule for the workflow, while accounting for the 
carbon cost of each interval (\EST does not consider intervals at all). 
%
%Due to the construction of the communication DAG $G_c$ we do not have to make a difference between computation and communication tasks and hence we do not make difference in 
%the following description. In general, 
%
The idea is to assign a score to each task, and sort the tasks accordingly. 
Afterwards, we process the tasks in this order and try to find a good starting time for them.

\paragraph{Scores for the tasks.}\label{par.algo.scores}
%We now describe the scores for the tasks. The scores should 
The goal of the scores is to express how beneficial it is to schedule a task before other tasks. 

The first  score is the {\bf slack} $s(v)$ of task~$v$, which represents %. The slack of a task $v$, $s(v)$, describes 
the difference between the 
latest possible starting time of a task~$v$, $LST(v)$,  %, i.e., $LST(v)+\omega(v)$ 
and its earliest start time $EST(v)$.

$LST$ can be computed similarly to $EST$, using a queue to handle tasks. 
We set $LST(v) = T - \omega(v)$ if $v\in V_c$ and decrease by one the out-degree of predecessor tasks
(tasks~$u$ such that $(u,v)\in E_c$). A task~$u$ obtains an out-degree of~$0$ once all of its successors 
have been handled, and we can 
then compute its latest start time as:
\begin{equation*}
  LST(u) = \min_{(u,v) \in E_c} \{LST(v) - \omega(u)\}. 
\end{equation*}

Hence, the slack $s(v) = LST(v) - EST(v)$ describes the number of time units by which a task~$v$ can be shifted, 
since its start time has to be between $EST(v)$ and~$LST(v)$. 
%$$s(v) = LST(v) - EST(v). $$
If the slack of a task is large,
then it usually means that we have some flexibility to schedule it. 
We therefore try first to schedule tasks with a small slack, since there will still be room to shift tasks
with a higher slack later. Note, however, that the slack does  
%Hence, a task with small slack usually means that there is not much flexibility to schedule it and hence it might 
%be beneficial to schedule the task first, since we have for tasks with large slack still room for shifting the tasks to time units with a higher power budget. 
%However, this can be misleading for the scheduler since it does 
not account for the running time of the task. 
