\label{app.cost}

In this section, we detail how to compute the cost of a schedule in polynomial time.
The schedule~$\sigma$ gives the starting time of each task: task~$u\in V_c$ starts
at time~$\sigma(u)$, and hence completes at time $\sigma(u) + \omega(u)$. 

We proceed interval by interval. 
Recall that we are given $J$ intervals
$\{I_{1}, \dots, I_{J}\}$, where interval $I_{j}$ has length~$\ell_{j}$, and $\sum_{j=1}^{J} \ell_{j} =T$.
We have
$I_j = [b_j, e_j[$ so that $\ell_j = e_j -b_j$ for every $1 \leq j \leq J$, and 
