\title{
Complementary Time--Space Tradeoff for Self-Stabilizing Leader Election: Polynomial States Meet Sublinear Time
}
\date{}

\author{Yuichi Sudo}

\affil{Hosei University, Tokyo, Japan}



\begin{document}

\maketitle


\begin{abstract}
We study the self-stabilizing leader election (SS-LE) problem in the population protocol model, assuming exact knowledge of the population size $n$. Burman, Chen, Chen, Doty, Nowak, Severson, and Xu (PODC 2021) showed that this problem can be solved in $O(n)$ expected time with $O(n)$ states. Recently, Gąsieniec, Grodzicki, and Stachowiak (PODC 2025) proved that $n+O(\log n)$ states suffice to achieve $O(n \log n)$ time both in expectation and with high probability (w.h.p.). 
If substantially more states are available, sublinear time can be achieved.
Burman~et~al.~(PODC 2021) presented a $2^{O(n^\rho\log n)}$-state SS-LE protocol with a parameter $\rho$:
setting $\rho = \Theta(\log n)$ yields an optimal $O(\log n)$ time both in expectation and w.h.p., while $\rho = \Theta(1)$ results in $O(\rho\,n^{1/(\rho+1)})$ expected time. Very recently, Austin, Berenbrink, Friedetzky, Götte, and Hintze (PODC 2025) presented a novel SS-LE protocol parameterized by a positive integer $\rho$ with $1 \le \rho < n/2$ that solves SS-LE in $O(\sfrac{n}{\rho}\cdot\log n)$ time w.h.p.\ using $2^{O(\rho^2\log n)}$ states. This paper independently presents yet another time--space tradeoff of SS-LE: for any positive integer $\rho$ with $1 \le \rho \le \sqrt{n}$, SS-LE can be achieved within $O\left(\sfrac{n}{\rho}\cdot \log\rho\right)$ expected time using $2^{2\rho\lg\rho + O(\log n)}$ states.
The proposed protocol uses significantly fewer states than the protocol of Austin~et~al.\ requires to achieve any expected stabilization time above $\Theta(\sqrt{n}\log n)$.
When $\rho = \Theta\left(\sfrac{\log n}{\log \log n}\right)$,
the proposed protocol is the first to achieve sublinear time while using only polynomially many states.
A limitation of our protocol is that the constraint $\rho\le\sqrt{n}$ prevents achieving $o(\sqrt{n}\log n)$ time, whereas the protocol of Austin et~al.\ can surpass this bound.
\end{abstract}


\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}
This paper investigates the self-stabilizing leader election problem (SS-LE) in the population protocol model introduced by Angluin, Aspnes, Diamadi, Fischer, and Peralta~\cite{AAD+06}, which has been extensively studied in distributed computing for over two decades. Recently, the time--space tradeoff for SS-LE under exact knowledge of the population size $n$ has attracted intensive study, particularly in 2025. We present a novel time--space tradeoff that dramatically improves the upper bound on space complexity required to achieve any target time complexity in the range between $\Theta(\sqrt{n}\log n)$ and $o(n)$.

\subsection{Population Protocols}
In population protocols, we consider $n\ge2$ anonymous state machines called \emph{agents}, which together form a single \emph{population}. At each time step, an ordered pair of agents is selected uniformly at random to have a (pairwise) \emph{interaction}. The chosen agents assume the roles of \emph{initiator} and \emph{responder} and, following the transition function of a given protocol, update their states based on their current states and roles. Each agent outputs a symbol determined by its current state. Throughout this paper, $A$ denotes the set of all agents.

Formally, a \emph{protocol} is a five-tuple $\calP = (Q, \sinit, Y, \delta, \pi)$, where $Q$ is the set of agent states, $\sinit$ is the initial state (unused in self-stabilizing protocols), $Y$ is the set of output symbols, $\delta\colon Q \times Q \to Q \times Q$ is the transition function, and $\pi\colon Q \to Y$ is the output function.\footnote{
We omit the input symbol set and the input function here, as the leader election problem involves no external inputs.
}
A global state of the population (or \emph{configuration}) is a function $C\colon A \to Q$ that represents the current state of each agent. A configuration $C$ is \emph{output-stable} (or simply \emph{stable}) if no agent ever changes its output in any subsequent execution from $C$.



The complexity of a protocol is measured by its stabilization time and number of states.
 The \emph{stabilization time} of a protocol $\calP$ is the number of interactions divided by $n$ required for the population under $\calP$ to reach a stable configuration.\footnote{This definition applies to static problems such as leader election and majority; a more intricate definition is required for non-static problems. See \cite{AAF+08}.} Generally, the number of interactions divided by $n$ is called \emph{parallel time}, reflecting the practical behavior where agents often interact concurrently. Throughout this paper, ``time'' refers to parallel time. Since interacting agents are chosen uniformly at random, we evaluate stabilization time \emph{in expectation} or \emph{with high probability}. We define ``with high probability''(w.h.p.) as an event occurring with probability $1 - O(n^{-\eta})$ for any fixed positive constant $\eta =\Theta(1)$ (see Section~\ref{sec:notation} for the formal definition).
 The \emph{number of states} of a protocol is defined as the cardinality of its state set $Q$, i.e., $|Q|$.
 Because super-exponentially many states are sometimes used, we also express space complexity in \emph{bits}: $x$ bits correspond to $2^x$ states.

\subsection{Related Work}
Time–space tradeoffs arise for many problems: the more states available, the shorter the stabilization time. For example, the time–space tradeoff of the (non-self-stabilizing) leader election problem was intensively studied from 2015 to 2020. As the name implies, the leader election problem requires exactly one agent to output ‘L’ (leader), while all others output ‘F’ (follower). Doty and Soloveichik~\cite{DS18} proved that any $O(1)$-state protocol requires $\Omega(n)$ expected time to solve leader election (DISC 2015), establishing the time-optimality of the two-state protocol by Angluin et~al.~\cite{AAD+06}, which stabilizes in $O(n)$ expected time. Simultaneously, Alistarh and Gelashvili~\cite{AG18} presented a protocol using $O(\log^3 n)$ states and achieving $O(\log^3 n)$ expected time (ICALP 2015). Subsequent work~\cite{AAE+17, BCER17, AAG18, GS20, GSU18, SOI+20} successively improved these upper bounds from 2017 to 2019. Finally, Berenbrink, Giakkoupis, and Kling~\cite{BGK20} presented an $O(\log\log n)$-state and $O(\log n)$-expected-time protocol (STOC 2020). This protocol is both time- and space-optimal, owing to two lower bounds: Alistarh, Aspnes, Eisenstat, Gelashvili, and Rivest~\cite{AAE+17} showed that any protocol with fewer than $\tfrac{1}{2}\log\log n$ states requires $\Omega(n^2/\poly(\log n))$ expected time; and Sudo and Masuzawa~\cite{SM20} proved that any protocol requires $\Omega(\log n)$ states.\footnote{The latter lower bound goes beyond the simple coupon-collector argument, as it must handle initial configurations with all agents in the follower state.}


As mentioned above, this paper addresses the self-stabilizing leader election problem (SS-LE). Self-stabilization, introduced by Dijkstra~\cite{Dij74}, is a fault-tolerance concept that requires a protocol to converge to a correct configuration even when execution begins from an \emph{arbitrary} configuration. A population protocol $\calP$ solves SS-LE if, from any initial configuration, it eventually stabilizes with exactly one leader.
Solving SS-LE is more challenging than (non-self-stabilizing) leader election. An SS-LE protocol must create a leader when none exists and reduce multiple leaders to one; these opposing goals often conflict. Indeed, using the partition technique of \cite{AAF+08,CIW12}, one can easily show that SS-LE is impossible unless each agent knows the exact population size $n$ (see \cite{SOK+20}, p.~618, footnote).
Despite this impossibility result, SS-LE has been continuously studied under additional assumptions or relaxed self-stabilization requirements in the population protocol model.


One approach to solving SS-LE is to assume that each agent knows the exact population size $n$, i.e., $n$ is hard-coded into the protocol.
Tables~\ref{table:silent} and \ref{table:non-silent} summarize the upper and lower bounds in this setting, including our contributions, for \emph{silent} and \emph{non-silent} SS-LE protocols, respectively. Silent protocols require the population to eventually reach a configuration from which no agent changes its \emph{state} in any subsequent execution, a stronger requirement than stabilization.
In some protocols listed in these tables, the expected and w.h.p.~stabilization times coincide; in others, they differ. Nonetheless, the discrepancy is at most an $O(\log n)$ multiplicative factor. By the definition of self-stabilizing protocols, the following relationship holds:
\begin{remark}
For any function $f:\mathbb{N}^+\to\mathbb{N}^+$, if a protocol solves SS-LE in $O(f(n))$ expected time, then it solves SS-LE in $O(f(n)\log n)$ time w.h.p. Conversely, if a protocol solves SS-LE in $O(f(n))$ time w.h.p., then it solves SS-LE in $O(f(n))$ expected time.
\end{remark}

It is also worth mentioning here that all SS-LE protocols that assume exact knowledge of the population size $n$, listed in Tables~\ref{table:silent} and~\ref{table:non-silent}, also solve the more general problem of \emph{self-stabilizing ranking} (SS-RK). This problem requires $n$ agents, starting from an arbitrary configuration, to stabilize to have distinct labels (or \emph{ranks}) in $\{1,2,\dots,n\}$. Once they obtain unique ranks, the agent with rank $1$ can serve as the unique leader; 
hence, SS-LE reduces to SS-RK, while the converse reduction remains open. Nevertheless, the fact that all existing SS-LE protocols (assuming only knowledge of $n$) solve SS-RK suggests the following conjecture.
\begin{conjecture}
\label{conjecture:ss-rk}
Every SS-LE protocol solves SS-RK, at least implicitly: for any SS-LE protocol $\calP$ with state set $Q$, there exists a function $\xi\colon Q \to \{1,2,\dots,n\}$
such that for every stable configuration $C$ of $\calP$, $|\{\xi(C(a)) : a \in A\}| = n$.
\end{conjecture}

Assuming knowledge of $n$, Cai, Izumi, and Wada~\cite{CIW12} showed that any SS-LE protocol requires at least $n$ states and presented a silent, state-optimal ($n$-state), $O(n^2)$-expected-time SS-LE protocol.
Later, Burman, Chen, Chen, Doty, Nowak, Severson, and Xu~\cite{BCC+21} showed that increasing the number of states by a constant factor reduces the expected stabilization time to $O(n)$. They also proved this bound is optimal: any silent SS-LE protocol requires $\Omega(n)$ expected stabilization time (and $\Omega(n\log n)$ time w.h.p.). Dropping the silent requirement enables sublinear time (i.e., $o(n)$ time): they presented an SS-LE protocol that stabilizes in $O(\log n)$ time w.h.p., using super-exponentially many states (i.e., $O(n^{\log n}\cdot\log n)$ bits). No faster protocol exists, since any SS-LE protocol requires $\Omega(\log n)$ time---a trivial lower bound following from the coupon collector argument, unlike the non-self-stabilizing case~\cite{SM20}. They also proposed a protocol parameterized by a constant $\rho=\Theta(1)$ that stabilizes in $O(\rho\,n^{1/(\rho+1)})$ expected time using a reduced yet still super-exponential number of states, namely $O(n^\rho\log n)$ bits.


