Building on Burman et al.~\cite{BCC+21}, SS-LE protocols with exact knowledge of $n$ have attracted intensive study recently. Four new works---Berenbrink, Elsässer, Götte, Hintze, and Kaaser~\cite{BEG+25}; Gąsieniec, Grodzicki, and Stachowiak~\cite{GGS25}; Araya and Sudo~\cite{AS25}; and Austin, Berenbrink, Friedetzky, Götte, and Hintze~\cite{ABF+25}---
were made public on arXiv in the first half of 2025 and will appear in the proceedings of ICDCS 2025, PODC 2025, SIROCCO 2025, and PODC 2025, respectively.

The first two studies, Berenbrink et al.~\cite{BEG+25} and Gąsieniec et~al.~\cite{GGS25}, provide new insights on silent SS-LE. Recall that any SS-LE protocol requires at least $n$ states. A natural question is how many additional states suffice to substantially reduce the stabilization time of the space-optimal protocol of Cai et~al.~\cite{CIW12}, which stabilizes in $O(n^2)$ time (both in expectation and w.h.p.). Berenbrink et al.~\cite{BEG+25} showed that adding $O(\log^2 n)$ states yields a silent SS-LE protocol within optimal $O(n\log n)$ stabilization time w.h.p. Gąsieniec et al.~\cite{GGS25} further reduced the additional states to $O(\log n)$ while preserving optimal w.h.p.\ time. Moreover, they proved that a single additional state suffices to achieve $O(n^{7/4}\log^2 n)$ time w.h.p., still maintaining the silent property.

For non-silent SS-LE research, Burman et al.~\cite{BCC+21} identified promising directions. They proved that dropping the silent requirement enables sublinear stabilization time. However, all their sublinear-time protocols require a super-exponential number of states (i.e., $\omega(n)$ bits), even when minimizing space by setting $\rho = 1$, which yields an $O(\sqrt{n})$-expected-time protocol.
Moreover, if Conjecture~\ref{conjecture:ss-rk} holds, SS-LE has the same time--space tradeoff as SS-RK, which inherently requires detecting rank collisions (i.e., two or more agents with the same rank). In their words, ``the core difficulty'' of fast SS-RK lies in collision detection. Indeed, their non-silent protocols rely on a super-exponential number of states for this task. Based on this discussion, Burman et al.~\cite{BCC+21} posed the following open problems:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item   
  Does there exist an SS-LE protocol that achieves sublinear stabilization time using only a subexponential number of states (i.e., $o(n)$ bits, which results in $o(c^n)$ states for any constant $c>1$)?
  \item Does there exist a protocol that detects collisions in sublinear time using only a subexponential number of states even in the non‐self‐stabilizing setting? Here, ``non‐self‐stabilizing'' means that agents begin with adversarially assigned ranks but otherwise share the designated initial state.
\end{enumerate}

Araya and Sudo~\cite{AS25} answered the second question affirmatively: a near-linear number of states suffices to detect collisions in polynomial time. Specifically, they proposed a protocol that, in the non-self-stabilizing setting, detects collisions in $O(\sqrt{n\log n})$ expected time using only $O(n\cdot\mathrm{poly}(\log n))$ states.
However, this protocol does not immediately resolve the “core difficulty” of fast SS-RK. It comprises two sub-protocols---a fast primary protocol and a slow backup protocol---executed in parallel by each agent. The primary protocol stabilizes in $O(\sqrt{n}\,\log^{3/2}n)$ time w.h.p.\ but eventually terminates, ceasing collision-detection attempts; this termination is necessary to avoid false positives. The backup protocol never terminates but requires $\Theta(n)$ time. Together, they detect any collision with probability $1$ in $O(\sqrt{n\log n})$-expected time. (See \cite{AS25} for the detailed analysis.)
To apply this protocol to SS-RK, we must repeatedly invoke the primary protocol and reset its internal state across all agents. Without this reset, execution may start in an arbitrary configuration where the primary protocol has already terminated; in that case, only the backup protocol can detect collisions, incurring an inevitable $\Theta(n)$ delay.
However, repeating this collision-detection cycle also introduces a critical issue: since a pair of agents is chosen uniformly at random (we write \textbf{u.a.r.}\ in the rest of this paper) at each time step, there remains a nonzero (albeit arbitrarily small) probability that a global reset fails---some agents may retain outdated information even when execution begins from a stable configuration. This residual information causes false-positive detections and forces rank reassignment, thereby violating stability.


Very recently, Austin~et al.~\cite{ABF+25} presented a dramatically improved time--space tradeoff for SS-LE, affirmatively answering the first open question. They proposed a protocol parameterized by a positive integer $\rho$ ($1 \le \rho < n/2$) that solves SS-RK (and thus SS-LE) in $O(\sfrac{n}{\rho}\cdot\log n)$ time w.h.p.\ using $O(\rho^2\log n)$ bits.
Choosing $\rho = \omega(\log n)\cap o(\sqrt{n/\log n})$ yields a sublinear-time, sub-exponential-state protocol:
for example, $\rho=\Theta(\sqrt{n}/\log n)$ gives $O(\sqrt{n}\log^2 n)$ time w.h.p.\ with $2^{O(n/\log n)}=2^{o(n)}$ states, while $\rho=\Theta(\log^2 n)$ gives $O(n/\log n)$ time w.h.p.\ with $2^{O(\log^5 n)}$ states.
Allowing super-exponentially many states---but still far fewer than in \cite{BCC+21}---yields a time-optimal solution: setting $\rho=\Theta(n)$ achieves $\Theta(\log n)$ time w.h.p.\ using $2^{O(n^2\log n)}$ states.
Their collision-detection module also independently resolves the second question posed to Araya and Sudo~\cite{AS25}. Although it uses significantly more states ($2^{O(\rho^2\log n)}$ vs.\ $O(n\cdot\mathrm{poly}(\log n))$), it never terminates and thus does not require repeated invocation, enabling the first sublinear-time, sub-exponential-state SS-RK protocol.


While the majority work on population protocols studies the setting where every pair of agents can interact, quite a few studies deal with a more general setting: interactions may occur between restricted pairs of agents, introducing a \emph{interaction graph} $G=(V,E)$, where $V$ represents the set of agents and $E$ the set of interactable pairs.
SS-LE and its weaken variant have been also studied in rings~\cite{FJ06,CC19,YSM21,YSO+23}, regular graphs~\cite{CC20}, and general graphs~\cite{FJ06,SOK+18,SOK+20det,SSN+21,KESI24}. 


\subsection{Our Contribution}
\label{sec:contribution}
This paper affirmatively resolves the first open problem of Burman et al.~\cite{BCC+21}, independently of and nearly concurrently with Austin et~al.~\cite{ABF+25}.\footnote{We posted this paper on arXiv in May 2025, the same month that \cite{ABF+25} was released on arXiv.} Specifically, we prove the following theorem. (We write $\lg x$ for $\log_2 x$ throughout this paper.)

\begin{theorem}[Main Theorem]
\label{theorem:main}
For any positive integer $\rho$ with $1 \le \rho \le \sqrt{n}$, there exists a protocol that solves SS-RK (and hence SS-LE) within $O(\goalT)$ expected time using $2^{2\rho\lg\rho + O(\log n)}$ states
(equivalently, $2\rho\lg\rho + O(\log n)$ bits).
\end{theorem}
Choosing any $\rho=\omega(1)$ yields a sublinear-time, sub-exponential-state protocol. For example, $\rho=\lfloor \sqrt{n}\rfloor$ achieves $O(\sqrt{n}\log n)$ expected time with $2^{(\sqrt{n}/2) \lg n}\cdot\mathrm{poly}(n)$ states, while $\rho=\Theta\left(\sfrac{\log n}{\log\log n}\right)$ achieves $O\left(n \cdot \sfrac{\log\log n}{\log n}\right)=o(n)$ expected time with only polynomially many states (see Table \ref{table:non-silent}).

The proposed protocol and that of Austin et~al.~\cite{ABF+25} exhibit complementary strengths and trade-offs.
The proposed protocol uses significantly fewer states than the protocol of Austin et~al.\ requires to achieve any expected stabilization time above $\Theta(\sqrt{n}\log n)$. However, the restriction $\rho\le\sqrt{n}$ prevents us from attaining stabilization time $o(\sqrt{n}\log n)$. Conversely, the protocol of Austin et~al.\ can exceed this bound; in particular it can achieve an optimal $O(\log n)$-time solution with $2^{O(n^2\log n)}$ states.

The second example mentioned above (i.e., the case of $\rho = \Theta\left(\sfrac{\log n}{\log\log n}\right)$) resolves the first open question of Burman et al.~\cite{BCC+21} in stronger form, upgrading the state bound from sub-exponential to \emph{polynomial}:
\begin{corollary}
\label{col:strong}
There exists a protocol that solves SS-RK (and hence SS-LE) in sublinear expected time using only \emph{polynomially} many states.
\end{corollary}
Note that the protocol of \cite{ABF+25} does not achieve this: it requires $\rho=\omega(\log n)$ to stabilize in sublinear time, which entails $\omega(\log^2 n)$ bits (super-polynomial states). One might argue that Austin et al.~\cite{ABF+25} only claim an $O(\sfrac{n}{\rho}\cdot\log n)$ bound for w.h.p.\ stabilization time and that a refined analysis or slight modification could reduce their expected time to $O(\sfrac{n}{\rho}\cdot\log \rho)$ or $O(\sfrac{n}{\rho})$.
However, the (non-self-stabilizing) ranking component employed in their SS-RK protocol,
named $\assignranks_{\rho}$ with $1 \le \rho < n/2$, inherently requires $\Omega(\sfrac{n}{\rho}\cdot\log n)$ expected stabilization time, while using $2^{O(\rho \log n)}$ states.


\begin{remark}[Proof in Appendix~\ref{sec:proofs}]
\label{remark:lower_bound_abf}
The ranking protocol $\assignranks_{\rho}$ of Austin et al.~\cite{ABF+25} requires $\Omega(\sfrac{n}{\rho}\cdot\log n)$ expected stabilization time.
\end{remark}

It is worth mentioning that the proposed SS-LE protocol employs a \emph{loosely-stabilizing leader election} (LS-LE) protocol as a subroutine. LS-LE~\cite{SNY+12, SOK+20, SEIM21} is a weakened variant of SS-LE: from any configuration, the population quickly reaches a \emph{safe} configuration from which exactly one leader persists for an arbitrarily long (but finite) expected time. The time-optimal LS-LE protocol of Sudo, Eguchi, Izumi, and Masuzawa~\cite{SEIM21}, parameterized by $\tau\ge1$, reaches a safe configuration in $O(\tau \log n)$ expected time and thereafter maintains a unique leader for $\Omega(n^{\tau})$ expected time, using only $O(\tau\log n)$ states. Since its time and space complexities are negligible, we can assume a unique leader \emph{almost always} exists when designing our SS-LE protocol. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first work where an LS-LE protocol aids the design of an SS-LE protocol---an intriguing approach in which a stable leader is elected with the help of an unstable leader. 




\section{Preliminaries}
\label{sec:preliminaries}
\subsection{Term and Notation}
\label{sec:notation}
Throughout this paper, we write
$[x,y] = \{ i \in \mathbb{N} : x \le i \le y\}$
for any real numbers $x$ and $y$. We write $\lg x$ for $\log_2 x$.
For any set of configurations $\calC$, we say the population \emph{enters} $\calC$ when it reaches some configuration in $\calC$. 


Although SS-RK was informally introduced in Section~\ref{sec:intro}, we now give its formal definition.
\begin{definition}[Self-Stabilizing Ranking (SS-RK)]
A protocol $\calP$ solves SS-RK if, with probability~1, the population under $\calP$ eventually reaches an \emph{output-stable} configuration in which all agents output distinct integers in $[1,n]$.
\end{definition}


We often use \emph{variables} to define protocols.
In this context, an agent’s state is determined by the values of all protocol variables. For any state $q\in Q$ and variable $\var$, we denote by $q.\var$ the value of $\var$ in state $q$. For any agent $a\in A$, we denote by $a.\var$ the value of $\var$ in $a$’s current state.

Multiple definitions of “with high probability” (w.h.p.) appear in the population-protocol literature (e.g., with probability~$1 - n^{-1}$ or $1 - n^{-\Omega(1)}$). As in~\cite{BEF21,AS25}, we adopt the following definition.

\begin{definition}[with high probability]
\label{def:whp}
A property of a protocol $\calP$ holds \emph{with high probability}
if, for any constant $\eta = \Theta(1)$, we can ensure
the property is satisfied with a probability of $1-O(n^{-\eta})$
by adjusting the constant parameters of $\calP$, if necessary.
\end{definition}

\noindent 
For example, if a protocol $\calP_{\tau}$ with parameter $\tau=\Theta(1)$ elects a leader in $O(\tau\,n\log n)$ interactions with probability $1 - n^{-c\tau}$ (for some constant $c=\Theta(1)$), we say that $\calP_{\tau}$ elects a leader in $O(n\log n)$ interactions w.h.p. (or more precisely $O(\tau\,n\log n)$ interactions w.h.p.).





\section{Self-stabilizing Ranking}
\label{sec:ssle}
This section proves our main theorem (Theorem~\ref{theorem:main}) by presenting the SS-RK protocol $\ssrk(\rho)$. Given any integer $\rho \in [1,\sqrt{n}]$, this protocol solves SS-RK
within $O(\goalI)$ interactions (i.e., $O(\goalT)$ time) in expectation using $2^{2\rho\lg\rho + O(\log n)}$ states.

\subsection{Protocol Overview}
The protocol $\ssrk(\rho)$ comprises three sub-protocols: $\findtarget$, $\ccd(r,\rho)$, and $\ranking$ (parametrized by $2\le\chi\le n$).
These components serve the following roles:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $\findtarget$: if some rank $r\in[1,n]$ is held by two or more agents, selects one such $r$ as the \emph{target rank} with probability $1-o(1)$.
  \item $\ccd(r,\rho)$: if the target rank $r$ is held by two or more agents, detects the collision with probability $\Omega(1)$.
  \item $\ranking$: upon collision detection, reassigns unique ranks in $[1,n]$ to all agents with probability $1-o(1)$.
\end{itemize}

We execute these components sequentially in cycles. To ensure the above success probabilities, the sub-protocols $\findtarget$, $\ccd(r,\rho)$, and $\ranking$ require $O(n^{3/2}\log n)$, $O(\goalI)$, and $O(n^{3/2}\log n)$ interactions, respectively; the second bound dominates for all $\rho\in[1,\sqrt{n}]$. By the union bound, each cycle succeeds with probability $1 - (o(1) + (1 - \Omega(1)) + o(1)) = \Omega(1)$. Therefore, the expected number of cycles to success is $O(1)$, yielding $O(\goalI)$ expected interactions overall.

The core novelty of $\ssrk(\rho)$ lies in $\ccd(r,\rho)$. As noted in Section~\ref{sec:contribution}, repeating a collision-detection protocol with periodic resets carries a small but nonzero probability of reset failure, leaving outdated information in some agents. Such residual data causes false-positive detections and triggers rank reassignment, preventing stabilization. Consequently, the polynomial-time, polynomial-state protocol of Araya and Sudo~\cite{AS25} does not immediately yield a polynomial-time, polynomial-state SS-RK protocol. Austin et~al.~\cite{ABF+25} resolving this issue by avoiding periodical resets: they reinitialize their collision-detection module only when a collision is detected. In contrast, our component $\ccd(r,\rho)$ tolerates outdated information: once each agent has initialized its variables at least once, $\ccd(r,\rho)$ incurs no false positives, even if some agents retain outdated information from cycles long past.
Therefore, once a single complete cycle of the three components successfully assigns unique ranks, $\ccd(r,\rho)$ produces no false positives in subsequent cycles, ensuring stabilization.

The tolerance for outdated data allows $\ccd(r,\rho)$ to incorporate the time- and space-efficient collision detection protocol of Araya and Sudo~\cite{AS25}. We obtain $\findtarget$ by modifying their protocol so it outputs a target rank $r$ shared by multiple agents, if one exists. Restricting detection to a single rank $r$ greatly reduces the state complexity of $\ccd(r,\rho)$: agents need only track collisions on $r$, resulting in significantly fewer states than the protocol of Austin et~al.~\cite{ABF+25}. 

\subsection{Existing Tools}
\label{sec:tools}

\subsubsection{Loosely-stabilizing Leader Election}
Several components of $\ssrk(\rho)$ assume a unique leader exists; to satisfy this, we run in parallel the LS-LE protocol of Sudo et~al.~\cite{SEIM21}, denoted by $\lsle(\tau)$ here (line 1 in Algorithm \ref{al:main}).  
The following lemma allows us to assume, throughout the remainder of Section~\ref{sec:ssle}, that there is exactly one agent $a_L$ with $a_L.\leader=1$ and every other agent $a\in A\setminus\{a_L\}$ has $a.\leader=0$, where $\leader\in\{0,1\}$ is the leader-indicator variable.
\begin{lemma}[immediate consequence of \cite{SEIM21}]
\label{lemma:lsle}
For any $T=\poly(n)$, there exists a constant $\tau=\Theta(1)$ and a set $\csafe$ of configurations such that:
  (i) starting from any configuration, $\lsle(\tau)$ enters $\csafe$ within $O(\tau n\log n)$ expected interactions, and
  (ii) once in $\csafe$, it maintains a unique leader for at least $T$ interactions w.h.p. 
\end{lemma}
\noindent
For completeness, we include the proof of the above lemma in Appendix~\ref{sec:proofs}.


\subsubsection{Epidemic Protocol}
\label{sec:epdemic}

The \emph{one-way epidemic} (or simply \emph{epidemic}) protocol~\cite{AAE08}, frequently used in population protocols, propagates the maximum value of any given variable $\var$ to all agents. It consists of a single transition rule
$$
\res.\var \leftarrow \max(\ini.\var, \res.\var),
$$
where $\ini$ and $\res$ denote the initiator and responder.
Angluin~et~al.~\cite{AAE08} proved that this protocol stabilizes within $O(n\log n)$ interactions (i.e., $O(\log n)$ time) w.h.p.

\begin{lemma}[\cite{AAE08}]
\label{lemma:epidemic}
If the epidemic protocol for variable $\var$ starts from a configuration $C$ with $M = \max_{a \in A}C(a).\var$,
then $\min_{a \in A} a.\var = M$ within $O(n\log n)$ interactions w.h.p.
\end{lemma}


\subsubsection{Phase Clock with a Single Leader}
\label{sec:phase_clock}
Many variants of the phase clock have been used to loosely synchronize the population in protocol design.
We use the simplest variant: the single-leader phase clock of Angluin et~al.~\cite{AAE08}. This protocol assumes a fixed unique leader.
Each agent maintains a single variable $\clock \in [0,\constM\cdot T]$,
where $\constM=\Theta(1)$  and $T = \poly(n)$ are protocol parameters.
The initial value of $\clock$ is set to $0$ for all agents, including the leader.\footnote{Originally in \cite{AAE08}, the leader’s clock starts at $0$ and all others at $-1$. We instead initialize $\clock$ to $0$ for every agent to simplify the protocol description; this change does not affect the correctness of Lemma~\ref{lemma:phaseclock}.}
When an initiator $\ini$ and responder $\res$ interacts, $\ini$ does nothing and $\res$ updates its clock as follows:
\begin{align}
\res.\clock \gets 
\begin{cases}
\min(\res.\clock + 1,\constM \cdot T) & \tif \left (
\begin{aligned}
&\res.\leader = 1 \\
&\land \ini.\clock = \res.\clock
\end{aligned}
\right )\\
\max(\ini.\clock,\res.\clock)& \totherwise,
\end{cases}
\end{align}
where $\leader$ is an indicator variable with $a.\leader = 1$ if and only if $a$ is the unique leader.
We denote this protocol by $\phaseclock(T,\constM)$.

We partition the interval $[-1,\constM\cdot T]$ into \emph{phases} of uniform length $\constM=\Theta(1)$. Specifically, for each $i \in [-1,T]$, we say agent $a$ is in phase $i$ if $\constM\cdot i \le a.\clock < \constM\cdot (i+1)$. For each $i\in[0,T]$, let $\cphase(i)$ denote the set of configurations in which all agents are in phase $i$.
\begin{lemma}[Immediate consequence of Lemma~1 and Theorem~1 in \cite{AAE08}]
\label{lemma:phaseclock}
Let $C_0$ be the configuration in which every agent has $\clock=0$. For any $T=\poly(n)$ and any constant $\cmin=\Theta(1)$, there exists a constant $\constM$ such that, with high probability, an execution of $\phaseclock(T,\constM)$ starting from $C_0$ simultaneously satisfies for all $i\in[1,T]$:
\begin{itemize}
  \item the population enters $\cphase(i)$ within $O(i\cdot n\log n)$ interactions, and
  \item after entering $\cphase(i)$, remains there for at least $\cmin\cdot n\lg n$ interactions.
\end{itemize}
\end{lemma}







