\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

In this paper, we have presented a new time--space tradeoff for self-stabilizing leader election (SS-LE) in the population protocol model under exact knowledge of the population size $n$. Independently of the recent protocol of Austin et~al.~\cite{ABF+25}, we showed:
\begin{itemize}
  \item For any integer $1\le \rho\le \sqrt{n}$, there is an SS-LE protocol that stabilizes in $O(\sfrac{n}{\rho}\cdot\log\rho)$ expected time using $2^{2\rho\lg\rho+O(\log n)}$ states.
  \item By choosing $\rho=\Theta\left(\sfrac{\log n}{\log\log n}\right)$, this yields the first \emph{polynomial}-state protocol with sublinear expected time.  
\end{itemize}
Compared to the protocol of Austin et~al., ours uses drastically fewer states whenever the target expected time is at least $\Theta(\sqrt{n}\log n)$.

Our result complements prior work by filling the gap between the linear-state, linear-time regime and the super-exponential-state, optimal-time regime.  A remaining limitation is that the restriction $\rho\le\sqrt{n}$ prevents us from achieving $o(\sqrt{n}\log n)$ expected time; by contrast, the protocol of Austin et~al.\ can reach the optimal $\Theta(\log n)$ time at the cost of super-exponential state size. 


The following questions remain open:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Does there exist an SS-LE protocol using only polynomially many states that stabilizes in $O(n^{1-\epsilon})$ time for some constant $\epsilon>0$?  Our time--space tradeoff achieves only $O\left(n/\poly(\log n)\right)$ time under polynomial‐state constraints.
  \item Does Conjecture~\ref{conjecture:ss-rk} hold?  Equivalently, must one solve SS-RK in order to achieve SS-LE?
\end{itemize}


