\section{Limitations}
\label{appdx:limitations}

% bias and coverage
The framework does not currently address issues in bias \cite{rakivnenko2024biastextembeddingmodels,Cao_2025}. Coverage in other diverse domains like arts, culture, health \cite{cao2024recentadvancestextembedding} is also limiting.  

% backward compat
MTEB has also received criticism from model makers for its loose adherence to semantic versioning and backwards compatibility.
These issues mainly came about as a result of the large refactoring effort that was required for multilingual expansion.
While these refactoring effort did benefit the community and the package at large,
breaking changes were at times introduced in minor/patch releases,
which frequent users of the package found confusing and frustrating.
We have since then put more emphasis on maintaining backward compatibility with older versions of the library to avoid further inconvenience.

% documentation
Since the initial launch of MTEB, the package has maintained a simple documentation structure, initially in the README and as it expanded across multiple hyperlinked markdown files. Although this was initially simple to maintain, the code base has grown in size and complexity and is now more prone to code-documentation inconsistencies. We plan to incorporate documentation within the docstrings and generate docs automatically in future releases (v2.0.0 or above). 


\section{Conclusion}
MTEB’s transition from a standalone benchmark to a scalable evaluation ecosystem has hinged on robust design choices. We present our approach to maintaining MTEB towards better reproducibility, maintainability, and seamless integration of new tasks and modalities. These infrastructure choices have enabled broad community engagement and sustained growth without compromising quality. 
%MTEB's open framework empowers communities to create and share benchmarks, fostering inclusion in multilingual and multimodal AI. 
%Recent additions, including multilingual tasks (MMTEB) and image-based (MIEB) benchmarks, further support this goal by broadening the scope of evaluation and encouraging research that better reflects global linguistic and perceptual diversity. 
%This helps steer the field toward more globally representative and responsible research.
Our experience offers practical guidance for designing and maintaining benchmarks, contributing to more reproducible, reliable, and globally representative machine learning research.

