Hoogeboom et al.~\cite{hoogeboom2022blurring} noticed that better FID metric can be achieved for higher final blur $\sigma$ at the price of a more difficult (longer) training. 
Eventually, the optimal $\Pe$ depends on the final amount of blur.
In our opinion, the effect is related to the ''overall'' destruction of images, which can be obtained by using different corruption operators. 
The presence of turbulent advection introduces additional degrees of freedom which allow for better training as compared to a purely deterministic dissipative model.

\vspace{-10pt}
\paragraph{Image Interpolation. }
We also evaluate our method on interpolated initial states and compare it with a baseline approach (IHD). 
Two samples from FFHQ dataset undergo the forward process, followed by linear interpolation in the latent space. 
The interpolated states are then denoised with generative noise and interpolated with SLERP~\citet{SLERP85}.
The analysis provides an insight into the smoothness and consistency of the learned latent space. 
By denoising the linearly interpolated noisy inputs we assess the model's ability to generate coherent transitions. 
\Cref{fig:faces_interpolation} depicts the qualitative results.

\vspace{-10pt}
\paragraph{Discussion and Limitations. }

An immediate drawback of introducing a nonlinear advection term into the diffusion process is the necessity of step-by-step computation up to the given time. 
In practice, we precompute PDE corruption for the dataset to avoid repeated runs, which adds $\sim10\%$ overhead. In our examples, the corruption process lead to the blurry prior from which new samples can be generated. 
As a consequence, we have not found a distribution (like a standard Gaussian) from which samples could be easily drawn.
However, a sufficiently long process with properly tuned balance between advection and diffusion terms shall converge to 
a well defined stationary turbulent field analogous to the noising Markov process.





