\begin{abstract}
With advancements in additive manufacturing (AM) capabilities, new opportunities arise to design compact heat exchangers (cHEXs) that leverage AM's degrees of freedom (DOFs) to enhance energy and material efficiency. However, excessive size reduction in counterflow cHEXs can compromise effectiveness due to axial heat conduction through the solid material, which is strongly influenced by thermal conductivity and wall thickness. This study investigates how AM material selection and related limitations in AM thin-wall production might compromise the core size of counterflow plate heat exchangers when targeting maximum power density. An optimization framework evaluates maximum power densities for six materials, i.e., plastic, austenitic steel, Al\textsubscript{2}O\textsubscript{3}, AlN, aluminum, and copper. The evaluations are conducted under constant effectiveness and pressure drop while accounting for AM-specific plate thickness limitations and a lower bound on plate spacing to address fouling concerns. Across all scenarios, copper cHEXs exhibit the lowest maximum power density, despite their high thermal conductivity. Without constraints on plate thickness and spacing, the optimal plastic heat exchanger achieves a power density 1800 times greater than the steel baseline, while copper shows a decrease by a factor of 0.98. With an equal plate thickness of 0.5 mm for all materials, plastic retains the highest power density, achieving 12.2 times more than copper. Introducing a fouling constraint of 0.8 mm plate spacing shifts the optimal material to austenitic steel. When material-specific plate thicknesses are considered, the plastic cHEX achieves the highest power density, five times greater than copper, due to superior thin-wall resolution. This study highlights the significant impact of AM constraints on the energy and material efficiency of cHEXs. It also demonstrates that materials with lower thermal conductivity, such as plastic or austenitic steel, can outperform high-conductivity materials like copper in compact heat exchanger designs.
\end{abstract}
