\subsection{Thickness-constrained power density optimization for a design effectiveness of 0.79}
The optimizations in this section use the same fluid properties, pressure drop, and effectiveness as the baseline heat exchanger, outlined in the previous section and detailed in Table~\ref{use_case}. The results of these optimizations are illustrated in Figure~\ref{opt_results}, which compares three distinct optimization strategies aimed at maximizing power density. 

The first strategy, labeled ``\textit{Thickness Constraint}", maintains a fixed \(t_d\)=0.5 mm for all materials, with no constraint on plate spacing. The second strategy, labeled ``\textit{Thickness and Fouling Constraints}", introduces a fouling constraint that imposes $D_{min}$=0.8 mm, while still maintaining the fixed plate thickness of 0.5 mm for all materials. The third strategy, labeled ``\textit{Material-Specific Thickness and Fouling Constraints}", incorporates both plate thickness and fouling constraints simultaneously. Unlike the previous strategies, this approach applies a material-specific thickness constraint to account for variations in manufacturing resolution in AM. 

To address AM resolutions for ceramic and metal heat exchangers, the plate thickness is determined based on the melt pool size, a characteristic of the manufacturing process. It is assumed that a sealed plate requires a thickness of twice the melt pool size. Consequently, the following plate thickness values are used: 0.5 mm for copper~\cite{Jadhav2021}, 0.3 mm for aluminum~\cite{Ghasemi2021}, and 0.25 mm for aluminum nitride (AlN), aluminum oxide (Al\textsubscript{2}O\textsubscript{3})~\cite{Yao2023}, and austenitic steel~\cite{Yang2021}. These values are characteristic of the Laser Powder Bed Fusion (LPBF) process, which is the most common technique for AM of metals and ceramics. 
For plastic heat exchangers, the plate thickness is not derived from melt pool size, as LPBF is not typically used for plastic printing. Instead, Material Extrusion (MEX), Vat Photopolymerization (VPP), and Sheet Lamination (SHL) processes are widely used~\cite{Bhatia2023, Ligon2017}. While these techniques can achieve thin walls with resolutions below 0.1 mm, a plate thickness of 0.1 mm is assumed in this study. This value is selected to balance manufacturing feasibility with structural integrity, as heat exchangers with wall thicknesses below 0.1 mm are prone to plastic deformation~\cite{Rua2015}. 

In addition to the three optimization strategies, Figure~\ref{opt_results} presents the non-dimensional power density values for heat exchangers with baseline length and plate spacing, labeled as ``\textit{Additive Manufacturable Initial}” in the figure. These designs serve as a reference for comparison and are adapted from the baseline heat exchanger by applying material-specific plate thicknesses for each of the six considered materials. Unlike in the optimal designs, the pressure drop in these cases is lower than 1150 Pa to maintain a constant effectiveness of 0.79. As a result, $\psi$ in the non-dimensional power density equation (Eq.~\ref{NPD}) is no longer equal to 1; instead, it takes values of 0.96, 0.99, 0.98, and 0.75 for plastic, austenitic steel, AlN, and Al\textsubscript{2}O\textsubscript{3}, respectively. However, for copper and aluminum, even with adjustments to the pressure drop, the design with the baseline length and plate spacing is insufficient to achieve the target effectiveness. Therefore, no Additive Manufacurable Initial designs are included in the figure for these materials.

