As shown in Figure~\ref{opt_results}, under the ``\textit{Thickness Constraint}" strategy, where the plate thickness is fixed at 0.5 mm and the fouling constraint is not considered, plastic, the material with the lowest thermal conductivity, achieves the highest power density. This result corresponds to a plate spacing of 0.14 mm and a length of 5.6 mm, dimensions that are significantly smaller than those of all other designs (see Table~\ref{opt_with_manuf_const}). When the fouling constraint is included, austenitic steel emerges as the material with the highest power density, while the plastic heat exchanger drops to third place, following Al\textsubscript{2}O\textsubscript{3}. However, in the ``\textit{Material-Specific Thickness and Fouling Constraints}" strategy, where material-specific resolutions are explicitly accounted for, plastic achieves the highest power density due to its ability to utilize thinner plates.

In contrast, despite having the highest thermal conductivity among the materials considered, copper exhibits the lowest power density across all optimization strategies in Figure~\ref{opt_results}. This indicates that the dimensions of copper heat exchangers need to be significantly higher than the others to achieve the design effectiveness of 0.79, as confirmed by Table~\ref{opt_with_manuf_const}.

Across all three optimization scenarios, heat exchangers made from relatively higher-conductivity materials such as copper and aluminum consistently result in increased length and spacing compared to those made from materials with lower thermal conductivity. Consequently, the fouling constraint for copper and aluminum remains inactive. As marked in Table~\ref{opt_with_manuf_const}, for AlN, the fouling constraint is inactive under the ``\textit{Thickness and Fouling Constraints}" strategy, where the plate thickness is 0.5 mm, but becomes active under the ``\textit{Material-Specific Thickness and Fouling Constraints} strategy, where the plate thickness is reduced to 0.25 mm. These results underscore that using thicker plates and high-conductivity materials in cHEX designs leads to larger heat exchangers to offset axial wall conduction effects and maintain the target design effectiveness.

\subsection{Influence of design effectiveness on optimal heat exchanger}
In the previous optimizations, a design effectiveness of 0.79 was used to ensure consistency with the baseline heat exchanger (see Table~\ref{use_case}). These studies demonstrated that both material selection and plate thickness significantly influence cHEX power density. To further explore these effects, this section investigates how varying the design effectiveness influences material selection and power density under AM-imposed plate thickness constraints. The ``\textit{Material-Specific Thickness and Fouling Constraints}" optimization strategy is repeated across a range of $\varepsilon_d$, from 0.55 to 0.94, to examine the interplay between effectiveness, material type, and power density. The outcomes of these optimizations for all materials are illustrated in Figure~\ref{opt_results_varying_eff}.

